// VoxelBrickTraverse.ush -- THE VOLUME, BEHIND AN INTERFACE.
//
// P3 of docs/ray-marching-plan-2026-08-19.md. This file is the ONE place
// VoxelMarch.usf learns what the world is made of; everything above it -- the
// ray, the tile classify, the GBuffer emit -- is written against
// FVoxelMarchHit and VoxelMarchTraverse() and knows nothing else.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY AN INTERFACE AND NOT JUST THE BRICK POOL
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// FVoxelBrickPool did not exist when this was written -- P2 is a separate
// workstream, concurrently owned. The plan's instruction was explicit:
// "develop against FVoxelFluidOccupancy exactly as VoxelMarchSpike.usf does,
// and keep the traversal behind an interface so swapping the source is a
// one-line change."
//
// That one line is VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE, a shader PERMUTATION (not a uniform
// branch), for the reason VoxelMarchSpike.usf records at length about its own
// no-fetch arm: a runtime branch leaves BOTH sources' loads in the binary and
// silently re-bases whichever arm you are measuring. Permutations here are
// cheap -- the source dimension multiplies the march kernel only.
//
//   VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE 0   FVoxelFluidOccupancy, the 512^3 / 51.2 m bit volume
//                          -- the SAME volume VoxelMarchSpike.usf marched and
//                          the same one the fluid collides against. ONE level,
//                          10 cm, and NO PER-VOXEL MATERIAL (it is a bitfield;
//                          see THE MATERIAL HOLE below).
//   VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE 1   FVoxelBrickPool, docs/brick-volume-format.md.
//                          Multi-level, per-voxel material, real skip masks.
//                          Declared here and #error'd until P2 lands, so that
//                          "the pool is wired up" can never be true by
//                          accident -- it has to be typed.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE MATERIAL HOLE, STATED ONCE AND LOUDLY
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// FVoxelFluidOccupancy stores ONE BIT PER VOXEL. It does not know that a voxel
// is snow rather than bedrock and it never did -- vxc::packBrickSolidBits
// throws the material away, which is correct for a collision volume and is
// exactly what docs/brick-volume-format.md 3a is about.
//
// So under VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE 0 the marcher CANNOT produce ADR-0008 colour. It
// reports VOXEL_MARCH_PLACEHOLDER_MATERIAL for every solid voxel, and the
// resulting frame is grey rock everywhere. THAT IS NOT A PALETTE BUG AND IT IS
// NOT A LOD BUG. It is the source having no materials in it.
//
// Consequences that must not be papered over:
//   * a colour A/B against the raster path is MEANINGLESS on source 0. Gate 4
//     of plan section 10 (colour statistics) cannot run until source 1 exists.
//   * the DEPTH gate (plan section 10, gate 3) CAN run on source 0 and is the
//     one that matters for P3: it asks whether the march lands where PrePass +
//     BasePass landed, which is a question about geometry, not colour.
//   * the per-voxel hash tint (VoxelMaterialColor's invariant 3) is still
//     evaluated, because the hash is keyed on the VOXEL COORDINATE and not on
//     the material -- so the boiling-at-range behaviour the cone fade exists to
//     suppress is fully exercised even with one material.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT COMES BACK, AND WHY EACH FIELD IS THERE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// ADR-0008: the march returns the EXACT voxel and the EXACT crossed face, so
// per-voxel colour is a direct VoxelMaterialColor() call with no interpolants
// and the normal is the geometric axis, free, with no derivative anywhere.
// Every field below is either that, or the cone state the LOD rule needs, or
// the eight neighbour bits the corner AO needs. Nothing is here "in case".
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LOD IS CONE-BASED. IT IS NOT, AND MUST NEVER BE, DERIVATIVE-BASED.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// ADR-0008 records that hardware derivatives (ddx/ddy) are DISCONTINUOUS across
// voxel edges: two pixels in the same 2x2 quad routinely land on different
// faces of different voxels, so ddx of anything voxel-derived is a step
// function and the mip it selects flickers along every silhouette. A marcher
// does not need them and must not use them.
//
// Instead each ray carries its own footprint:
//
//     coneRadiusUU(t) = t * PixelConeSlope
//
// where PixelConeSlope is the world-space half-width of one pixel per unit of
// distance -- a pure function of the projection and the viewport, computed once
// on the host (see FVoxelMarchRenderer's PixelConeSlope note). The level rule
// is then, per plan section 5:
//
//     pick the SMALLEST level L with voxelSizeUU(L) >= 1.5 * coneRadiusUU(t)
//
// i.e. "no voxel smaller than ~1.5 px", enforced PER RAY. That is the property
// a per-chunk mesher structurally cannot have: a mesher picks one LOD for a
// whole chunk from the chunk's distance, so the near corner of a far chunk is
// always over-tessellated and the far corner always under-.
//
// THE FACTOR 1.5 IS NOT A TUNING KNOB WITH AN OBVIOUS BEST VALUE and it is
// named rather than inlined. Below ~1.0 the marcher resolves voxels smaller
// than a pixel and the result aliases into TSR as boiling; above ~2 it throws
// away silhouette detail TSR would otherwise have resolved. 1.5 is the plan's
// number and the first thing to sweep if the far field reads wrong.

// ===========================================================================
// READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A LEVEL TO THE HIERARCHY
// ===========================================================================
//
//   INTEGER COORDINATES ARE THE TRUTH. FLOAT BOUNDS ARE AN OPTIMISATION.
//   EVERY PLACE THEY BOTH DECIDE THE SAME THING IS A BUG WAITING.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// AND ABOVE ALL OF THEM, BECAUSE IT IS THE ERROR THAT GENERATED THEM:
//
//   DO NOT MINE A SELECTION EFFECT FOR MECHANISM.
//
// For six rounds the guiding fact was that 94% of disagreements sit on cell
// boundaries. Every hypothesis below was reverse-engineered from it. But a
// disagreement is OBSERVABLE only where the two walks straddle a plane --
// `floor` absorbs any difference everywhere else -- so for ANY perturbation
// whatsoever, bug or drift or rounding, disagreements concentrate on
// boundaries BY CONSTRUCTION. The 94% is the observability window. It could
// not have come out any other way, and it is what a NULL RESULT looks like.
//
// The cost of missing that: four hypotheses, THREE OF WHICH WERE REAL DEFECTS
// and were correctly found and fixed -- which is exactly what made the method
// feel productive -- and NONE of which moved the population. Being right about
// three mechanisms is not evidence the search is aimed correctly.
//
// The tell was available the whole time and was read as encouragement instead:
// REPEATED REAL FIXES THAT DO NOT MOVE THE POPULATION mean the selection is
// doing the work, not the mechanism. When a measured concentration could not
// have come out otherwise, it constrains nothing -- and the first question
// about any concentration is not "what causes it" but "what else could this
// number have been". If the answer is "nothing", it is not evidence.
//
// What replaced it: measure the perturbation's MAGNITUDE (VoxelMarch.usf,
// "THE BAND HISTOGRAM"), which is a fingerprint, and adjudicate with something
// that is not one of the two disputing walks ("THE INTEGER ARBITER").
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// That sentence cost four separate defects in this file, three of them the
// identical shape, and every one of them produced a plausible picture rather
// than an error. They are listed because the next person to add a level will
// otherwise make it a fifth time:
//
//  1. THE NUDGE (fixed). The fine walk was bounded by the brick exit PLUS the
//     advance nudge, so it stepped one voxel past the brick every crossing and
//     tested that voxel against the wrong brick's occupancy.
//     -> `gained` 20,233 rays.
//
//  2. THE ULP (fixed). With the nudge gone, `Span` (a slab test from the
//     brick's min corner) and `tMax` (accumulated from the segment entry) still
//     disagreed by ULP at the exit plane -- an unbiased coin flip, once per
//     crossing, in the same direction as (1).
//     -> containment moved into integers: `voxel >> 3` against the loaded brick.
//
//  3. THE MIRROR (fixed). Removing the over-run left the float `Span` as the
//     ONLY early-out, so its ULP error could now only cut SHORT -- one voxel at
//     the far face, which the brick loop then skipped past entirely.
//     -> the float bound became the RAY end; the integer test became sole
//        authority.
//
//  4. THE CHUNK HANDOFF (fixed). Same shape one scale up: the brick loop's only
//     guard on chunk membership was the float `t < tChunkEnd`, while the voxel
//     was recomputed from the current t. Within a ULP of a chunk plane the ray
//     was in the NEXT chunk while the bound said otherwise.
//     -> `lost`, `gained` and `moved` simultaneously, from one cause.
//
//  5. THE DUPLICATED EXPRESSION (fixed). VoxelMarchCellExitT recomputed
//     `floor((Origin + Dir*t) / voxelUU)` that the caller had already computed.
//     TEXTUALLY IDENTICAL, so it looked correct at both sites and in the diff --
//     but the compiler may contract one and not the other, and one cell of
//     disagreement at a plane SKIPS A BRICK when travelling positively while
//     merely stalling when travelling negatively.
//     -> 92% of disagreements on positive travel; the cell is now passed in.
//
//     THE COROLLARY, WHICH IS THE NON-OBVIOUS PART:
//         THE SAME EXPRESSION WRITTEN TWICE IS STILL TWO AUTHORITIES.
//     Every earlier instance had two DIFFERENT formulations to notice. This one
//     had none, and survived three rounds of reading for exactly that reason.
//
//  6. THE TRADE (open). Fixing (5) collapsed `lost onFace` 550 -> 22 and pushed
//     `moved` 0 -> 29..93 on the same legs. Rays that previously SKIPPED a brick
//     -- and a skipped brick cannot produce a wrong cell, which is why `moved`
//     had been exactly zero -- now descend correctly and some land on the wrong
//     voxel.
//
//     THE LESSON, WHICH IS NEW AND COST A ROUND TO LEARN:
//         A FIX THAT REMOVES ONE AUTHORITY CAN EXPOSE A SECOND DEFECT THAT THE
//         FIRST WRONG ANSWER WAS HIDING.
//     Every earlier fix here reduced every term at once. This one traded, and a
//     pre-registered "this term must not move" is the only reason that was
//     visible as a regression rather than banked as progress.
//
//  7. THE CHUNK-LEVEL ADVANCE (OPEN, NOT FIXED). The brick level is now a real
//     DDA and cannot skip. The CHUNK level still is not: a non-resident or
//     all-air chunk is skipped with `t = tChunkExitTrue + NUDGE` and the next
//     chunk is found by re-flooring the position -- the identical construction
//     that made (5)/(6) skip bricks, one scale up.
//
//     It is narrower here: it needs a ray to clip a chunk for less than the
//     nudge AND the chunk beyond it to hold content within that sliver. But
//     "narrower" is what (5) looked like too, and it produced 977 rays. LEFT
//     DELIBERATELY UNFIXED so the brick-level result can be attributed; it is
//     the next candidate if a residue survives in high mode.
//
//  8. THE DDA REWRITE (REVERTED, and the diagnosis is NOT thereby refuted).
//     (7) motivated rewriting the brick level as a real DDA, on the reasoning
//     that a DDA cannot skip a cell by construction while jump-and-re-floor
//     jumps any brick clipped for less than the nudge. THE REASONING MAY STILL
//     BE RIGHT. THE IMPLEMENTATION WAS NOT:
//
//         disagree      1,060 -> 199,839   (188x worse)
//         lost offFace      6 ->  79,769   (13,000x worse)
//         ratio        37.61x ->  31.12x   (fell 17%: more steps, worse answers)
//
//     THE SHAPE CHANGED, WHICH IS THE DIAGNOSTIC. The old defect was 94-96%
//     boundary-concentrated; the new one is 40% OFF boundary, and BOTH descent
//     bins exploded together (941 -> 149,028 and 42 -> 50,312) with their ratio
//     barely moving. A skip mechanism raises only the first. Both rising is a
//     walk wrong in a GENERAL way, not at a geometric configuration.
//
//     SUSPECTED CAUSE, recorded for the second attempt rather than acted on:
//     the rewrite handed the fine walk a brick-entry t that lands EXACTLY on a
//     brick plane (the DDA's crossing t), where `floor` of the entry position
//     resolves to either side. The old code entered a nudge INSIDE the brick and
//     never sat on the plane. If so the fix is not to restore the nudge but to
//     take the entry voxel's BRICK part from the integer brick coordinate and
//     only its CELL part from the position -- the same construction already used
//     for the uniform-SOLID hit. UNVERIFIED.
//
//     A DIAGNOSIS AND ITS IMPLEMENTATION ARE SEPARABLE, and conflating them here
//     would either throw away a correct analysis or preserve a broken rewrite.
//
//     RESOLVED 2026-08-20 -- CORRECT AND IRRELEVANT. The coincidence test ran
//     over four legs, two windows each. In EVERY window the number of thin-clip
//     hit bricks among all rays equalled the number among disagreeing rays:
//     1/1, 4/4, 3/3, 2/2, and 0/0 six times.
//
//         EVERY THIN CLIP ON A HIT BRICK IS A DISAGREEMENT. 100%, ten windows.
//
//     The diagnosis was right. But there are only 0-4 per window against
//     138-652 disagreements, so THE CORNER CLIP IS ~0.6% OF THE DEFECT AND
//     OFTEN ZERO. Do not build the second brick-DDA attempt: not because it
//     cannot be measured, but because it was measured and it is worth 0.6%.
//     A CONFIRMED MECHANISM IS NOT NECESSARILY THE MECHANISM, and "it fires
//     every time it can" says nothing about how often it can.
//
//  9e. WHEN YOU FIND ONE INSTANCE OF A DEFECT, COUNT THE INSTANCES.
//     The arbiter's validity test held THREE strict float comparisons against
//     boundaries -- span (F > N), near bound (F > TIn), far bound (N < TOut).
//     The 8-ulp tolerance was derived correctly, argued carefully, and applied
//     TO ONE OF THEM. The other two were the same defect, untouched, and the
//     far bound then produced 648 rejections against the near bound's exactly 0.
//
//     THE ZERO IS WHAT MADE IT LEGIBLE, and the reason is worth keeping: TOut
//     is TMax - BOX_INSET where TMax is the PREPASS DEPTH, so the far bound sits
//     1/200 of a voxel in front of the very surface every hit lands on. TIn is
//     pinned to nothing. A perfectly one-sided count is rarely a property of the
//     rays; it is usually a property of what the two bounds are MADE OF.
//
//     Doctrine 5 says the same expression written twice is two authorities.
//     This is its sibling: THE SAME MISTAKE MADE THREE TIMES IS NOT FIXED BY
//     ONE CORRECTION, and a fix that leaves siblings in place will read as a
//     partial success -- degenerate fell, so the tolerance "worked" -- while the
//     population simply moves into the neighbouring bin.
//
//     IT LANDED A THIRD TIME THE SAME NIGHT, in a different file. A minimum-count
//     guard was written for the ARBFAIL verdict and for gate v4; the ARBFAIL one
//     tested the denominator, the gate one tested the SUM. So the gate printed
//     FAIL at 4.09x, 13.50x and 1.63x over denominators of 11, 2 and 19 -- a
//     ratio guarded by a bar its denominator never had to clear. A GUARD ON A
//     RATIO MUST TEST THE DENOMINATOR; a sum lets one large arm carry a tiny one
//     past the bar. Writing the same guard twice is writing it twice.
//
//     AND THE SEGMENT TEST WAS NEVER FIXABLE BY TOLERANCE AT ALL. It re-decided,
//     with an independent computation, something the walk had already guaranteed
//     -- doctrine 5 again. The arbiter recomputes entry t directly (O(1) ulp);
//     the walk accumulates (O(N) ulp). Sizing the arbiter's band to swallow the
//     walk's drift would be tuning one instrument's constant to absorb another
//     instrument's error, which is the thing this file refuses everywhere else.
//     It is now a counter with a MAGNITUDE, not a filter.
//
//  9c. A FILTERED COUNT IS A LOWER BOUND, NEVER A DECISION BASIS.
//     After 9b was fixed, the uncontested skip counts were quoted as "~20 rays
//     per leg, negligible, characterise and stop" -- BY BOTH OF US, one round
//     after establishing that pre-fix counts were suppressed. The real value is
//     ~3,870 per leg (0.29% of frame), about 200x larger, and still a lower
//     bound because 8-44% of rays remain ill-posed and every one of those is
//     drawn from the same population.
//
//     THE RULE: while any known defect DISCARDS members of a population, every
//     count over that population is a floor. It may be quoted as a floor. It may
//     NEVER be quoted as a magnitude, and it may never justify a decision to
//     stop -- because the decision to stop is exactly the one that a suppressed
//     count makes look safe. This is the selection-effect error one level down:
//     there the SELECTION was mistaken for a mechanism, here a SUPPRESSED count
//     was mistaken for a measurement.
//
//  9d. INSTRUMENT A FALSIFIED HYPOTHESIS INSTEAD OF DELETING IT.
//     The degenerate-span rejection was recorded as the suspected cause of the
//     arbiter's ill-posedness, then apparently falsified when the first
//     decomposition showed the sentinel instead -- so it was DEMOTED IN THE
//     COMMENT BUT ITS COUNTER WAS KEPT. Two legs later that counter read 70 of
//     80 failures and the verdict fired SELF-INFLICTED, naming it. Both causes
//     were real; neither was universal; the decomposition refused to fit one to
//     all three legs.
//
//     Deleting the bin would have cost the finding entirely. A hypothesis that
//     looks wrong is cheap to keep measured and expensive to re-derive, and
//     "wrong in the leg I happened to read" is not the same as wrong.
//
//  9b. THE MISS THAT LOOKS LIKE A HIT. VoxelMarchTraverseBrick sets
//     `H.Voxel = StartVoxel` before it walks, so ON A MISS THE HIT VOXEL FIELD
//     HOLDS THE RAY'S ENTRY VOXEL -- a large, plausible, perfectly legal
//     coordinate -- with H.THitUU left at 0. `bHit` is the only thing that says
//     otherwise, and three separate consumers forgot to ask:
//
//       - the arbiter scored `gained` rays as a contest the flat walk lost,
//         manufacturing `hier-correct` counts and, where the surviving side also
//         failed validity, the entire `ill-posed` population (ill-posed ~=
//         gained, four legs running, 30-43%);
//       - the brick-mismatch assertion compared a real brick against the ENTRY
//         brick and fired as an Error on a condition it declared impossible;
//       - the sample dump printed the sentinel as a position, which is how it
//         was finally caught: three pixels "hitting" one voxel at t=0.0000.
//
//     THE GUARD THAT EXISTED WAS ASYMMETRIC AND THAT IS THE LESSON. The
//     assertion read `Class != 0` -- someone had already noticed that `lost`
//     rays have no HIER voxel and excluded them. The identical hole in the
//     other direction, `gained` rays having no FLAT voxel, was not seen. WHEN
//     YOU FIND YOURSELF EXCLUDING ONE DIRECTION OF A SYMMETRIC RELATION, THE
//     OTHER DIRECTION IS ALREADY BROKEN.
//
//     This is doctrine 5's shape (a masked index yields a legal answer) moved
//     up a level: an unset FIELD yielding a legal COORDINATE. The struct cannot
//     express "no hit" except through a flag nobody is forced to read.
//
//  9. A COUNT IS NOT A MEASUREMENT UNTIL ITS POPULATION IS CHARACTERISED.
//     Every number quoted in (8) is a POINT FROM AN UNMEASURED DISTRIBUTION.
//     N=6 legs on six distinct index hashes -- six different worlds, not six
//     draws of one -- put the reverted build's disagree count at:
//
//         15   41   148   527   1,060   10,428      RANGE 695x (~2.8 decades)
//
//     while the skip ratio moved 3.2% (36.86 - 38.04) across that same swing.
//
//     So `1,060`, treated as "the high mode" for six rounds of investigation,
//     is simply a common draw. The 188x in (8) is real -- 199,839 is outside
//     this population by more than a decade -- but every SMALLER movement read
//     during that period proved nothing, in either direction, and neither did
//     the pre-registrations written over them. A criterion stated on a metric
//     whose band has never been measured is not a prediction; it is a guess
//     that will be read as confirmation whichever way it lands. That is worse
//     than no criterion at all.
//
//     WHY MORE LEGS CANNOT FIX IT: the variance is not sampling noise, it is
//     world content -- how much defect-triggering geometry each world holds.
//     Averaging more worlds estimates the mean of a distribution nobody cares
//     about. Normalising by RAYS fails for the same reason: the density varies,
//     not the population size.
//
//     WHAT I PROPOSED, AND WHY IT COULD NOT HAVE WORKED. I added BrickEntries
//     and ThinClips as denominators, asserting both "scale with world content".
//     MEASURED, N=6:
//
//         brick entries   12.12M - 12.20M    spread 0.7%
//         thin clips       1,881 - 1,898     spread 0.9%
//         per thin clip   0.0048 - 0.3435    spread 72x
//
//     Neither scales with content. THEY ARE CONSTANTS OF THE CAMERA POSE.
//     A thin clip is a ray-versus-grid-plane event -- decided by ray origin
//     and direction against the brick lattice, with the world entering only
//     through where the ray stops. Same pose, same snapped origin, same clips.
//     It could not have varied, so it could not have explained variation.
//
//     AND THE NORMALISATION WAS ARITHMETICALLY INERT: 72x per clip against
//     72.4x raw. DIVIDING BY A CONSTANT CANNOT CHANGE A SPREAD. The column
//     is the raw count rescaled, and it was read as a new measurement.
//
//     THIS DID NOT REFUTE THE CORNER CLIP -- IT FAILED TO TEST IT. The rate
//     is a per-ray verdict over an all-ray sum of an unrelated quantity;
//     NOTHING IN THE INSTRUMENT EVER CHECKED THAT A DISAGREEMENT AND A THIN
//     CLIP OCCUR ON THE SAME RAY, LET ALONE THE SAME BRICK. Until they are
//     intersected, (8) is UNTESTED, not disproved.
//
//     THE GENERAL TRAP: a denominator only normalises if it CO-VARIES with
//     what it divides. One that holds still is decoration on the numerator.
//     Check that a candidate denominator MOVES before trusting a rate built
//     on it -- the check is free and it is the whole validity of the metric.
//
//     REPLACED BY A TEST THAT CAN ANSWER (VoxelMarch.usf, "THE COINCIDENCE
//     TEST"): a closed-form slab test on the brick the FLAT walk hit, asked
//     per ray, reported as the LIFT of the thin share among disagreeing rays
//     over the thin share among ALL hit rays. It consults neither walk, and it
//     answers for bricks the hierarchy NEVER ENTERED -- the exact case the
//     mechanism describes and the one no counter inside the brick loop can
//     ever see, which is why the previous attempt was structurally blind to it.
//     A BARE SHARE IS STILL NOT A RESULT. The control is the whole test.
//     Vindicated immediately: single windows read 0/138 and 0/151 -- "REFUTED"
//     if either had been read alone -- while others read 4/4 and 3/3. The
//     two-window print is what stopped a real mechanism being dismissed.
//
// AND THE REASON ALL FIVE WERE SILENT IS THE SAME: the index helpers MASK.
// VoxelMarchCellIndex takes `& 7`, VoxelMarchBrickIndex takes `& 3`. An
// out-of-range coordinate therefore produces a perfectly legal in-range index,
// so every value downstream looks correct and the only symptom is geometry in
// the wrong place. A mask is an assertion that the caller already checked; if
// nothing checked, the mask is what hides it.
//
// So: when you add a level, the cell you are IN is decided by integer
// coordinates and nothing else. Use the float parameter to skip work, never to
// decide membership, and never let two things answer the same question.
//
// ===========================================================================

#pragma once

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Source selection
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE
#define VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE 0
#endif

#define VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE_OCCUPANCY 0
#define VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE_BRICKPOOL 1

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// COMPILING BOTH SOURCES AT ONCE, for voxel.March.VerifySource
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The two traversals are normally either/or -- one permutation compiles one of
// them, and the other's bindings do not exist. That is what keeps a source-0
// dispatch from having to bind four pool SRVs it will never read.
//
// The comparator needs BOTH in one kernel, over the SAME rays, in the SAME
// frame -- that is the entire point of it, and a comparison across two
// dispatches would be comparing two camera positions as well as two volumes. So
// VOXEL_MARCH_VERIFY_SOURCE turns both on, and ONLY the comparator kernel sets
// it (through ModifyCompilationEnvironment, exactly as the index probe forces
// VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE 1). The march and emit permutations are untouched.
#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_VERIFY_SOURCE
#define VOXEL_MARCH_VERIFY_SOURCE 0
#endif

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// VOXEL_MARCH_SKIP_LEVELS -- and it is a PERMUTATION, never a uniform
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   0  flat. Every voxel stepped, every voxel tested. THE CONTROL, and it is
//      the same walk that produced the 5.144 -> 6.087 ms indirection number.
//   1  brick skipping: an empty 8^3 brick is rejected by one bit test against a
//      register and jumped over. 512 voxels, no memory traffic.
//   2  chunk skipping as well: a non-resident or all-air 32^3 chunk is rejected
//      on one index dword. 32,768 voxels.
//
// A RUNTIME BRANCH WOULD RE-BASE THE ARM. VoxelMarchSpike.usf recorded exactly
// this: `if (Skip) ...` leaves the other side's loads in the binary, so the
// control pays memory traffic it never uses and the ratio the whole project
// rests on comes out wrong in the flattering direction. Three legs were spent
// this week discovering that comparing two implementations tells you about the
// implementations; this is the same lesson applied before the fact.
// Seven rings to 8.19 km (level 6 landed 2026-08-23; walked only when
// MarchRingCount says so -- the default cascade still stops at six). Bounded
// here so the traversal loop has a compile-time trip count and so a bad
// uniform cannot walk off the end of the index. Mirrors kVoxelMarchMaxRings
// (VoxelMarchRenderer.cpp) and FVoxelMarchChunkIndex::kLevels.
#define VOXEL_MARCH_MAX_RINGS 7u

// The ring cascade arm. 0 = single level over one t interval, which is every
// leg measured before 2026-08-20 and is the control. 1 = the ring loop.
#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_RINGS
#define VOXEL_MARCH_RINGS 0
#endif

#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_SKIP_LEVELS
#define VOXEL_MARCH_SKIP_LEVELS 0
#endif

// FINE -> COARSE FALLTHROUGH (Phase 1, the no-hole invariant). How many
// COARSER levels a ring segment may retry after a miss that crossed a
// NON-RESIDENT chunk: a missing level-L chunk then renders as level-L+1 detail
// instead of a hole. 0 = off, and off is BYTE-IDENTICAL to today -- every
// fallthrough site below is #if'd out, not branched out, for the reason
// VOXEL_MARCH_RINGS is a permutation: a runtime branch leaves the other arm in
// the binary and re-bases whatever is being measured.
//
// THE GATE IS "NON-RESIDENT", NEVER "INVALID", and the difference is the
// biggest risk in this change: VoxelMarchLookupChunk answers bValid=false both
// for "we do not have this chunk" (a hole -- fall through) and for "we have it
// and it is genuinely EMPTY" (correct air -- falling through would let a
// coarser level's fatter voxelisation plug cave mouths, doorways, overhangs
// and the sky above a ridge with rock, a visible regression no counter
// catches). bResident below carries the distinction.
//
// Depth 1 is the intended arm: it delivers "a missing level-0 chunk renders as
// level-1" at a worst case of two walks per segment. Unbounded depth would be
// 21 walks per ray against 6 today, and the gate does NOT protect sky rays --
// sky-band-trimmed chunks above the terrain are LEGITIMATELY non-resident, so
// a sky ray pays the extra walks at every depth it is allowed. Keep it small
// and measured.
//
// Only VoxelMarchTraverseRings reads this. The shadow and GI marchers pick
// their level by their own rule, include this file without the define, and
// compile today's code unchanged.
#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH
#define VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH 0
#endif

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHICH ABSENCE OPENS THE LADDER -- VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL (2026-08-24)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   1 (default) the retry gate is bCrossedShellAbsent: a GAP IN GROUND THE
//               STREAMING SYSTEM HOLDS.
//   0           the retry gate is bCrossedAbsentChunk: anything not resident,
//               AIR INCLUDED. THE CONTROL ARM, and it is the gate that shipped
//               before this date, compiled with none of the shell machinery in
//               the binary (see VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL).
//
// WHY THE GATE MOVED. bCrossedAbsentChunk answers "did this ray cross anything
// absent", and for a sky ray the answer is YES. The streamed set is a thin
// shell around the surface -- about 4.4 chunks per column at level 0 -- the
// sky-band trim leaves everything above the terrain legitimately non-resident,
// and ring segment 0 starts AT THE CAMERA, so the first absent chunk on
// essentially every ray is the air the camera is sitting in. The old gate's own
// comment admitted the consequence in one line ("sky rays DO take the retries")
// and then priced it as a cost ceiling. It is worse than a cost: a retry that
// succeeds at the coarser level puts COARSE ROCK WHERE THE FINE LEVEL PROVED
// AIR, which is the regression the "non-resident, never invalid" rule exists to
// prevent, arriving through the other door.
//
// THE POPULATION IS A READING, NOT AN ARGUMENT. This file's own note on
// bAnyShellAbsent (at :3186 before this edit, quoting
// Saved/capture-zcut-alt120.log) records the old flag firing on 25.27% of rays
// on a SETTLED STATIONARY world. Near-horizon rays enter every ring, so the
// doubled population is the expensive TAIL rather than the average -- and the
// frame-rate gate in force is a 1%-low gate, i.e. the tail is the thing being
// graded. NO NUMBER IS CLAIMED HERE; the leg has not been run.
//
// THE MACHINERY IS NOT NEW. VoxelMarchAbsentTouchesShell already separates a
// gap in held ground from air nobody holds, with six index loads, an early-out
// and the Z probes first; it was already trusted enough to be the trigger for
// the entire level-2 breakdown. All that changes is WHEN it is compiled
// (VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL) and WHO reads it.
//
// WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS, written down before any leg runs, because a gate that
// can only come out one way is the mistake this file has paid for most often:
//
//   * `uncShell` MUST NOT RISE. It counts misses that crossed a gap in ground
//     the streaming system holds. If narrowing the retry gate makes it rise,
//     the shell test is REJECTING REAL GAPS -- fills the owner could see are
//     being dropped -- and this change is WRONG, not merely cheaper.
//   * `substituted` must stay near its parked value. It counts hits won by a
//     coarser walk and parks near zero on a settled world. `substituted`
//     collapsing while `uncShell` HOLDS is the change working: the
//     substitutions it removed were sky and camera-column air. `substituted`
//     collapsing while `uncShell` RISES is the change removing real fills.
//   * `uncovered` collapsing while `uncShell` holds is the intended reading --
//     the air population leaves the metric and the holes stay counted.
//   * FVoxelMarchHit.FallthroughTaken / FallthroughConsidered -- the retry rate
//     -- MUST FALL between the arms. If it does not move, the switch is inert,
//     seven switches in this repo were inert while looking armed this week, and
//     no frame-time reading taken off it means anything.
//
// THE KNOWN RESIDUAL, stated because a bounded change that hides its bound is
// how this file earns its next retraction: a level-L gap whose whole
// neighbourhood is also absent at level L -- a cold start, or flight far ahead
// of streaming -- touches no shell, so it gets NO retry where the old gate gave
// it one. The near face of such a region still touches the shell whenever any
// level-L chunk around it is resident, which is the common case; a region with
// no resident level-L chunk anywhere near it is the uncommon one, and
// MarchIndexLevelPopulated already short-circuits the degenerate version of it
// (a level that streams nothing at all). If that residual is real it shows up as
// `uncShell` RISING, which is the falsifier above and not a separate one.
//
// A PERMUTATION, NOT A RUNTIME BRANCH, for the reason VOXEL_MARCH_SKIP_LEVELS
// records above: `if (bShellGate) ...` would leave the other arm's six index
// loads in the binary, and the control would pay for memory traffic it never
// reads -- which flatters whichever arm is called the control.
//
// HOW THE CONTROL ARM IS ARMED. This is #ifndef-guarded, so it can be pushed
// from ModifyCompilationEnvironment (SetDefine) or ridden as a permutation
// dimension exactly as VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH is, with no edit here. Until such
// a dimension exists, flipping the 1 below to 0 and recompiling the shaders IS
// the control leg -- and both legs must be compiled separately, never one binary
// asked to behave two ways.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// VOXEL_MARCH_SKY_LADDER (voxel.March.SkyLadder, 2026-08-25) -- the retry
// ladder's gate, narrowed by PROOF instead of by a neighbour probe
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// THE PROBLEM, AND IT IS THIS FILE'S OWN WORDS. The live gate is
// bCrossedAbsentChunk -- "did this ray cross ANYTHING not resident" -- and the
// streamed set is a thin shell around the surface. Ring segment 0 starts AT THE
// CAMERA and the camera is in air, so the first absent chunk on essentially
// every ray is that air, the gate opens at every rung, and A SKY RAY WALKS THE
// CHUNK INDEX AT UP TO SEVEN LEVELS INSTEAD OF ONE. The measured population,
// recorded above and quoted from Saved/capture-zcut-alt120.log, is 25.27% of
// rays ON A SETTLED STATIONARY WORLD. That is a multiplier on exactly the
// empty-space cost the marcher is 54% of the GPU frame for.
//
// VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL WAS THE AUTHORED FIX AND IT IS NOT COMPILED.
// It has no permutation dimension and no SetDefine anywhere in Source/ -- the
// only two matches are prose comments -- so it defaults to 0 here and the #else
// gate below is what actually runs. Its answer, VoxelMarchAbsentTouchesShell,
// also costs UP TO SIX EXTRA SCATTERED INDEX LOADS per non-resident chunk, a
// trade this file flags as unproven.
//
// THIS ARM ASKS THE SAME QUESTION FOR FREE. voxel.Stream.SkyMark writes, into
// the non-resident cell dword, that the coverage rule PROVED this chunk is
// above the terrain surface and declined to admit it. The lookup already loads
// that dword for the residency test, so the answer costs a mask and a compare
// and NOT ONE ADDITIONAL LOAD. Air nobody holds is not a hole and buys no
// second walk; every other absence still does.
//
// GETTING IT BACKWARDS PLUGS CAVE MOUTHS AND DOORWAYS WITH COARSE ROCK. A
// sky-marked absence licenses NOT retrying. Any OTHER absence -- pending,
// evicted, never-admitted, an alias's tag, a stale record -- must still retry,
// because those are the gaps the ladder exists to fill. The predicate is
// therefore written as "absent AND NOT PROVEN SKY", never as "not proven
// non-sky": an unmarked cell must fall on the retry side, and it does, because
// VoxelMarchIsOpenSky returns false for every bit pattern this feature did not
// write.
//
// bCrossedAbsentChunk IS UNTOUCHED, at every one of its five write sites. It
// still fires on air exactly as it does today, so `uncovered`, the hole metric
// and the shell counters all read what they read on the control arm. This arm
// adds a SECOND flag and changes only which flag the gate consults.
//
// A PERMUTATION AND NOT A UNIFORM, unlike voxel.March.BlockSkySkip: this one
// adds a field to the chunk cache and a fold at five sites in the walk, i.e.
// register pressure and ALU in the inner loop on every arm that carries it. The
// control must not carry it.
//
// AND IT CHANGES WHAT THE OWNER SEES, so it ships as a measurable arm and a
// screenshot decides. WHAT WOULD REFUTE IT: `uncShell` RISING against the
// control, which would mean the sky mark is being read over REAL gaps and this
// gate is dropping fills that are visible. `uncovered` collapsing while
// `uncShell` holds is the intended reading. FallthroughTaken /
// FallthroughConsidered is the proof of traffic and MUST FALL; if it does not
// move, the switch is inert and no timing on that leg means anything.
//
// AND IT ALREADY REFUTED ITSELF ONCE, WHICH IS WHY THE GATE BELOW IS NOT THE
// ONE THIS BLOCK ORIGINALLY DESCRIBED. Measured 2026-08-25, pinned pose,
// deterministic harness (control-vs-control 0.048%): armed with band 32, 96.33%
// of pixels changed -- a near mountain deleted and the valley behind it drawn
// instead. Two things were wrong and both are fixed:
//
//   1. THE MARK WAS USED BEYOND WHAT IT PROVED. A level-L mark declined a retry
//      at level L+1, whose chunk covers four level-L footprints and whose voxels
//      are a downsample. MarkOpenSkyColumn now takes its bound as a MAX over a
//      dilated set -- 3x3 in XY at this level plus every level the ladder can
//      retry into -- so nothing is marked unless every level the consumer might
//      read instead is also proven air.
//   2. THE GATE READ A TRUNCATED WALK AS EVIDENCE. See the gate itself for the
//      512-chunk cap and the step budget, and why the control arm never noticed.
//
// FOR AN ARM THAT CHANGES WHAT IS DRAWN, THE IMAGE COMES FIRST AND THE TIMING
// SECOND. The band was swept for timing, the knee was made the default and the
// binary was rebuilt before anyone looked at a frame. The timing was clean and
// correct the whole time the arm was destroying the image.
#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_SKY_LADDER
#define VOXEL_MARCH_SKY_LADDER 0
#endif

#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL
#define VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL 0
#endif

// THE HOLE METRIC (voxel.March.HoleStats). Counts, per frame, on the shipping
// kernel: rays marched, rays that hit, hits that came from a level COARSER
// than the segment that owns that ground (`substituted`), and misses that
// crossed a chunk the streaming system does not HOLD while pointing below the
// horizon (`uncovered`). The owner's complaint is holes while flying at
// 30 m/s and until this counter existed the only instrument was an eyeball.
//
// WHY THIS SHAPE AND NOT THE OBVIOUS ONE, recorded because the obvious one
// was specified and is wrong: "ray exited the cascade with no hit inside the
// outer ring" is the definition of SKY. Every sky ray satisfies it, the
// counter would move with camera pitch, and it could never reach the zero the
// gate demands -- a gate that can only come out one way, which is the mistake
// this project has paid for most often. Both counters here are built so they
// can move BOTH ways: `substituted` counts HITS (sky cannot contaminate a
// hit) and `uncovered` requires crossing a chunk that is ABSENT -- not merely
// empty -- plus a downward direction, so a settled world reads near zero and
// a starved one (-VoxelMaxRingLevel=0) reads large.
//
// The bookkeeping it needs (bResident / bCrossedAbsentChunk below) is the
// SAME bookkeeping the fallthrough needs, so VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT turns
// it on for EITHER define. That is deliberate: the most important `uncovered`
// reading is the one taken with fallthrough OFF -- today's shipping picture,
// the baseline hole rate the invariant exists to remove -- and tying the
// tracking to the fallthrough alone would make that leg unmeasurable.
//
// Observation only, on purpose: nothing under VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS may
// change any ray's bHit or THitUU, which is why this is NOT a walk-shape
// dimension for the comparator (see FVoxelMarchWalkShape in
// VoxelMarchRenderer.cpp -- the comparator grades the picture, and this
// cannot change the picture).
#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
#define VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS 0
#endif

// THE COARSE OCCUPANCY LEVEL (voxel.March.BlockSkip). One level above the chunk
// index and EXACTLY ONE -- see the cvar in VoxelMarchRenderer.cpp for the
// measurements that settled both halves of that (a second level moved miss-cost
// 56.5 -> 34.6 steps and hit-cost 48.6 -> 68.9; Levoy 1990 says why).
//
// A PERMUTATION, NOT A UNIFORM, and deliberately the opposite choice to
// MarchZCutEnable below. That arm adds a handful of ALU per SEGMENT and no
// memory traffic, so an off-arm carrying its code carries nothing. THIS arm adds
// TWO BUFFER LOADS PER BLOCK, and a control that pays for loads it never reads
// flatters whichever arm is under test -- the same reason
// VOXEL_MARCH_SKIP_LEVELS' own note gives, and what VoxelMarchSpike.usf paid
// three legs to learn. 0 must be BYTE-IDENTICAL to the pre-block kernel, and it
// is only byte-identical if the code is not in the binary.
#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_BLOCK_SKIP
#define VOXEL_MARCH_BLOCK_SKIP 0
#endif

// LEVEL 2 OF THE HOLE METRIC (voxel.March.HoleStats 2): every uncovered ray
// also reports WHICH ring level owned the ground at its first absent crossing,
// and WHY that chunk was absent, in four buckets whose codes are the readback
// word offsets (VoxelMarchRenderer.h pushes the base index; the bucket code is
// added to it, so the codes below are part of the buffer layout, not just
// names):
//
//   NEVER (0)   the index cell carries no streaming annotation for this coord
//               -- the desired set never admitted it (or the annotation writer
//               is disarmed; the CPU side prints its write counters precisely
//               so a reader can tell those apart). A tag mismatch lands here
//               too, and that is a deduction, not a guess: every admission and
//               eviction of the wanted coord stamps the wanted coord's tag, so
//               an alias's surviving tag proves the wanted coord had no
//               streaming transition since that cell was last written.
//               UP: -VoxelMaxRingLevel=0 (levels 1-5 are never admitted at
//               all, so every miss beyond 128 m must land HERE -- the run that
//               proves the split is not lying). DOWN: normal config, settled.
//   PENDING (1) a streaming record exists; the chunk is queued or in flight.
//               UP: flying faster than generation feeds (the R0 pending=242
//               reading predicts this bucket dominates the owner's arcs).
//               DOWN: hovering until the queues drain -- pending falls to 0
//               and this bucket must fall with it or the tag plumbing is
//               broken.
//   EVICTED (2) the pool held this chunk and removed it. UP:
//               -VoxelHierarchicalCoverage (measured 32,923 evictions from
//               pool overflow -- the second proving run). DOWN: settled world,
//               ample pool (evictions 0 in the shipping config's own log).
//   UNATTRIBUTED (3) the instrument declines to guess: a resident entry that
//               failed record validation (stale index), or a reserved reason
//               code. If this bucket is ever large the INSTRUMENT is what
//               needs investigating, and that is exactly why it is printed
//               instead of folded into a real bucket.
#define VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_NEVER         0u
#define VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_PENDING       1u
#define VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_EVICTED       2u
#define VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_UNATTRIBUTED  3u

#define VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT (VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH > 0 || VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS)

// WHO NEEDS THE SHELL TEST, and since 2026-08-24 it is no longer only the
// instrument: the level-2 breakdown triggers on it AND the retry ladder gates
// on it whenever VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL is on.
//
// SPELLED AS ITS OWN CONDITION rather than folded into VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT,
// which would have been one token shorter and wrong: TRACK_ABSENT is also true
// for the shell-gate CONTROL arm (fallthrough on, shell gate off) and for
// hole-stats level 1, and compiling up to six index loads per absent chunk into
// arms that never read the answer would leave dead loads in the control's
// binary -- the precise way VoxelMarchSpike.usf's note says an A/B gets
// flattered. Every site guarded by this compiles only where something reads it.
#define VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL (VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2 || (VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH > 0 && VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL))
// VOXEL_MARCH_SKY_LADDER, folded with what it needs -- see its doctrine block
// above VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL.
//
// PLACED HERE, BELOW VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT, and not beside its own #ifndef.
// Object-like macros expand at USE, so the forward reference would in fact have
// worked -- and that is exactly the kind of thing that works until someone adds
// a #if that reads it earlier. Defined after its ingredients so the order on the
// page is the order of dependency.
#define VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SKY (VOXEL_MARCH_SKY_LADDER && VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH > 0 && VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT)

#define VOXEL_MARCH_HAS_OCCUPANCY 	(VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE == VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE_OCCUPANCY || VOXEL_MARCH_VERIFY_SOURCE)
#define VOXEL_MARCH_HAS_BRICKPOOL 	(VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE == VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE_BRICKPOOL || VOXEL_MARCH_VERIFY_SOURCE)


// The step budget must be declared before VoxelFluidCollision.ush is included:
// the walk's loop bound is a macro there, exactly as VoxelMarchSpike.usf does
// it, and for the same reason (an arbitrary cvar value cannot be a permutation).
#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_STEP_BUDGET_DECLARED
#define VOXEL_MARCH_STEP_BUDGET_DECLARED 1
int MarchStepBudget;
#define VOXEL_FLUID_MAX_COLLISION_STEPS MarchStepBudget
#endif

#include "/VoxelEarth/VoxelFluidCollision.ush"
// VOXEL_FACE_TOP/SIDE/BOTTOM and VoxelMaterialColor. Included HERE rather than
// left to the caller so that VoxelMarchFaceClass below sits next to the
// constants it must agree with -- the raster path and the marcher picking
// different palette rows for the same voxel is the one way an A/B can look
// broken while both halves are individually correct.
#include "/VoxelEarth/VoxelMaterialPalette.ush"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The placeholder material (source 0 only)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// MAT_ROCK. Chosen because it is (a) a terrain material, so the "is this an
// asset voxel" branch in VoxelMaterialPalette.ush takes the terrain side and
// the whole colour path is exercised, (b) mid-grey, so a wrong LIGHTING result
// is visible rather than hidden inside a saturated colour, and (c) NOT
// MAT_WATERMARK, whose magenta would be read as a debug instrument firing.
//
// It is a #define and not a uniform so that it cannot be set at runtime and
// mistaken for real per-voxel material.
#define VOXEL_MARCH_PLACEHOLDER_MATERIAL 2u   // vxc::MAT_ROCK

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LOD constants
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Direction components below this are nudged away from zero before the
// reciprocal, so an axis-aligned ray produces +/-inf slab distances rather than
// 0 * inf = NaN.
//
// DEFINED HERE RATHER THAN IN VoxelMarch.usf, because the hierarchical walk in
// this file needs it and this file is included FIRST. Guarded so the consumer's
// own copy is a no-op rather than a redefinition warning -- the two must stay
// the same number, and one definition is how that is guaranteed.
#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_MIN_DIR_COMPONENT
#define VOXEL_MARCH_MIN_DIR_COMPONENT 1e-6f
#endif

// "No voxel smaller than ~1.5 px" -- see the header. Sweep this before
// anything else if the far field boils or smears.
#define VOXEL_MARCH_CONE_VOXELS_PER_PIXEL 1.5f

// How many ring levels the source can offer.
//
// Source 0 has exactly one -- the fluid volume is a single 10 cm grid.
//
// SOURCE 1 IS PINNED TO ONE AS WELL, AND THAT IS P3-B1's SCOPE, NOT THE POOL's
// LIMIT. The pool holds all six rings (L0 is 19.2% of residency and the
// distribution is near-uniform -- measured, against gpu-g0-sizing.md's now
// twice-refuted ~80%). P3-B1 marches level 0 only: no cone LOD, no ring
// transitions. Raising this to 6 is P3-B2 and it must arrive WITH the
// ring-transition logic, because a cone rule that selects level 3 while the
// traversal only knows how to walk level 0 produces a hit at the wrong scale
// rather than an error.
#define VOXEL_MARCH_LEVEL_COUNT 1

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PHASE 6: THE COVER LEVEL, AND WHY IT IS A LEVEL AND NOT A SECOND VOLUME
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Ground cover (grass, flowers, reeds, small bush) is authored at 50 mm and the
// world lattice is 100 mm, so cover is EXACTLY ONE LEVEL FINER than ring 0 --
// docs/detail-assets-in-the-volume-2026-08-19.md section 3, and 100 % 50 == 0
// is checked in voxelcore/covervolume.h's coverVolumeInit rather than assumed.
//
// The ring cascade already knows how to walk a lattice at a different scale:
// VoxelMarchTraverseRings divides the ray by 2^L so that one level-L voxel
// measures exactly VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU in the walk's own units, and all
// twenty-four VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU sites stay correct unchanged. Cover is that
// same trick with a scale of 1/2 instead of 2^L. Nothing about the traversal,
// the descriptor decode, the occupancy read or the palette read changes -- a
// cover chunk IS a canonical 32^3 brick chunk (voxelcore/covervolume.h says so:
// kCoverChunkEdgeCells == kMarchChunkEdgeVoxels) and it is packed by the SAME
// vxc::packChunkBricksCanonical the terrain path is packed by.
//
// 7. Seven ring levels now exist (VOXEL_MARCH_MAX_RINGS -- level 6, the 8 km
// ring, took the slot this comment used to hold free). 7 is the largest value
// that fits BOTH the chunk record's four-bit LevelAndFlags[0:3] and the
// VisBuffer's three-bit level field, which is what lets a cover hit travel
// from the march kernel to the emit pixel shader with no new packing -- and it
// is also why level 6 was the LAST ring this format can take: an eighth ring
// would collide with cover here.
#define VOXEL_MARCH_COVER_LEVEL 7

#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_COVER
#define VOXEL_MARCH_COVER 0
#endif

// THE COVER WALK'S SKIPPING IS ITS OWN DIAL, AND IT DEFAULTS TO FLAT.
//
// Not VOXEL_MARCH_SKIP_LEVELS, but NOT for the reason this comment used to
// give. It cited a budget sweep "PASS -> 3.80x -> 32.25x" as evidence that the
// hierarchy misses real content the flat walk finds. THAT FINDING WAS RETRACTED
// THE SAME DAY (see the 2026-08-21 retraction in
// docs/measurements/armA-drawpath-ceiling-2026-08-19.txt): the progression was
// built by picking one stats print per leg, and the SAME leg at the SAME budget
// reads FAIL 32.25x and PASS 1.34x fifty seconds apart. The gate verdict is not
// reproducible print-to-print; only the ratio is.
//
// Independently falsified since: at a pose where the marcher rendered an empty
// frame, the flat walk (SkipLevels 0) produced an image identical to the
// hierarchical one to within TSR noise -- max channel delta 4 -- at ten times
// the march cost. The hierarchy was never losing the geometry.
//
// The real reason to keep cover's dial separate is simply that cover is a
// different population -- a thin shell of 5 cm shells, not a solid heightfield
// -- and deserves its own number rather than inheriting terrain's.
//
// Cover is a 64 m near-field segment. The skip ratio buys little over 64 m and
// correctness buys everything, so the default is the walk with no open defect.
// Raise it deliberately, on its own, once the directional skip defect is closed
// -- and then it is a measurement of the hierarchy over COVER, which is a
// different population from terrain (a thin shell of 5 cm shells, not a solid
// heightfield) and deserves its own number rather than inheriting terrain's.
#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_COVER_SKIP
#define VOXEL_MARCH_COVER_SKIP 0
#endif

// The voxel edge, in UU, at a level. The ring cascade IS the LOD pyramid
// (brick-volume-format.md section 5): level L is 2^L times coarser, and this is
// the only place that relationship is written down on the traversal side.
//
// THE COVER LEVEL IS THE ONE EXCEPTION AND IT IS FINER, NOT COARSER. 1 << 7
// would answer 128x, which is not merely wrong but wrong in the direction that
// looks like distant terrain, so it is intercepted here -- in the one function
// that owns the level-to-size relationship -- rather than at each call site.
float VoxelMarchVoxelSizeUU(int Level)
{
	if (Level == VOXEL_MARCH_COVER_LEVEL)
	{
		return VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * 0.5f;
	}
	return VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * float(1 << Level);
}

// The cone rule. Returns the level this ray should be sampling at, at distance
// TUU along it. Monotonic in TUU by construction, which is what lets the
// traversal test it once per brick rather than once per voxel.
//
// ConeSlopeUU is dimensionless: world-space pixel half-width per unit of
// distance along the ray. The host computes it; see the .cpp.
int VoxelMarchLevelForCone(float TUU, float ConeSlopeUU)
{
	const float WantSizeUU = VOXEL_MARCH_CONE_VOXELS_PER_PIXEL * (TUU * ConeSlopeUU);
	int Level = 0;
	// A loop and not a log2: VOXEL_MARCH_LEVEL_COUNT is 1 or 6, the compiler
	// unrolls it, and an exact comparison against the same expression the
	// renderer uses elsewhere beats a float log that has to be floor'd and then
	// corrected at the boundary anyway.
	[unroll]
	for (int L = 1; L < VOXEL_MARCH_LEVEL_COUNT; ++L)
	{
		if (VoxelMarchVoxelSizeUU(Level) < WantSizeUU)
		{
			Level = L;
		}
	}
	return Level;
}

// THE PER-VOXEL TINT FADE, and it is not cosmetic.
//
// VoxelMaterialColor's invariant 3 gives every voxel its own lightness/hue
// jitter. At one voxel per pixel that reads as material texture; at a third of
// a pixel per voxel it is white noise, and TSR's 32-sample history turns white
// noise into BOILING -- the far field shimmers as the camera creeps.
//
// So the tint is faded out as the voxel shrinks toward the cone. Returns 1 when
// a voxel is comfortably larger than the pixel footprint and 0 when it is at or
// below it. Plan section 5: "fade it as voxelSize/coneRadius -> 1".
//
// smoothstep and not a step, because a hard cutoff puts a visible ring in the
// terrain at whatever distance the ratio crosses -- the exact artefact class
// the ring skirts existed to hide.
float VoxelMarchTintFade(float VoxelSizeUU, float TUU, float ConeSlopeUU)
{
	const float ConeRadiusUU = max(TUU * ConeSlopeUU, 1e-6f);
	const float Ratio = VoxelSizeUU / ConeRadiusUU;
	// Full tint at >= 3 voxel-widths per pixel footprint, none at <= 1.
	return smoothstep(1.0f, 3.0f, Ratio);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What a march returns
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

struct FVoxelMarchHit
{
	bool  bHit;          // terminated on a solid voxel inside the segment
	bool  bStartedInside;// the ray origin was already inside solid (or unbuilt)
	bool  bExhausted;    // step budget spent with segment remaining

	int3  Voxel;         // the solid voxel entered, in LEVEL-`Level` voxel coords
	int   FaceAxis;      // 0/1/2 -- THE GEOMETRIC NORMAL, free, exact (ADR-0008)
	int   FaceSign;      // +1 == entered moving in +axis, i.e. through the -face

	float THitUU;        // distance along the ray, in UU, of the crossing.
	                     // THE FLOAT t. Depth and velocity are both reconstructed
	                     // from THIS, never from an encoded depth -- plan section
	                     // 5 item 2, and the reason is that a reconstruction from
	                     // a 24-bit reverse-Z buffer quantises worse than the
	                     // velocity vectors it would feed.
	float3 HitPosLocalUU;// the crossing point, in the source's local UU frame

	uint  Material;      // vxc::Material of the voxel hit
	int   Level;         // ring level actually sampled (cone rule)
	float VoxelSizeUU;   // = VoxelMarchVoxelSizeUU(Level), carried so callers do
	                     // not recompute it and cannot disagree about it

	// The eight neighbours of the hit FACE, as an 8-bit occupancy mask, in the
	// order the AO evaluator below expects. Filled by the traversal because that
	// is where the bits are cheapest -- under source 1 they are already in
	// registers from the brick's 16 occupancy dwords; under source 0 they cost
	// eight bit reads. See VoxelMarchCornerAO.
	uint  FaceNeighbourMask;

	int   Steps;         // voxel advances, for the census and the cost model

	// ---- where the HIERARCHY stopped, and why -----------------------------
	//
	// ONLY MEANINGFUL ON A MISS, AND THAT IS THE POINT. The source comparator's
	// dump prints both walks' voxels and the brick each belongs to -- which
	// separates a stepping error from an aliasing one, and is exactly what
	// resolved `gained`. It is BLIND to `lost`: when the hierarchy misses there
	// is no hit voxel, so Voxel stays zero-initialised and the brick comparison
	// compares against chunk (0,0,0).
	//
	// So a miss now records where it ended. Without these three fields, "never
	// entered the brick" and "entered and stopped short" and "ran out of budget
	// before arriving" are indistinguishable -- and the third is a possibility
	// neither reading of `lost` had named.
	int   TermReason;      // VOXEL_MARCH_TERM_*
	int3  LastChunkCoord;  // the last chunk the walk resolved
	int   LastBrickIndex;  // ... and the last brick CONSIDERED, -1 if none

	// ---- and whether it actually DESCENDED, which is a different question --
	//
	// LastBrickIndex is set at the top of the brick loop, before any test, so it
	// names a brick the walk LOOKED AT. It does not say the fine walk ran there.
	// The r6 dump showed lastChunk and lastBrick matching the flat walk's hit
	// exactly -- so "it skipped at a coarse level" was already excluded and the
	// question moved inside one brick, where nothing was recorded.
	//
	// These three answer it:
	//   LastFineBrickIndex != the hit's brick   the fine walk never ran there
	//   LastFineEntryT     >  the flat hit t    it ran, but the ray had already
	//                                           been advanced past the hit
	//   LastFineEntryT     <  the flat hit t    it ran from before the hit and
	//                                           the inner DDA missed it
	float LastFineEntryT;
	int   LastFineBrickIndex;
	int   FineWalks;

	// WHICH BRICKS WERE ACTUALLY DESCENDED INTO, as a 64-bit mask over the LAST
	// chunk walked, with that chunk's coordinate beside it.
	//
	// LastFineBrickIndex records the LAST descent, not whether a given brick was
	// EVER descended into -- so a ray that entered the hit's brick, missed, and
	// then descended into a later one is indistinguishable from one that never
	// entered it at all. That made `neverDescended = 47 of 54` an UPPER BOUND
	// rather than a measurement, and it could not separate "skipped past" from
	// "descended and missed". This can.
	// WHICH RINGS THIS RAY ENTERED, one bit per level. Recorded by the ring loop
	// because that is the only place the interval test happens -- a comparator
	// recomputing "did it enter ring N" would be a second authority for one
	// decision, which is doctrine 5 and is how "zero at L1" spent four rounds
	// being ambiguous. entered==0 means the ray never got there; entered set
	// with no hit means it got there and found nothing. Different findings.
	uint RingsEntered;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
	// Set by a walk that SKIPPED a chunk which was not merely invalid but NOT
	// RESIDENT (see FVoxelMarchChunkCache.bResident for the two meanings of
	// invalid). This is the fallthrough gate: a miss that never crossed absent
	// ground is a true miss -- everything on the segment was resident air --
	// and retrying it coarser could only add rock where the fine level proved
	// air. A miss that DID cross absent ground may be a hole, and the ring
	// loop retries that segment one level coarser. Zero-initialised with the
	// rest of the struct by (FVoxelMarchHit)0.
	//
	// On the MISS returned by VoxelMarchTraverseRings this is the OR across
	// every walk of every segment, not the last walk's own flag -- the hole
	// metric asks "did this ray cross ground we do not hold anywhere", and a
	// ray whose absent crossing was in segment 2 of 6 must still count.
	bool bCrossedAbsentChunk;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SKY
	// Did this walk cross an absence that is NOT proven open sky? The retry
	// ladder's gate under VOXEL_MARCH_SKY_LADDER.
	//
	// A SECOND FLAG RATHER THAN A NARROWING OF THE FIRST, and that is the
	// requirement rather than a preference: bCrossedAbsentChunk feeds
	// `uncovered`, the hole metric and the shell counters, and every one of them
	// must read on this arm exactly what it reads on the control or the two arms
	// are not comparable. So the old flag is untouched at all five of its write
	// sites and this one is folded beside it.
	bool bCrossedNonSkyAbsent;
#endif
	// A HIT whose winning walk was COARSER than the segment that owns that
	// ground (WalkL > SegL under fallthrough): the player is looking at a
	// stand-in. Countable at the ring loop's return and nowhere else, because
	// only the ring loop knows both levels. Structurally false whenever
	// VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH is 0 -- no coarser walk exists to win.
	bool bSubstituted;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2
	// THE FIRST absent crossing's classification (VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_*), set by
	// the walk at the moment it first flips bCrossedShellAbsent and never
	// overwritten -- the first crossing is the nearest one, i.e. the ground
	// the owner's eye lands on, and averaging reasons across a ray would
	// launder three different failures into one number, which is the exact
	// mistake the level-2 metric exists to undo. Read from the SAME index
	// load the residency test paid for (FVoxelMarchChunkCache.AbsentReason),
	// so this costs registers, not bandwidth. Meaningful only while
	// bCrossedShellAbsent is true.
	uint FirstAbsentReason;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL
	// THE FLAG THE BREAKDOWN TRIGGERS ON -- AND, SINCE 2026-08-24, THE FLAG THE
	// RETRY LADDER GATES ON. It fires only where an absent chunk is
	// FACE-ADJACENT to a resident one (see VoxelMarchAbsentTouchesShell), i.e.
	// on a GAP IN GROUND THE STREAMING SYSTEM HOLDS.
	//
	// IT USED TO BE DELIBERATELY NOT THE GATE, and the reason recorded here was
	// "bCrossedAbsentChunk must keep firing on every non-resident chunk or the
	// retry ladder changes shape". Changing the ladder's shape is now the POINT.
	// bCrossedAbsentChunk fires on air as readily as on holes -- the same
	// "absent means air" contamination that pinned byLevel to L0 in all three
	// zcut rungs -- so gating on it made every sky ray, and every ray through
	// the camera's own air column, re-walk its segments for nothing, and let a
	// coarser walk answer with rock where the fine walk had proved air. Two
	// flags, still two meanings; WHICH ONE THE LADDER READS is now a permutation
	// (VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL) so both can be measured against each other
	// instead of argued about.
	bool bCrossedShellAbsent;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2
	// WHICH ring level's segment owned the ground at that first crossing --
	// the level the ray WANTED, not whatever level a fallthrough retry was
	// walking. Set by the ring loop (only it knows SegL) on the returned MISS;
	// initialised to the 0xFFFFFFFF sentinel there, and the kernel counts a
	// level word ONLY when this is < 6. That is deliberate honesty plumbing:
	// a defaulted 0 would silently inflate L0 -- the very ring under
	// investigation -- while the sentinel makes an uncaptured ray show up as
	// sum(byLevel) < uncovered in the printed identity check, i.e. as an
	// instrument defect, which is what it would be.
	uint AbsentLevel;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
	// THE ENGAGEMENT PAIR -- "was a retry CONSIDERED" against "was a retry
	// TAKEN", summed over the ray's ladder rungs rather than reduced to a bit.
	// The kernel divides them: Taken / Considered IS THE RETRY RATE, the number
	// that must FALL when VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL narrows the gate, and
	// the only reading that can show the switch is not inert. Structurally zero
	// with VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH 0 -- there is no ladder to consider -- in
	// exactly the way bSubstituted is, and for the same reason.
	//
	// GUARDED BY VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS AND NOT BY THE FALLTHROUGH DEPTH: the
	// counting kernel is compiled once per hole-stats permutation and has to be
	// able to name these fields on the fallthrough-0 arm too, where they read a
	// real, measured zero rather than failing to compile.
	uint FallthroughConsidered;
	uint FallthroughTaken;
	// ---- THE RESIDENT-Z BOUND'S ENGAGEMENT TRIO (voxel.March.ZCut) --------
	//
	// SAME DISCIPLINE AS THE PAIR ABOVE, AND FOR THE SAME REASON: this project
	// has shipped eleven arms in one night that were armed and inert, so an arm
	// that cannot prove it engaged is not measured. Summed over the ray's
	// (segment, walk-level) pairs, folded onto whichever of the hit or the miss
	// leaves VoxelMarchTraverseRings.
	//
	//   ZCutConsulted  pairs where the arm was ON and the level HAD a usable
	//                  bound, i.e. decisions the arm was actually asked to
	//                  make. THE DENOMINATOR. Zero while the cvar reads 1 is
	//                  the arm being inert and nothing else.
	//   ZCutSkipped    pairs whose interval came back EMPTY -- the walk never
	//                  ran at all.
	//   ZCutClipped    ray length, in whole world UU, removed from the
	//                  intervals actually handed to the walk. A cut that
	//                  NARROWS without emptying moves only this, which is why
	//                  ZCutSkipped alone would under-report the arm on exactly
	//                  the near-horizon rays it exists for.
	//
	// GUARDED BY VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS AND NOT BY MarchZCutEnable, because the
	// enable is a UNIFORM: the fields must exist in the binary on the control
	// arm too, where they read a real measured zero rather than failing to
	// compile. Same argument the fallthrough pair carries against the depth.
	uint ZCutConsulted;
	uint ZCutSkipped;
	uint ZCutClipped;
	// ---- THE COARSE OCCUPANCY LEVEL'S TRIO (voxel.March.BlockSkip) --------
	//
	// SAME DISCIPLINE AS THE TWO GROUPS ABOVE. Accumulated by the chunk loop
	// directly onto this struct (it is the walk's own H and starts zeroed), then
	// summed across segments and rungs by VoxelMarchTraverseRings.
	//
	//   BlockConsulted     4^3 blocks the walk loaded a bit for. THE
	//                      DENOMINATOR. Zero while the arm is compiled in is
	//                      the arm being INERT and nothing else.
	//   BlockSkipped       blocks that held no resident chunk AND whose exit
	//                      jump beat the ordinary one-chunk advance -- skips
	//                      actually TAKEN. A block can be consulted, read
	//                      empty, and still not be skipped (the progress
	//                      guard), and that case must stay visible rather than
	//                      inflate the skip rate.
	//   BlockCellsAvoided  chunk cells the jumps spanned, as ray length divided
	//                      by the chunk size at this level. DELIBERATELY NOT
	//                      64 x BlockSkipped, which would carry no information
	//                      at all AND would reach ~8e9 in a frame and WRAP A
	//                      uint32 SILENTLY -- a counter that reads small
	//                      precisely when the arm worked hardest, which is the
	//                      failure the Z bound's group refused a fourth word
	//                      over. It is a LOWER bound on cells avoided: a
	//                      diagonal DDA visits up to sqrt(3) times the axis
	//                      count over the same span.
	//
	// GUARDED BY VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS AND NOT BY VOXEL_MARCH_BLOCK_SKIP, so
	// the fields exist in the binary on the control arm too, where they read a
	// real measured zero rather than failing to compile. Same argument the two
	// groups above carry.
	uint BlockConsulted;
	uint BlockSkipped;
	uint BlockCellsAvoided;
	//   BlockSkyLicensed   consults whose all-sky bit was SET. Its denominator
	//                      is BlockConsulted, already here, so the rate reads
	//                      "how much of the space this ray crossed is PROVABLY
	//                      empty" rather than "how many blocks happened to hold
	//                      nothing resident" -- which is the difference between
	//                      this arm and the one that ran 30% slower.
	//   BlockSkyRunBlocks / BlockSkyRuns   A RUN is a maximal sequence of
	//                      CONSECUTIVE sky-licensed consults along one walk. The
	//                      mean (blocks/runs) is computed on the CPU because a
	//                      mean cannot be summed across rays. THIS IS THE NUMBER
	//                      THE ARM IS JUDGED ON: one licensed block is a 4-chunk
	//                      advance off one load, so break-even is a run of 1.0
	//                      and a mean near 1.0 against a high licensed rate says
	//                      open sky is SCATTERED at 4^3 granularity -- which
	//                      indicts the block size, not the mark.
	uint BlockSkyLicensed;
	uint BlockSkyRunBlocks;
	uint BlockSkyRuns;
#endif
#endif
	uint2 FineBrickMask;
	int3  FineMaskChunk;

	// ---- OPPORTUNITY COUNTS, so a defect can be normalised ----------------
	//
	// Six independent worlds gave a disagreement range of 695x (15 .. 10,428).
	// A COUNT cannot certify anything against that, because it mixes "how often
	// the defect fires" with "how much defect-triggering geometry this world
	// happens to contain" -- and only the first is a property of the code.
	//
	// The fix is to divide by the OPPORTUNITY, not by the ray count. Normalising
	// by rays does not help: the variance is in defect DENSITY, not in population
	// size, so per-ray and per-million-rays swing together.
	//
	//   BrickEntries  every brick the hierarchy considered. A general, mechanism-
	//                 agnostic denominator.
	//   ThinClips     bricks whose span along the ray is under the advance nudge
	//                 -- the exact configuration the corner-clip mechanism says
	//                 gets jumped. A targeted denominator, and a direct test of
	//                 that hypothesis: if it is right, lost/ThinClips is near
	//                 constant across worlds and goes to zero on a fix while
	//                 ThinClips itself does not move.
	//
	// Accumulated per ray and pushed with ONE atomic each by the comparator --
	// an atomic per brick entry would be ~13M per frame.
	int BrickEntries;
	int ThinClips;
};

#define VOXEL_MARCH_TERM_HIT        0u  // not a miss
#define VOXEL_MARCH_TERM_SEGMENT    1u  // walked the whole segment, found nothing
#define VOXEL_MARCH_TERM_BUDGET     2u  // step budget spent with segment left
#define VOXEL_MARCH_TERM_CHUNK_CAP  3u  // outer loop iteration cap -- should be unreachable

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CORNER AMBIENT OCCLUSION -- 20 ALU, zero extra memory under source 1
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This is the one piece of shading a marcher gets for free that a mesher pays
// for. The mesher bakes AO per vertex at mesh time, which is why it needs a
// one-brick apron (docs/brick-volume-format.md section 8) and why a greedy quad
// spanning 8x8 voxels can only carry four AO values across sixty-four voxels.
// The marcher knows the exact voxel and the exact face, so it can ask the eight
// neighbours in that face's plane directly.
//
// THE LAYOUT. Given the hit face's axis, call the two in-plane axes U and V.
// The eight neighbours of the face, in (u, v) offsets, are indexed:
//
//        bit 0 (-1,-1)   bit 1 ( 0,-1)   bit 2 (+1,-1)
//        bit 3 (-1, 0)                   bit 4 (+1, 0)
//        bit 5 (-1,+1)   bit 6 ( 0,+1)   bit 7 (+1,+1)
//
// all sampled ONE STEP OUT along the face normal (i.e. in the air cell in front
// of the face, not inside the solid). Occluders behind the face cannot shadow
// it; occluders in front can.
//
// THE VERTEX RULE is the standard one and it is standard because it is right:
// a face corner's occlusion is decided by its two edge neighbours and its
// diagonal, and when BOTH edges are solid the diagonal is irrelevant (the
// corner is already sealed). Getting that last clause wrong produces the
// classic "AO leaks through a sealed inside corner" artefact.
//
//     side1 && side2  ->  0     (fully occluded, diagonal not even read)
//     otherwise       ->  3 - (side1 + side2 + diagonal)
//
// scaled to [0,1] by /3. Then bilinear across the face from the hit point's
// fractional position, which is why THitUU has to be exact.
uint VoxelMarchAOBit(uint Mask, uint Bit)
{
	return (Mask >> Bit) & 1u;
}

float VoxelMarchCornerValue(uint Side1, uint Side2, uint Diagonal)
{
	// The sealed-corner clause. `Side1 & Side2` and not `+`: two solids seal the
	// corner regardless of what the diagonal says, and averaging three bits here
	// instead would let a 2/3 leak through every inside corner in the world.
	if ((Side1 & Side2) != 0u)
	{
		return 0.0f;
	}
	return float(3u - (Side1 + Side2 + Diagonal)) * (1.0f / 3.0f);
}

// `FaceUV` is the hit point's position within the face, both components in
// [0,1], with u along the first in-plane axis and v along the second -- the
// same U/V the mask above is indexed in.
float VoxelMarchCornerAO(uint Mask, float2 FaceUV)
{
	const uint N00 = VoxelMarchAOBit(Mask, 0); // (-1,-1)
	const uint N10 = VoxelMarchAOBit(Mask, 1); // ( 0,-1)
	const uint N20 = VoxelMarchAOBit(Mask, 2); // (+1,-1)
	const uint N01 = VoxelMarchAOBit(Mask, 3); // (-1, 0)
	const uint N21 = VoxelMarchAOBit(Mask, 4); // (+1, 0)
	const uint N02 = VoxelMarchAOBit(Mask, 5); // (-1,+1)
	const uint N12 = VoxelMarchAOBit(Mask, 6); // ( 0,+1)
	const uint N22 = VoxelMarchAOBit(Mask, 7); // (+1,+1)

	const float A00 = VoxelMarchCornerValue(N01, N10, N00); // u-, v-
	const float A10 = VoxelMarchCornerValue(N21, N10, N20); // u+, v-
	const float A01 = VoxelMarchCornerValue(N01, N12, N02); // u-, v+
	const float A11 = VoxelMarchCornerValue(N21, N12, N22); // u+, v+

	return lerp(lerp(A00, A10, FaceUV.x), lerp(A01, A11, FaceUV.x), FaceUV.y);
}

// The two in-plane axes of a face, in a FIXED order, so the mask the traversal
// writes and the UV the shader computes cannot disagree. (axis 0 -> (1,2),
// axis 1 -> (2,0), axis 2 -> (0,1) -- the right-handed cycle, so the pair is
// oriented consistently and the bilinear does not flip on one axis.)
void VoxelMarchFaceAxes(int FaceAxis, out int OutU, out int OutV)
{
	OutU = (FaceAxis + 1) % 3;
	OutV = (FaceAxis + 2) % 3;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SOURCE 0: the fluid occupancy volume
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HAS_OCCUPANCY

// Gather the eight in-plane neighbours of a face, one step out along the
// normal. Eight bit reads on this source, because a flat bitfield has no
// locality guarantee we can exploit; under source 1 these are eight bit TESTS
// against the brick's 16 occupancy dwords, which are already in registers from
// the traversal itself. The interface is identical either way, which is the
// whole point of it being an interface.
uint VoxelMarchGatherFaceNeighbours(int3 Voxel, int FaceAxis, int FaceSign)
{
	int U, V;
	VoxelMarchFaceAxes(FaceAxis, U, V);

	// One step out along the normal: the ray entered moving in +FaceSign, so
	// the AIR side of the crossed face is at -FaceSign.
	int3 Out = int3(0, 0, 0);
	if (FaceAxis == 0) { Out.x = -FaceSign; }
	else if (FaceAxis == 1) { Out.y = -FaceSign; }
	else { Out.z = -FaceSign; }

	int3 StepU = int3(0, 0, 0);
	int3 StepV = int3(0, 0, 0);
	if (U == 0) { StepU.x = 1; } else if (U == 1) { StepU.y = 1; } else { StepU.z = 1; }
	if (V == 0) { StepV.x = 1; } else if (V == 1) { StepV.y = 1; } else { StepV.z = 1; }

	const int3 Base = Voxel + Out;
	uint Mask = 0u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel(Base - StepU - StepV)) << 0u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel(Base         - StepV)) << 1u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel(Base + StepU - StepV)) << 2u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel(Base - StepU        )) << 3u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel(Base + StepU        )) << 4u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel(Base - StepU + StepV)) << 5u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel(Base         + StepV)) << 6u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel(Base + StepU + StepV)) << 7u;
	return Mask;
}

// THE TRAVERSAL.
//
// One level, so the cone rule cannot change the grid being walked -- but it is
// still EVALUATED and still reported, because the tint fade and the emit's
// roughness both consume it, and because a level that is computed on the arm
// nobody looks at is a level that is wrong on the arm everybody does.
//
// Amanatides & Woo comes from VoxelFluidCollision.ush, which mirrors
// voxelcore/raycast.h line for line and is cross-checked against it by
// tests/test_fluidoccupancy.cpp. This function does NOT reimplement it. That is
// deliberate and it is the same rule VoxelMarchSpike.usf followed: the one DDA
// in this repo that is unit-tested is the one that runs.
FVoxelMarchHit VoxelMarchTraverseOccupancy(float3 OriginLocalUU, float3 DirWorld,
                                           float TMinUU, float TMaxUU, float ConeSlopeUU)
{
	FVoxelMarchHit H = (FVoxelMarchHit)0;
	H.FaceAxis = -1;
	H.Level = 0;
	H.VoxelSizeUU = VoxelMarchVoxelSizeUU(0);
	H.Material = VOXEL_MARCH_PLACEHOLDER_MATERIAL;

	const float3 Entry = OriginLocalUU + DirWorld * TMinUU;
	const float3 Exit  = OriginLocalUU + DirWorld * TMaxUU;

	const FVoxelFluidWalkHit W = VoxelFluidWalkVoxelLine(Entry, Exit);

	H.bHit = W.Hit;
	H.bStartedInside = W.StartedInside;
	H.bExhausted = W.BudgetExhausted;
	H.Voxel = W.Voxel;
	H.FaceAxis = W.FaceAxis;
	H.FaceSign = W.FaceSign;

	// THE FLOAT t, RECONSTRUCTED IN THE RAY'S OWN PARAMETER AND NOT THE WALK'S.
	// W.TEnter is a fraction of the Entry->Exit SEGMENT; everything downstream
	// -- depth, velocity, the cone -- wants a distance in UU from the ray
	// origin. Converting here, once, is what stops three consumers each doing
	// it slightly differently.
	H.THitUU = TMinUU + (TMaxUU - TMinUU) * W.TEnter;
	H.HitPosLocalUU = OriginLocalUU + DirWorld * H.THitUU;

	// Evaluated at the HIT distance, not at the segment start: the cone widens
	// along the ray and the level that matters is the one where the surface is.
	H.Level = VoxelMarchLevelForCone(H.THitUU, ConeSlopeUU);
	H.VoxelSizeUU = VoxelMarchVoxelSizeUU(H.Level);

	// Steps, derived from the walk's own output exactly as the spike does it,
	// so VoxelFluidCollision.ush stays free of a field that exists only for a
	// measurement.
	const float3 SubEntry = Entry;
	const float3 SubExit = Exit;
	const int3 V0 = (int3)floor(SubEntry / VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU);
	const int3 V1 = W.Hit ? (W.Voxel - FluidVolumeOriginVoxel)
	                      : (int3)floor(SubExit / VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU);
	const int3 D = abs(V1 - V0);
	H.Steps = D.x + D.y + D.z;

	if (H.bHit)
	{
		H.FaceNeighbourMask = VoxelMarchGatherFaceNeighbours(H.Voxel, H.FaceAxis, H.FaceSign);
	}
	return H;
}

#endif // VOXEL_MARCH_HAS_OCCUPANCY

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The hit point's position WITHIN its face, in [0,1]^2, in the (U,V) order
// VoxelMarchFaceAxes fixes. Shared by both sources -- it is arithmetic on the
// hit, not a property of the volume.
//
// The subtraction is done in VOXEL units and then frac'd, rather than in UU and
// divided, because at 4 km a UU coordinate has already lost the fractional part
// this needs. (Same argument VoxelPalettePack makes about doing its arithmetic
// chunk-locally.)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
float2 VoxelMarchFaceUV(FVoxelMarchHit H)
{
	int U, V;
	VoxelMarchFaceAxes(H.FaceAxis, U, V);
	const float3 InVoxels = H.HitPosLocalUU / H.VoxelSizeUU;
	const float3 F = frac(InVoxels);
	const float FU = (U == 0) ? F.x : ((U == 1) ? F.y : F.z);
	const float FV = (V == 0) ? F.x : ((V == 1) ? F.y : F.z);
	return float2(FU, FV);
}

// The world-space geometric normal. EXACT, and free: the DDA already told us
// which face it crossed, so this is three selects and a negate and there is no
// derivative, no interpolant, and no normal map anywhere in this renderer.
//
// A ray entering with FaceSign +1 crossed the voxel's -axis face, so the
// outward normal points in -FaceSign.
float3 VoxelMarchFaceNormal(FVoxelMarchHit H)
{
	const float S = -float(H.FaceSign);
	return float3(H.FaceAxis == 0 ? S : 0.0f,
	              H.FaceAxis == 1 ? S : 0.0f,
	              H.FaceAxis == 2 ? S : 0.0f);
}

// vxc::FaceClass from the crossed face. Mirrors VoxelPaletteFaceClass in
// VoxelMaterialPalette.ush term for term -- only a Z face can be top or bottom
// -- and it MUST agree with it, because the raster path and the marcher have to
// pick the same palette row for the same voxel or the A/B is meaningless.
uint VoxelMarchFaceClass(FVoxelMarchHit H)
{
	if (H.FaceAxis != 2)
	{
		return VOXEL_FACE_SIDE;
	}
	// Outward normal points +z (a top) when the ray entered moving in -z.
	return (H.FaceSign < 0) ? VOXEL_FACE_TOP : VOXEL_FACE_BOTTOM;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SOURCE 1: the resident brick pool
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// P3-B1. LEVEL 0 ONLY: no cone LOD, no ring transitions, no mip pyramid, and no
// empty-space skipping beyond the L1 brick-mask test that costs nothing. That
// scope is deliberate -- it forces the one unavoidable unknown (the GPU chunk
// index) and delivers the first real per-voxel material, while excluding the
// two places correctness risk actually lives.
//
// THE DELIVERABLE IS ONE NUMBER: marchMs against the 5.06-5.21 ms cluster the
// occupancy source produced at the same pose and budget. That difference is the
// per-step cost of five indirections, and every projection in the plan is
// downstream of it. `0.13 + steps * 5.9 us` was measured on a 1-bit flat volume
// and does not survive this; it is dead until re-measured here.
//
// ===========================================================================
// WHY THIS FILE CONTAINS A SECOND DDA, WHICH IS NORMALLY A MISTAKE
// ===========================================================================
//
// VoxelFluidWalkVoxelLine is the one Amanatides & Woo walk in this repo that is
// unit-tested -- it mirrors voxelcore/raycast.h line for line and
// tests/test_fluidoccupancy.cpp cross-checks the two on real geometry. The
// occupancy source calls it and does not reimplement it, on purpose.
//
// It cannot be reused here. Its solidity test is a direct call to
// VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel, which reads a flat bitfield; there is no hook to swap
// in a five-indirection lookup, and adding one would mean editing the fluid
// contract file that the solver, the collision tests and the CPU mirror all
// depend on. That is a worse trade than a second walk.
//
// So the walk below is a STRUCTURAL MIRROR of VoxelFluidWalkVoxelLine: same
// initialisation, same tie rule (lowest axis first, raycast.h:74-80), same
// segment-exhausted test, same budget semantics, same started-inside handling,
// same plain-array-not-vector trick for the axis-indexed state. Only the
// solidity call differs.
//
// AND THE THING THAT MAKES THAT ACCEPTABLE IS THE INSTRUMENT, NOT THE CARE.
// voxel.March.VerifySource marches BOTH sources over the SAME rays in the SAME
// frame and compares (hit, voxel, t) per pixel inside the 51.2 m window where
// the occupancy volume has an opinion. A mirrored walk that has drifted shows
// up there as a disagreement, on real geometry, every frame it is enabled. That
// gate was pre-registered before this code was written, which is the only order
// in which it means anything.
//
// ===========================================================================
// THE FIVE INDIRECTIONS, AND WHICH OF THEM ARE FREE
// ===========================================================================
//
//   1. chunk index      worldVoxel >> 5 -> a dword. Cached across the whole
//                       chunk, so it is one load per 32 voxels of travel.
//   2. chunk record     32 B at the slot. Cached with the chunk.
//   3. L1 brick mask    A BIT TEST against a register already loaded with the
//                       record. FREE, and it rejects an empty brick with no
//                       memory traffic at all -- format section 5's whole point.
//   4. brick descriptor 8 B. Cached across the brick, so one load per 8 voxels.
//                       A uniform-AIR brick short-circuits here with no further
//                       fetch; a uniform-SOLID brick hits immediately and its
//                       material is IN the descriptor.
//   5. occupancy dword  one load per voxel tested.
//
// 3 and 4's short circuits pull the cost DOWN relative to the flat volume; 1, 2
// and 5 push it up. The net is the number this phase exists to produce.
//
// ONE DELIBERATE DEVIATION FROM FORMAT SECTION 3, STATED SO IT IS NOT MISTAKEN
// FOR AN OVERSIGHT. The format's design is "one 64 B occupancy load per entered
// mixed brick, then up to 22 DDA steps against registers with zero further
// memory traffic." This loads ONE DWORD PER VOXEL TESTED instead. The reason is
// that 16 dwords held across a loop are indexed dynamically by cellIndex >> 5,
// and DXC spills a dynamically indexed array to scratch memory -- which is
// slower than the loads it was meant to replace. The register-resident form
// pays off when a brick is traversed many times in a row, which is what the
// hierarchical walk in P3-B2 produces and what this step deliberately does not
// build. Revisit it there, with the thing that makes it worth having.
//
// ===========================================================================
// THE INDEX IS A HINT. THE RECORD IS AUTHORITATIVE.
// ===========================================================================
//
// Every 32 B record carries its own OriginVoxel and ring level, in the cache
// line the lookup already fetched. This traversal CHECKS them against the chunk
// it asked for, and treats a mismatch as "not resident".
//
// That check is what makes a stale index a MISS rather than a WRONG CHUNK -- one
// chunk's bricks drawn at another chunk's coordinates, which renders as terrain
// and not as an error. It costs two comparisons on data already in registers.
//
// It matters more than it looks: FVoxelBrickPool::GetEvictions() is zero today
// only because nothing calls RemoveChunk, so the index delta's Removed list is
// always empty and a consumer that never implemented removal would pass every
// test that currently exists. This check is what holds when that changes.

#if VOXEL_MARCH_HAS_BRICKPOOL

// ---- the pool's arenas, names from VOXEL_BRICK_POOL_PARAMETERS() ----------
//
// STRUCTURED, NOT TYPED, and the descriptors are uint2. A typed view over a
// structured buffer binds without complaint and then reads plausible nonsense;
// the pool's own writer reads descriptors as uint2 and the pairing of
// OccWord/MatWord is what makes a descriptor one thing.
StructuredBuffer<uint2> VoxelBrickDesc;
StructuredBuffer<uint>  VoxelBrickOcc;
StructuredBuffer<uint>  VoxelBrickMat;
StructuredBuffer<uint>  VoxelBrickChunkTable;
uint VoxelBrickDescSlots;
uint VoxelBrickOccWords;
uint VoxelBrickMatWords;
uint VoxelBrickChunkSlots;
uint VoxelBrickChunkRecordDwords;   // from FVoxelBrickPool::kChunkRecordDwords

// ---- the chunk index (VoxelMarchChunkIndex.h) -----------------------------
Buffer<uint> MarchChunkIndex;
uint3 MarchIndexDimChunks;   // powers of two; the wrap is a mask
// The local frame's origin, in LEVEL-0 voxel coordinates. Mirrors
// FluidVolumeOriginVoxel exactly: local UU is camera-relative and bounded by
// the ring, so it stays exact in float, and one integer add recovers the world
// voxel. A world-voxel float at planet scale is quantised coarser than a voxel
// long before it gets here.
int3 MarchBrickOriginVoxel;
// Cells per level in the index. The grid is kLevels deep, contiguous, so a
// level-L cell is at L * this + the wrapped xyz. One uniform rather than
// recomputing DimX*DimY*DimZ at every lookup.
uint MarchIndexCellsPerLevel;
// Ring segments as plain t intervals in UU, near to far. The ray starts at the
// camera with a normalised direction, so distance from the camera to P(t) IS t
// and a ring is an interval -- no cone rule is needed to SELECT the level
// because residency already decides it (only level-L chunks exist in ring L).
uint  MarchRingCount;
// Ring 0's outer radius in UU. EVERY OTHER BOUND IS DERIVED FROM IT, because
// the cascade is geometric by construction: ring L covers [R0 * 2^(L-1),
// R0 * 2^L) and ring 0 covers [0, R0). That is exactly kDefaultRingPresets --
// 0-128, 128-256, 256-512, 512-1024, 1024-2048, 2048-4096 m.
//
// CLOSED FORM RATHER THAN AN ARRAY OF EIGHT FLOATS, and the reason is the one
// this file keeps relearning: eight uploaded bounds are eight chances for the
// shader's idea of a ring to drift from the CPU's. One number cannot disagree
// with itself. The ring-discordant classifier and the frame reach read the same
// derivation, so all three are the same authority.
float MarchRing0OuterUU;
// The CAMERA's voxel, level 0. The vis pack is relative to THIS at the hit's own
// level when the cascade is on -- see VoxelMarchPackVis for why the frame origin
// stops working at six rings.
int3 MarchPackOriginVoxel;

// ---- PHASE 6: ground cover ------------------------------------------------
//
// Which slot of the index grid holds cover chunks. The host owns the mapping
// (FVoxelMarchChunkIndex::GridSlotForLevel) because how many ring grids sit in
// front of it is a host sizing decision; a literal here is how the two halves
// of an index drift apart.
uint MarchCoverIndexGrid;
// How far cover is marched, in UU. ZERO IS A LEGAL VALUE AND MEANS OFF -- the
// cover segment is then empty and the overlay costs one compare. Bounded on the
// host against the cover index grid's own aliasing proof, so a leg cannot ask
// for a reach the grid cannot address.
float MarchCoverReachUU;

// ---- THE RESIDENT-Z BOUND (voxel.March.ZCut) -------------------------------
//
// THE GAP THIS CLOSES. The ring walk is bounded HORIZONTALLY -- ring radii,
// corrected from spheres to CYLINDERS on 2026-08-20, see the long note in
// VoxelMarchTraverseRings -- and bounded VERTICALLY not at all. The streamed
// set is a thin shell around the surface (about 4.4 chunks per column at level
// 0), so a near-vertical ray steps through kilometres of air nothing holds, one
// scattered load into a ~67 MB buffer per chunk. Measured: VoxelMarch.March
// swings 1.108 -> 5.638 ms across a pitch sweep on ONE spawn, 5.1x, tracking
// EMPTY SPACE CROSSED rather than geometry hit. MarchStepBudget cannot bound it
// -- its only consumer is inside the BRICK loop and an empty ray never reaches
// a brick.
//
// 1 = cut, 0 = do not. A UNIFORM AND NOT A PERMUTATION, which is a deliberate
// departure from Rings / Fallthrough / HoleStats: those are permutations
// because a runtime branch would leave the OTHER ARM'S LOADS in the binary and
// re-base the control. This arm adds no load and no memory traffic -- a handful
// of ALU per SEGMENT, at most seven per ray, against hundreds of chunk steps --
// so there is no traffic for an off-arm to carry, and one build then serves
// both arms of a pitch sweep.
int MarchZCutEnable;
// One int4 per INDEX GRID SLOT (8 of them -- kGridSlots, seven rings plus
// cover). The size is spelled as a literal here and as [8] in the parameter
// struct; the static_assert beside the fill in VoxelMarchBindPool is the one
// authority that checks the two agree.
//
//   .x  min chunk Z at THIS SLOT'S OWN LATTICE, already padded outward
//   .y  max chunk Z, already padded outward, INCLUSIVE
//   .z  1 = usable; 0 = DO NOT CUT THIS SLOT
//   .w  unused
//
// PADDED ON THE HOST, NOT HERE, so there is exactly one place the pad can be
// forgotten. The pad is at least 1 chunk and that minimum is load-bearing: it
// is what guarantees every chunk this cut removes is at least TWO chunks in Z
// from anything resident, so none of them could have been face-adjacent to a
// resident chunk and none of them could have set bCrossedShellAbsent. That is
// the whole reason the retry ladder's meaning survives this change.
//
// .z == 0 FOR AN EMPTY SLOT, ON PURPOSE. VoxelMarchAbsentTouchesShell
// short-circuits to TRUE for a slot holding nothing (see MarchIndexLevelPopulated
// below), so cutting an empty slot would have to reproduce that short-circuit to
// keep the flag's meaning. Refusing to cut it is the conservative answer and
// costs an empty slot nothing it was not already paying.
int4 MarchLevelChunkZ[8];

#if VOXEL_MARCH_BLOCK_SKIP
// ---- THE COARSE OCCUPANCY LEVEL'S TWO BITFIELDS ---------------------------
//
// One bit per 4x4x4 block of chunk cells, per index grid slot: 32x32x32 = 32,768
// blocks a slot, 8 slots, 262,144 bits = 32 KiB each. Written by
// FVoxelMarchChunkIndex at the index publish -- the single point at which "this
// chunk is resident at this cell" becomes true -- and never by the worklist,
// which does not write the march index at all.
//
//   MarchBlockOccupied   >= 1 chunk cell in this block is RESIDENT.
//   MarchBlockAnyAbsent  >= 1 chunk cell in this block is NOT resident.
//
// TWO BITS BECAUSE THE FLAG NEEDS TWO ANSWERS, AND THAT IS CORRECTNESS RATHER
// THAN FIDELITY. bCrossedAbsentChunk gates the fine->coarse ladder that keeps
// black arcs out of LOD boundaries, and the walk below sets it for NON-RESIDENT
// chunks and deliberately NOT for resident-empty ones -- a resident-empty chunk
// is real air, and letting a coarser level stand in for it plugs cave mouths,
// doorways and overhangs with rock. So a skip has to know WHICH KIND of
// emptiness it just jumped over, and it READS that rather than deriving it from
// !Occupied. (Today the index carries only residency -- kAnySolidBit is a
// hardcoded 1 at every writer and says nothing -- so !Occupied does in fact
// imply AnyAbsent. The read is what stops that becoming a silent hole the day
// Occupied gains a solidity meaning; see the header's note.)
//
// DECLARED ONLY ON THIS PERMUTATION. The C++ parameter struct declares them on
// every arm (an unbound Buffer<uint> reads as ZEROS, and zeros in Occupied mean
// "nothing is resident anywhere", i.e. skip the world), but the off arm must not
// carry the loads -- so the globals exist only where something reads them,
// exactly as VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL's six index probes do.
Buffer<uint> MarchBlockOccupied;
Buffer<uint> MarchBlockAnyAbsent;
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SKY LICENCE (voxel.March.BlockSkySkip)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   MarchBlockAllSky  every one of this block's 64 chunk cells carries a valid
//                     open-sky mark AND none of them is resident.
//
// WHY THIS BIT AND NOT THE ONE ABOVE. `Occupied == 0` means ALL 64 ARE
// NON-RESIDENT, and non-resident has to be treated as BLOCKING here, because
// air is not resident in this world -- only a thin surface shell streams, and a
// chunk that has not streamed yet may contain surface. So the occupancy bit
// could never license an advance; it could only say "you may pass through what
// you would have walked anyway", which is why the arm paid 23.7 block tests per
// ray to avoid 11.2 cells and came out 30% slower. This bit is a PROOF from the
// streaming side that there is nothing here, so it licenses the jump outright.
//
// UNBOUND READS AS ZEROS AND THAT IS THE SAFE DIRECTION FOR THIS ONE, the exact
// opposite of MarchBlockOccupied's. Zero means "no block is provably sky", so a
// missing buffer makes the arm INERT and the marcher descends exactly as the
// control does. RegisterWithBlocks still falls it back to explicit all-ZEROS
// rather than leaving it null.
//
// THE MODE IS A UNIFORM, not a permutation, and the argument is in
// FVoxelMarchArm::BlockSkyMode: mode 0 issues no sky load at all (the branch
// below is uniform and coherent across the whole dispatch), so the
// residency-only arm keeps exactly the traffic its published result was
// measured with, and all three states fit on one binary.
//
//   0  residency only -- byte-identical to the shipped block arm
//   1  both: unoccupied OR provably sky
//   2  sky only: residency alone stops licensing a skip
Buffer<uint> MarchBlockAllSky;
uint MarchBlockSkyMode;

// THE BLOCK SIZE IS PINNED, NOT ASSUMED. Three places below spell 4 as a shift
// or a step -- VoxelMarchIndexBlockCompute's `>> 2`, the traversal's
// `ChunkCoord >> 2`, and the shell test's +/-4 neighbour offsets -- because at 4
// those are the cheap correct forms and at any other value they are silently
// wrong in the direction that skips ground the index holds. The host pushes its
// own kBlockChunks here (FVoxelMarchCS::ModifyCompilationEnvironment), so the
// two halves cannot disagree without failing to compile.
#if VOXEL_MARCH_BLOCK_CHUNKS != 4
#error "FVoxelMarchChunkIndex::kBlockChunks is no longer 4. The shader half of the coarse occupancy level spells the block size as a >>2 and as +/-4 neighbour steps in three places; fix all of them (and VoxelMarchIndexBlockCompute in VoxelMarchIndexCell.ush) before changing it, or the marcher will read the wrong block's bit and skip ground it holds."
#endif
#endif

#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL
// WHICH GRID SLOTS HOLD ANYTHING AT ALL, one bit per slot (bit S set => slot S
// has at least one resident chunk this frame). Filled from
// FVoxelMarchChunkIndex::GetNumEntriesAtLevel in VoxelMarchBindPool.
//
// IT EXISTS FOR ONE CASE AND THAT CASE IS THE PROVING RUN. The shell test
// below asks "is this absent chunk next to ground the streaming system holds",
// which is the right question whenever the level holds SOMETHING and a useless
// question when it holds NOTHING -- under -VoxelMaxRingLevel=0 levels 1-5 are
// never admitted, no chunk anywhere has a resident neighbour, and a shell test
// with no shell would answer "not a hole" for the one run whose whole purpose
// is to read near-100% holes above L0. So an empty slot short-circuits to
// "every absent chunk here is a hole", which is the literal truth for a level
// that streams nothing.
//
// Declared wherever the shell test is compiled (VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL): the
// level-2 instrument, and -- since 2026-08-24 -- the fallthrough arm whose retry
// gate reads the shell answer. The C++ side already fills it ON EVERY ARM (see
// the note directly above), so widening the shader-side guard binds nothing new
// and cannot read a stale or unset uniform.
uint MarchIndexLevelPopulated;
#endif

// Ring L's t interval, in UU. Both halves derived from the single radius above.
float VoxelMarchRingInnerUU(uint L)
{
	return (L == 0u) ? 0.0f : (MarchRing0OuterUU * float(1u << (L - 1u)));
}
float VoxelMarchRingOuterUU(uint L)
{
	return MarchRing0OuterUU * float(1u << L);
}

// ===========================================================================
// THE LEVEL CONTEXT, AND WHY IT IS A MUTABLE STATIC
// ===========================================================================
//
// The walk is written in "local UU with VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU per voxel and
// MarchBrickOriginVoxel as the world offset". Making it multi-level the obvious
// way means threading a level through ten functions and thirty-eight sites, and
// every one of those is a chance to thread it wrong somewhere that still
// compiles.
//
// INSTEAD THE RAY IS RESCALED PER RING. For level L the caller divides the ray
// origin and both t bounds by 2^L, so ONE LEVEL-L VOXEL MEASURES EXACTLY
// VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU IN THE WALK'S UNITS and all twenty-four VOXEL_FLUID_
// VOXEL_UU sites are correct unchanged. (The cone slope is a ratio -- radius/t
// -- so it is scale-invariant and must NOT be divided; dividing it would
// tighten the LOD by 2^L at every level.)
//
// That leaves exactly two things that genuinely differ per level: which index
// grid to read, and the frame origin expressed in level-L voxels. Both are
// carried here.
//
// THESE ARE PER-THREAD STATICS, AND NOTHING MAY WRITE THEM EXCEPT
// VoxelMarchBeginLevel. A mutable global is a hidden authority and this file
// spends eight doctrine entries on hidden authorities, so: one writer, named,
// and the values are the level-0 defaults until it is called. With rings off
// the defaults are what the walk has always used, which is what makes the
// rings-off arm a bit-exact control rather than a nearly-identical one.
static int3 GVoxelMarchOriginVoxelL = int3(0, 0, 0);
static uint GVoxelMarchIndexLevel = 0u;
static bool GVoxelMarchCtxSet = false;
// WHICH SLOT OF THE INDEX GRID, AND WHICH LEVEL THE RECORD MUST CLAIM, are two
// different numbers and conflating them is what pinned this file to level 0.
//
// The grid is a run of equal-sized sub-grids and a level-L chunk lives in slot
// L -- for RING levels. The cover level is 7 and the grid does not carry eight
// sub-grids (that would be 64 MiB to hold two populated ones), so cover lives
// in the slot after the last ring. The host owns that mapping
// (FVoxelMarchChunkIndex::GridSlotForLevel) and hands the slot in.
static uint GVoxelMarchIndexGrid = 0u;
// What LevelAndFlags[0:3] must read for the record to be OURS. This used to be
// the literal 0 in VoxelMarchLookupChunk, which meant EVERY LEVEL-1 CHUNK WAS
// REJECTED BY ITS OWN RECORD -- a second, independent cause of hitL1 = 0 sitting
// underneath the comparator defect of 2026-08-21. See the check itself.
static uint GVoxelMarchRecordLevel = 0u;

#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
// ---- THE CHUNK-CELL LOOKUP CENSUS -----------------------------------------
//
// EIGHT PER-THREAD COUNTERS, ACCUMULATED INSIDE VoxelMarchLookupChunk ITSELF
// and folded once per thread by the kernel (VoxelMarch.usf). Observation only:
// nothing here may change any ray's bHit or THitUU, exactly as the doctrine
// block on VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS requires.
//
// WHY STATICS AND NOT FIELDS ON FVoxelMarchHit, WHICH IS HOW EVERY OTHER GROUP
// IN THIS FILE IS PLUMBED. Those groups are accumulated by the WALK, which owns
// an H and hands it up one return path. This census is accumulated by the
// LOOKUP, which has four call sites (the chunk loop, the shell probe's own
// resolve, the fallthrough retry, and the kernel's post-walk shading lookup)
// and returns a FVoxelMarchChunkCache that three of them discard. Threading a
// count out through the cache would mean adding an accumulate at every call
// site, and the value of this instrument is precisely that its total must
// reconstruct from its parts -- a call site someone forgets to accumulate
// breaks the identity and reads as an instrument defect. Incrementing where the
// branch is taken makes MISSING A CALL SITE IMPOSSIBLE: there is one writer per
// outcome and it sits on the branch it names.
//
// A THREAD-LOCAL uint IS THE RIGHT WIDTH. These count chunk-cell decisions made
// by ONE ray, not by a frame -- hundreds to low thousands against a budget of
// MarchStepBudget -- so there is no wrap here. The frame-scale sum is a uint32
// in the readback buffer, which the CPU widens to uint64 per window; a single
// frame at 1552x873 with ~1e3 probes per ray reaches ~1.4e9 and is inside a
// uint32, but only just, which is why the CPU folds per frame rather than
// letting the GPU buffer accumulate across a window.
//
// READ THE ++ SITE, NOT THE NAME. Each of the seven outcome counters is
// incremented at exactly one place: the return site whose branch it describes.
static uint GVoxelMarchCellsProbed = 0u;        // every call
static uint GVoxelMarchCellNonResident = 0u;    // index resident bit clear
static uint GVoxelMarchCellIndexEmpty = 0u;     // index anySolid bit clear (never fires today)
static uint GVoxelMarchCellSlotReject = 0u;     // slot out of bounds, or stride mismatch
static uint GVoxelMarchCellStale = 0u;          // record fetched, origin/level disagreed
static uint GVoxelMarchCellResidentEmpty = 0u;  // record fetched, says entirely air
static uint GVoxelMarchCellBrickOOB = 0u;       // record fetched, brick base out of pool
static uint GVoxelMarchCellReal = 0u;           // a real chunk
#endif

void VoxelMarchBeginLevel(uint Level)
{
	GVoxelMarchIndexLevel = Level;
	GVoxelMarchIndexGrid = Level;
	GVoxelMarchRecordLevel = Level;
	// EXACT BECAUSE THE FRAME ORIGIN IS SNAPPED TO 1024 LEVEL-0 VOXELS. A chunk
	// at level L spans 32 * 2^L level-0 voxels; 1024 is that at L5, so >> L is
	// an exact division at every level we can reach and the level-L grid stays
	// chunk-aligned without a per-level fixup. An arithmetic shift is also the
	// right floor for negative coordinates, which a divide is not.
	GVoxelMarchOriginVoxelL = int3(MarchBrickOriginVoxel.x >> Level,
	                               MarchBrickOriginVoxel.y >> Level,
	                               MarchBrickOriginVoxel.z >> Level);
	GVoxelMarchCtxSet = true;
}

// THE COVER CONTEXT. The same three things, at scale 1/2.
//
// The frame origin doubles rather than shifting right: one level-0 voxel is two
// cover cells. It is EXACT for the same reason every ring's is -- the frame
// origin is snapped to 1024 level-0 voxels, so in cover cells it is a multiple
// of 2048 and therefore chunk-aligned (32 divides 2048) with no fixup.
//
// MarchCoverIndexGrid is a uniform and not a literal because the number of ring
// grids in front of it is a host decision (FVoxelMarchChunkIndex::kRingGrids);
// spelling it here as a constant is how the two halves of an index drift.
void VoxelMarchBeginCover()
{
	GVoxelMarchIndexLevel = MarchCoverIndexGrid;
	GVoxelMarchIndexGrid = MarchCoverIndexGrid;
	GVoxelMarchRecordLevel = uint(VOXEL_MARCH_COVER_LEVEL);
	GVoxelMarchOriginVoxelL = MarchBrickOriginVoxel * 2;
	GVoxelMarchCtxSet = true;
}

// The origin every level-aware site reads. Level 0 until VoxelMarchBeginLevel
// says otherwise, so the single-level paths are unchanged by construction.
int3 VoxelMarchOriginVoxel()
{
	return GVoxelMarchCtxSet ? GVoxelMarchOriginVoxelL : MarchBrickOriginVoxel;
}

// The entry bit layout and the coord->cell wrap now live in a shared include,
// because Phase 2 (GPU-written residency) gave the index a GPU WRITER as well
// as this reader, and two hand-mirrored spellings of the same math is the
// defect shape this project pays for most often. Same tokens, same values.
#include "/VoxelEarth/VoxelMarchIndexCell.ush"

#define VOXEL_MARCH_CHUNK_RECORD_DWORDS 16u
#define VOXEL_MARCH_BRICKS_PER_CHUNK    64u

// What one resident chunk looks like once looked up and validated.
struct FVoxelMarchChunkCache
{
	int3 ChunkCoord;
	bool bValid;        // resident AND the record agrees with the key
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
	// bValid=false COLLAPSES TWO ANSWERS and the fallthrough must not: "not
	// resident" (a hole -- the stand-in level should be consulted) and
	// "resident and genuinely empty" (correct air -- consulting a coarser
	// level would wall up caves and overhangs). True exactly where the walk
	// can trust "empty means air": the index's resident+anySolid-clear
	// early-out, the validated record's own anySolid-clear, and a fully valid
	// lookup. False on every stale/corrupt path (index slot out of bounds,
	// stride mismatch, origin/level mismatch, brick base out of bounds) --
	// those chunks are NOT renderable at this level, so a coarse stand-in is
	// the honest picture there too, matching what the eviction race already
	// looks like today (index cleared, chunk gone: a hole).
	bool bResident;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2
	// WHY bResident is false, pre-classified into a VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_* bucket
	// by the lookup -- the one place that holds both the raw index entry and
	// the coordinate that was asked for (the cache's own ChunkCoord is the
	// 0x7FFFFFFF sentinel on the absent path, so a caller could not run the
	// tag comparison itself). Costs one register on the level-2 permutation
	// and nothing anywhere else. Meaningful only while bResident is false.
	uint AbsentReason;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL
	// Whether this chunk's absence is a GAP IN THE SHELL rather than plain air
	// -- see VoxelMarchAbsentTouchesShell for the captures that made this the
	// gate on the whole level-2 breakdown, and, since 2026-08-24, the gate on
	// the retry ladder itself. Computed in the one place that holds both the
	// entry and the coordinate asked for, for the same reason AbsentReason is.
	// Meaningful only while bResident is false.
	bool bAbsentTouchesShell;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SKY
	// PROVABLY OPEN SKY for THIS coord -- reason 3 with a matching torus tag,
	// decoded from the entry the residency test already loaded. Meaningful only
	// while bResident is false. Computed in the one place that holds both the
	// entry and the coordinate asked for, for the same reason AbsentReason and
	// bAbsentTouchesShell are.
	bool bOpenSky;
#endif
#endif
	uint BrickBase;     // first descriptor slot
	uint2 BrickSolid;   // the 64-bit L1 mask, lo/hi

	// WAVE 2. Per-chunk shading, record dwords 7..9. Read on every lookup
	// because they sit in the same 64 B cache line as the traversal fields
	// above -- that is why the record is 16 dwords and not 10.
	uint ClimatePacked;      // [0:7] temperature u8, [8:15] precipitation u8
	uint SurfaceGradPacked;  // [0:11] QY+2048, [12:23] QX+2048, scale 256
	float SurfaceZRelUU;     // fitted plane at the chunk ORIGIN, relative to it
};

// AND WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE WHEN NOTHING IS THERE. An explicit constructor rather
// than a zeroed struct, because chunk (0,0,0) is a legal coordinate and a
// zero-initialised cache would claim to be a valid lookup of it.
FVoxelMarchChunkCache VoxelMarchNoChunk()
{
	FVoxelMarchChunkCache C;
	C.ChunkCoord = int3(0x7FFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF);
	C.bValid = false;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
	C.bResident = false; // "nothing here" must read as absent, never as empty
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2
	// A cache nobody looked up has no entry to classify. UNATTRIBUTED, not
	// NEVER: NEVER is a claim about the streaming system and this constructor
	// has not consulted it. The lookup overwrites this on every real path.
	C.AbsentReason = VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_UNATTRIBUTED;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL
	// A cache nobody looked up has touched no shell either. FALSE, so an
	// unconsulted cache can never be attributed -- the same refusal as the
	// UNATTRIBUTED above, one step earlier -- and, since the retry ladder gates
	// on this flag, so that an unconsulted cache can never OPEN THE LADDER
	// either. False is the conservative answer to both questions at once.
	C.bAbsentTouchesShell = false;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SKY
	// A cache nobody looked up has proved nothing. FALSE, which puts an
	// unconsulted cache on the RETRY side of the gate -- the same refusal
	// bAbsentTouchesShell makes one line up, and for the same reason: an
	// unconsulted cache must never be able to suppress a fill.
	C.bOpenSky = false;
#endif
#endif
	C.BrickBase = 0u;
	C.BrickSolid = uint2(0u, 0u);
	// NEUTRAL, NOT ZERO, and the distinction matters for exactly one field: a
	// zeroed SurfaceZRelUU would claim a real surface plane at the chunk's own
	// origin, so an absent chunk would gate as though the ground were at z=0.
	// -1e30 is kNoSurfaceGate -- "no gate" -- which is what "nothing here"
	// should mean.
	C.ClimatePacked = 128u | (128u << 8);
	C.SurfaceGradPacked = 0u;
	C.SurfaceZRelUU = -1.0e30f;
	return C;
}

// (level-0 chunk coord) -> the dense toroidal cell. Two's complement makes
// uint(x) & (dim-1) the correct modulo for negative coordinates, which is why
// the dims are powers of two.
uint VoxelMarchIndexCell(int3 ChunkCoord)
{
	// The grid-slot offset (GVoxelMarchIndexGrid) is zero on every single-level
	// path, so this is the same address it has always been until a ring loop or
	// the cover overlay says otherwise. SLOT, not level: the cover level is 7
	// and the grid does not carry eight sub-grids.
	//
	// The wrap itself is the SHARED function -- the same one the Phase 2
	// publish kernel uses to WRITE these cells. If this call site and that one
	// ever stop agreeing, the delta-verify readback gate is what says so.
	return VoxelMarchIndexCellCompute(ChunkCoord, MarchIndexDimChunks,
	                                  MarchIndexCellsPerLevel, GVoxelMarchIndexGrid);
}

#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// IS THIS ABSENT CHUNK A HOLE, OR IS IT JUST AIR? (2026-08-23)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// THE MEASUREMENT THAT FORCED THIS, because the instrument's first shape was
// wrong in a way no counter could show. Three headless captures at 120/400/
// 1200 m (Saved/capture-zcut-alt*.log) read, in EVERY window of EVERY rung:
//
//     byLevel L0=<everything> L1=0 L2=0 L3=0 L4=0 L5=0
//
// and a reason split that sat at never=100% or unattrib=100% -- one bucket,
// all of it, flipping between windows with nothing streaming (records/level
// R0[+0 -0 ev0], jobsInFlight=0) and the picture on screen unchanged.
//
// BOTH READINGS HAVE ONE CAUSE. `uncovered` and its attribution trigger on
// "the ray crossed a chunk that is not resident", and THE STREAMED SET IS A
// THIN SHELL AROUND THE SURFACE -- about 4.4 chunks per column at level 0
// (resident0=28103 over a ~6400-column ring-0 footprint). Everything above it
// (sky-band trim) and everything below it (buried skip) is absent AND CORRECTLY
// SO. The camera flies in that air. Ring boundaries are horizontal cylinders,
// so segment 0 starts AT THE CAMERA -- which means the first absent chunk on
// essentially every ray is the air the camera is sitting in, at level 0, and
// the "first crossing wins" rule hands the whole histogram to it. Hence L0 for
// 100% of rays at all three altitudes; hence one cell's annotation deciding the
// whole frame's reason split, which is why the split is all-or-nothing rather
// than a mixture.
//
// (The backlog's lead -- annotWrites freezing at 290,332 -- is a CONSEQUENCE,
// not a cause: it freezes because a settled world admits nothing, and the
// alt400 rung shows it CLIMBING 27,661 -> 48,018 through windows reading
// unattrib=100% and frozen through later windows reading never=100%. It moves
// independently of the split in both directions.)
//
// THE FIX IS TO ASK A DIFFERENT QUESTION. A hole is not "a chunk that is not
// there"; it is A GAP IN GROUND THE STREAMING SYSTEM HOLDS. So an absent chunk
// counts only if it is FACE-ADJACENT to a resident chunk -- it touches the
// shell. Air five chunks above the ridge does not; the missing chunk in the
// middle of the shell does; and so does the chunk on the near face of a large
// missing region, which is the one the ray reaches first and therefore the one
// that gets attributed. Six index loads, and only until a crossing is captured.
//
// WHAT THIS CANNOT DO, stated because a bounded instrument that hides its
// bound is how this file earns its next retraction: a ray grazing within ONE
// chunk of the surface without hitting still counts. That residual is bounded
// by the near-surface grazing band; the old rule's contamination was the whole
// 25.27% of rays the 120 m rung read as uncovered.
//
// WHO PAYS FOR IT, and that answer changed on 2026-08-24. It used to be the
// diagnostic permutation alone (voxel.March.HoleStats 2). Now the retry ladder
// gates on this answer, so VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL also compiles it into the
// fallthrough arm -- but ONLY while VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL is on, so the
// shell-gate CONTROL arm (fallthrough on, shell gate off) still gets a binary
// with none of this in it. That is the whole reason the gate is a permutation
// and not a runtime branch: a control that carries the other arm's loads
// flatters the arm under test, which VoxelMarchSpike.usf paid three legs to
// learn.
bool VoxelMarchAbsentTouchesShell(int3 ChunkCoord)
{
	// A slot that streams NOTHING has no shell to touch, and the honest answer
	// for it is that every absent chunk in it is uncovered ground -- see
	// MarchIndexLevelPopulated for why the proving run depends on this branch.
	if ((MarchIndexLevelPopulated & (1u << GVoxelMarchIndexGrid)) == 0u)
	{
		return true;
	}
	// Six faces, early out on the first resident neighbour. Not a 26-neighbour
	// gather: a gap in a shell is entered through a FACE by a ray that is
	// walking, and the diagonal-only case (a chunk touching the shell at a
	// corner alone) is a shell one chunk thick in two axes at once, which the
	// 4.4-chunk level-0 thickness says does not occur.
	//
	// SIX SPELLED-OUT OFFSETS, NOT a loop indexing N[A]. Dynamic component
	// indexing of a vector makes DXC spill the whole vector to scratch -- the
	// flat walk's `int v[3]` carries the same warning for the same reason --
	// and the Z probes are FIRST because the shell is a surface: the near
	// neighbour of an in-shell gap is overwhelmingly the chunk above or below
	// it, so the early-out lands on the first or second load in the common
	// case and the other four are the far-air path nobody pays twice.
	if ((MarchChunkIndex[VoxelMarchIndexCell(ChunkCoord + int3(0, 0, 1))] &
	     VOXEL_MARCH_INDEX_RESIDENT_BIT) != 0u) { return true; }
	if ((MarchChunkIndex[VoxelMarchIndexCell(ChunkCoord + int3(0, 0, -1))] &
	     VOXEL_MARCH_INDEX_RESIDENT_BIT) != 0u) { return true; }
	if ((MarchChunkIndex[VoxelMarchIndexCell(ChunkCoord + int3(1, 0, 0))] &
	     VOXEL_MARCH_INDEX_RESIDENT_BIT) != 0u) { return true; }
	if ((MarchChunkIndex[VoxelMarchIndexCell(ChunkCoord + int3(-1, 0, 0))] &
	     VOXEL_MARCH_INDEX_RESIDENT_BIT) != 0u) { return true; }
	if ((MarchChunkIndex[VoxelMarchIndexCell(ChunkCoord + int3(0, 1, 0))] &
	     VOXEL_MARCH_INDEX_RESIDENT_BIT) != 0u) { return true; }
	if ((MarchChunkIndex[VoxelMarchIndexCell(ChunkCoord + int3(0, -1, 0))] &
	     VOXEL_MARCH_INDEX_RESIDENT_BIT) != 0u) { return true; }
	return false;
}
#endif // VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL

#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2
// (raw index entry, wanted coord) -> VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_* bucket. Called on
// every lookup under the level-2 permutation and stored on the cache, because
// the walk that later discovers "this ray crossed absent ground" no longer has
// the entry in hand. A handful of ALU on a load the residency test already
// paid for; see the bucket definitions above the hole-metric block for what
// moves each bucket both ways.
uint VoxelMarchClassifyAbsent(uint Entry, int3 ChunkCoord)
{
	if ((Entry & VOXEL_MARCH_INDEX_RESIDENT_BIT) != 0u)
	{
		// The index says resident but the record failed validation (stale
		// slot, stride mismatch, origin/level disagreement). The streaming
		// system believes it holds this chunk; the marcher cannot use it.
		// Declining to guess is the point of this bucket.
		return VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_UNATTRIBUTED;
	}
	const uint Reason = Entry & VOXEL_MARCH_ABSENT_REASON_MASK;
	if (Reason == VOXEL_MARCH_ABSENT_REASON_NONE)
	{
		return VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_NEVER;
	}
	if (((Entry >> VOXEL_MARCH_ABSENT_TAG_SHIFT) & VOXEL_MARCH_ABSENT_TAG_MASK) !=
	    VoxelMarchAbsentTag(ChunkCoord))
	{
		// The annotation belongs to a torus alias of this coord. That PROVES
		// the wanted coord had no admit/evict transition since the cell was
		// last written (its own transition would have stamped its own tag),
		// which is the definition of never-admitted here.
		return VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_NEVER;
	}
	if (Reason == VOXEL_MARCH_ABSENT_REASON_PENDING)
	{
		return VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_PENDING;
	}
	if (Reason == VOXEL_MARCH_ABSENT_REASON_EVICTED)
	{
		return VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_EVICTED;
	}
	return VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_UNATTRIBUTED; // reserved code 3: refuse to guess
}
#endif

FVoxelMarchChunkCache VoxelMarchLookupChunk(int3 ChunkCoord)
{
	FVoxelMarchChunkCache C = VoxelMarchNoChunk();
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
	// THE DENOMINATOR, counted on ENTRY and before any early-out, so that no
	// arrangement of the branches below can leave a call uncounted. Every one
	// of the seven outcome counters is incremented on exactly one return path,
	// so the seven must sum to this -- the identity the CPU prints as PASS/FAIL.
	++GVoxelMarchCellsProbed;
#endif

	const uint Entry = MarchChunkIndex[VoxelMarchIndexCell(ChunkCoord)];
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2
	// Classified unconditionally rather than on the absent paths only: every
	// early return below is an absent path except the two resident-empty ones
	// and the full success, all three of which set bResident=true and make
	// AbsentReason unread. One classify against five return sites is cheaper
	// to prove correct than five classifies.
	C.AbsentReason = VoxelMarchClassifyAbsent(Entry, ChunkCoord);
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL
	// SKIPPED WHOLE ON THE RESIDENT PATH, and the skip is also the correct
	// answer there: a cell the index calls resident IS the shell, so a chunk
	// whose record later fails validation still reaches the breakdown and lands
	// in UNATTRIBUTED. That bucket has to stay reachable -- it is the one that
	// indicts the instrument -- and gating it behind a neighbour probe would
	// have quietly deleted it. Non-resident cells pay the probe (six loads
	// worst case, one in a healthy shell); resident ones pay nothing, which is
	// what keeps the hot path off it.
	//
	// THIS PROBE IS NOW ON THE SHIPPING FALLTHROUGH ARM, not only on the level-2
	// instrument, and that is a cost deliberately added in one place to remove a
	// larger one in another: up to six index loads on a NON-RESIDENT chunk,
	// against a whole second walk of the segment for every sky ray. The
	// short-circuit order inside VoxelMarchAbsentTouchesShell (Z probes first)
	// is what keeps the common case at one load, and the resident path here
	// still pays nothing at all.
	//
	// WHAT WOULD REFUTE THE TRADE: the marcher's GPU time not moving, or moving
	// the wrong way, on an arm where the retry rate demonstrably fell
	// (FVoxelMarchHit.FallthroughTaken / FallthroughConsidered). Then the six
	// loads cost more than the walks they saved and the gate belongs back where
	// it was -- which is exactly why the old gate is still COMPILABLE
	// (VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL 0) rather than deleted.
	C.bAbsentTouchesShell = ((Entry & VOXEL_MARCH_INDEX_RESIDENT_BIT) != 0u) ||
	                        VoxelMarchAbsentTouchesShell(ChunkCoord);
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SKY
	// ZERO ADDITIONAL LOADS. `Entry` is the dword the residency test below is
	// about to read; this is a mask, a compare and the tag test, all on a value
	// already in a register. That is the entire reason this approach can work
	// where three others could not -- see the doctrine block on
	// VOXEL_MARCH_SKY_LADDER.
	//
	// Computed BEFORE the resident early-out so there is one call site rather
	// than one per return path, exactly as AbsentReason is. VoxelMarchIsOpenSky
	// leads with the resident-bit test itself, so a resident cell whose pool
	// SLOT happens to be 3 can never read as sky.
	C.bOpenSky = VoxelMarchIsOpenSky(Entry, ChunkCoord);
#endif
	if ((Entry & VOXEL_MARCH_INDEX_RESIDENT_BIT) == 0u)
	{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
		++GVoxelMarchCellNonResident;
#endif
		return C;
	}
	// anySolid clear means the chunk is resident and entirely air. Rejected
	// here with no record fetch at all -- this is the cheapest possible skip and
	// it is why the bit rides the index dword instead of the record.
	if ((Entry & VOXEL_MARCH_INDEX_ANYSOLID_BIT) == 0u)
	{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
		// Resident and empty: REAL AIR, not a hole. Marking it resident is
		// what stops the fallthrough from plugging a cave mouth with the
		// coarse level's rock. The record is never validated on this path --
		// the same trust the skip itself already extends, sound for the same
		// reason: the full-coverage span is 80 chunks against the index's 128
		// per axis, so no live chunk can alias this cell.
		C.bResident = true;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
		// EXPECTED TO STAY AT EXACTLY ZERO, and counted anyway. All three
		// writers of the index entry hardcode this bit SET
		// (VoxelMarchChunkIndex.cpp:867 and :1362, VoxelMarchIndexScatter.usf
		// :184), so this branch has never fired -- which is the whole reason
		// the census exists, since the chunks it WOULD reject are instead
		// paying a record fetch and landing in GVoxelMarchCellResidentEmpty
		// below. A non-zero here means the bit became real and the headline
		// ratio is now measuring the remainder rather than the prize.
		++GVoxelMarchCellIndexEmpty;
#endif
		return C;
	}

	const uint Slot = Entry & VOXEL_MARCH_INDEX_SLOT_MASK;
	// BOUNDS-CHECKED AGAINST THE CAPACITY THE POOL HANDED US. A corrupt index
	// then reads as a missing chunk instead of somebody else's payload, which is
	// the one failure on this path that looks like terrain.
	if (Slot >= VoxelBrickChunkSlots)
	{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
		++GVoxelMarchCellSlotReject;
#endif
		return C;
	}

	// THE STRIDE CROSS-CHECK, AND IT IS DESIGNED TO EMPTY THE WORLD.
	//
	// This file carries VOXEL_MARCH_CHUNK_RECORD_DWORDS as a #define while the
	// pool's C++ owns kChunkRecordDwords. Growing the record means moving both,
	// and if only one moves EVERY lookup lands at the wrong offset -- which is
	// not a crash and not a null: it reads a neighbouring record's fields and
	// most of them survive the origin validation below often enough to draw
	// SOMETHING. That is the worst available outcome, terrain in the wrong
	// place with no error.
	//
	// It also catches the second stride failure, which is subtler: a pass that
	// forgets to bind the parameter. An unbound loose uint reads 0, so without
	// this test every lookup would collapse onto slot 0 and draw ONE CHUNK
	// EVERYWHERE.
	//
	// Both become a completely empty world here -- unmissable, and impossible to
	// mistake for terrain. One compare per lookup buys that.
	if (VoxelBrickChunkRecordDwords != VOXEL_MARCH_CHUNK_RECORD_DWORDS)
	{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
		// THE SAME BUCKET AS THE SLOT BOUNDS CHECK ABOVE, deliberately: both
		// are pre-fetch corruption guards, both are structurally zero on a
		// healthy build, and separating them would add a word to the readback
		// to distinguish two impossibilities from each other. If this bucket is
		// ever non-zero the world is already empty and the census is the least
		// of it.
		++GVoxelMarchCellSlotReject;
#endif
		return C;
	}

	const uint Base = Slot * VOXEL_MARCH_CHUNK_RECORD_DWORDS;
	const int3 RecOrigin = int3(int(VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 0u]),
	                            int(VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 1u]),
	                            int(VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 2u]));
	const uint LevelAndFlags = VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 3u];

	// THE VALIDATION. The record's own OriginVoxel and ring level against what
	// we asked for. A stale index becomes a miss here, never a wrong chunk.
	// The level MASKS to four bits (it does not clamp) -- confirmed against
	// BuildChunkRecord, which is the writer, not against the document.
	//
	// AGAINST THE LEVEL THE WALK IS AT, NOT AGAINST THE LITERAL 0. This test read
	// `!= 0u` from P3-B1, when the traversal was level-0-only. It was never
	// updated when VoxelMarchBeginLevel and the per-level index grid arrived, so
	// EVERY LEVEL-1 CHUNK WAS REJECTED BY ITS OWN RECORD and read as air. That is
	// a SECOND, INDEPENDENT cause of hitL1 = 0, underneath the comparator defect
	// found on 2026-08-21 -- fixing the comparator alone would have left hitL1 at
	// zero and looked like a ring-residency problem. It is also what the cover
	// level (7) needs, so the two are one change rather than two.
	//
	// Rings OFF is byte-identical: GVoxelMarchRecordLevel is 0 there and this is
	// the same comparison it always was.
	const int3 WantOrigin = ChunkCoord * 32;
	if (any(RecOrigin != WantOrigin) || (LevelAndFlags & 0xFu) != GVoxelMarchRecordLevel)
	{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
		// ON THE PAYING SIDE OF THE CENSUS. The four record dwords above have
		// already been fetched at this point -- that is precisely how the
		// staleness was discovered -- so this outcome belongs in the
		// recordFetches denominator and not with the pre-fetch rejects.
		++GVoxelMarchCellStale;
#endif
		return C;
	}
	// bit 4 anySolid, bit 5 allSolid. Re-checked from the record because the
	// index's copy is the hint and this one is the truth.
	if ((LevelAndFlags & 0x10u) == 0u)
	{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
		// The validated record itself says empty: real air, so no fallthrough.
		C.bResident = true;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
		// THE NUMBER THE CENSUS EXISTS FOR. This chunk is resident, its record
		// is valid, and the record says it is entirely air -- so the four
		// scattered dwords fetched out of the ~15.7 MB chunk table bought
		// exactly one bit of information, and that bit already has a home in
		// the index dword this function loaded first
		// (VOXEL_MARCH_INDEX_ANYSOLID_BIT). Every increment here is one fetch
		// an honest anySolid writer would delete, at zero added cost, because
		// the mask and compare that would reject it sit on a value already in
		// a register.
		++GVoxelMarchCellResidentEmpty;
#endif
		return C;
	}

	const uint BrickBase = VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 4u];
	if (BrickBase + VOXEL_MARCH_BRICKS_PER_CHUNK > VoxelBrickDescSlots)
	{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
		// A SEVENTH BUCKET, AND IT IS NOT A TIDINESS DECISION. This return site
		// is post-fetch, so folding it into the pre-fetch slotReject bucket --
		// which it superficially resembles, both being pool bounds checks --
		// would move a paying lookup out of the recordFetches denominator and
		// silently inflate the headline ratio. It is structurally zero on a
		// healthy pool; it is counted so the identity cannot be broken by the
		// one path a reader of the stage list is most likely to overlook.
		++GVoxelMarchCellBrickOOB;
#endif
		return C;
	}

	C.ChunkCoord = ChunkCoord;
	C.bValid = true;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
	C.bResident = true;
#endif
	C.BrickBase = BrickBase;
	C.BrickSolid = uint2(VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 5u], VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 6u]);
	// Wave 2: the shading dwords, from the line this lookup has already paid for.
	C.ClimatePacked     = VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 7u];
	C.SurfaceGradPacked = VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 8u];
	C.SurfaceZRelUU     = asfloat(VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 9u]);
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
	// The work the fetch is for. Counted on the fall-through return rather than
	// derived as probed minus the six above, because a derived total cannot
	// disagree with its parts and a census whose identity cannot fail is not a
	// census.
	++GVoxelMarchCellReal;
#endif
	return C;
}

// The fitted surface plane evaluated at a point INSIDE the chunk, in the chunk's
// own frame -- the marcher's half of the quad path's bNearSurface test.
//
// TRANSCRIBED FROM VoxelQuadVertexFactory.ush:326-338, NOT re-derived. The 12+12
// split, the 2048 bias and the 256 scale are that file's encoding; a second
// derivation of the same plane is how two renderers come to disagree about where
// the ground is. The integer form is used rather than its float test because the
// record stores the packing as a uint, so `== 0` is exact.
float VoxelMarchSurfaceZRelAt(FVoxelMarchChunkCache C, float2 LocalXYUU)
{
	if (C.SurfaceGradPacked == 0u)
	{
		return C.SurfaceZRelUU;   // flat plane, or no gradient data
	}
	const float DZDX = (float((C.SurfaceGradPacked >> 12u) & 0xFFFu) - 2048.0f) / 256.0f;
	const float DZDY = (float( C.SurfaceGradPacked        & 0xFFFu) - 2048.0f) / 256.0f;
	return C.SurfaceZRelUU + DZDX * LocalXYUU.x + DZDY * LocalXYUU.y;
}

// One brick's descriptor, decoded. Format section 2.
struct FVoxelMarchBrickCache
{
	int  BrickIndex;    // -1 = nothing cached
	uint Kind;          // 0 uniform AIR, 1 uniform SOLID, 2 MIXED
	uint OccBase;       // dword offset into BrickOcc
	uint MatBase;       // dword offset into BrickMat
	uint BppCode;       // 0,1,2,4,8
	uint UniformMat;    // valid when Kind == 1
	bool bInBounds;
};

FVoxelMarchBrickCache VoxelMarchNoBrick()
{
	FVoxelMarchBrickCache B;
	B.BrickIndex = -1;
	B.Kind = 0u;
	B.OccBase = 0u;
	B.MatBase = 0u;
	B.BppCode = 0u;
	B.UniformMat = 0u;
	B.bInBounds = false;
	return B;
}

// bppCode is 4 bits holding one of 0,1,2,4,8 -- powers of two only, so no
// payload entry straddles a dword (format section 2).
FVoxelMarchBrickCache VoxelMarchLoadBrick(FVoxelMarchChunkCache Chunk, int BrickIndex)
{
	FVoxelMarchBrickCache B = VoxelMarchNoBrick();
	B.BrickIndex = BrickIndex;

	const uint2 D = VoxelBrickDesc[Chunk.BrickBase + uint(BrickIndex)];
	B.Kind = (D.x >> 28u) & 0x3u;
	B.OccBase = D.x & 0x0FFFFFFFu;
	B.MatBase = D.y & 0x0FFFFFFFu;
	B.BppCode = (D.y >> 28u) & 0xFu;
	// A uniform SOLID brick carries its material in MatWord[0:7] and has NO
	// payload at all -- eight bytes and nothing else, which is the whole reason
	// the format is affordable and the direct analogue of Brick::tryCollapse.
	B.UniformMat = D.y & 0xFFu;

	// Bounds, again against the capacities the pool handed us.
	B.bInBounds = (B.Kind != 2u) || ((B.OccBase + 16u) <= VoxelBrickOccWords);
	return B;
}

// Cell index within a brick: x + 8*(y + 8*z) -- already canonical, identical in
// Brick<8>::cellIndex and fluidBrickBitIndex. Do not introduce a second order.
uint VoxelMarchCellIndex(int3 WorldVoxel)
{
	const uint cx = uint(WorldVoxel.x) & 7u;
	const uint cy = uint(WorldVoxel.y) & 7u;
	const uint cz = uint(WorldVoxel.z) & 7u;
	return cx + 8u * (cy + 8u * cz);
}

// Brick index within a chunk: bx + 4*(by + 4*bz). Pinned by format section 6 --
// a z-major producer would pass every per-brick test and fail only as a
// transposed world.
int VoxelMarchBrickIndex(int3 WorldVoxel)
{
	const uint bx = (uint(WorldVoxel.x) >> 3u) & 3u;
	const uint by = (uint(WorldVoxel.y) >> 3u) & 3u;
	const uint bz = (uint(WorldVoxel.z) >> 3u) & 3u;
	return int(bx + 4u * (by + 4u * bz));
}

// The 16 x uint8 local palette, ascending by MaterialId, packed 4 per dword.
uint VoxelMarchPaletteEntry(uint MatBase, uint Index)
{
	const uint Word = VoxelBrickMat[MatBase + (Index >> 2u)];
	return (Word >> (8u * (Index & 3u))) & 0xFFu;
}

// The material of one SOLID cell in a MIXED brick.
//
// The payload holds one entry per SOLID voxel in scan order, so a cell's entry
// index is the POPCOUNT OF OCCUPANCY BITS BELOW IT (format section 4). That is
// up to 16 dwords read on a hit -- once per ray, never in the step loop.
uint VoxelMarchMixedMaterial(FVoxelMarchBrickCache B, uint CellIndex)
{
	const uint Word = CellIndex >> 5u;
	const uint Bit = CellIndex & 31u;

	uint Rank = 0u;
	[loop]
	for (uint w = 0u; w < 16u; ++w)
	{
		if (w >= Word)
		{
			break;
		}
		Rank += countbits(VoxelBrickOcc[B.OccBase + w]);
	}
	// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - Bit is masked to 0..31 above, so the
	// distance is provably below the 32-bit operand's width; Bit == 0 yields a
	// zero mask, which is correct (no bits below the first cell).
	Rank += countbits(VoxelBrickOcc[B.OccBase + Word] & ((1u << Bit) - 1u));

	// bppCode 0: a MIXED brick whose solid voxels are all ONE material. Palette
	// present, payload omitted entirely -- so there is nothing to index and the
	// answer is palette entry 0.
	if (B.BppCode == 0u)
	{
		return VoxelMarchPaletteEntry(B.MatBase, 0u);
	}

	// bppCode 8 means direct global ids and NO local palette, so the payload
	// starts at MatBase. Everything else has the 16 B palette in front of it.
	const uint PayloadBase = (B.BppCode == 8u) ? B.MatBase : (B.MatBase + 4u);
	const uint BitOffset = Rank * B.BppCode;
	const uint PayloadWord = PayloadBase + (BitOffset >> 5u);
	if (PayloadWord >= VoxelBrickMatWords)
	{
		return VOXEL_MARCH_PLACEHOLDER_MATERIAL;
	}
	// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - BitOffset & 31 is 0..31 by construction
	const uint Shift = BitOffset & 31u;
	const uint Code = (VoxelBrickMat[PayloadWord] >> Shift) & ((1u << B.BppCode) - 1u);
	return (B.BppCode == 8u) ? Code : VoxelMarchPaletteEntry(B.MatBase, Code);
}

// THE SOLIDITY TEST, with the chunk and brick caches threaded through it.
//
// Caching is what makes the indirection affordable: the chunk lookup is one
// load per 32 voxels of travel and the descriptor one per 8, because a DDA
// walks a straight line and a straight line stays inside a chunk for a while.
bool VoxelMarchBrickSolidAt(int3 WorldVoxel, inout FVoxelMarchChunkCache Chunk,
                            inout FVoxelMarchBrickCache Brick, out uint OutMaterial)
{
	OutMaterial = VOXEL_MARCH_PLACEHOLDER_MATERIAL;

	const int3 ChunkCoord = int3(WorldVoxel.x >> 5, WorldVoxel.y >> 5, WorldVoxel.z >> 5);
	if (any(ChunkCoord != Chunk.ChunkCoord))
	{
		Chunk = VoxelMarchLookupChunk(ChunkCoord);
		Brick = VoxelMarchNoBrick();
	}
	if (!Chunk.bValid)
	{
		// OUTSIDE THE RESIDENT SET IS AIR, and that is the opposite of the fluid
		// volume's contract, deliberately. "Outside is solid" is right for a
		// collision volume -- a particle must never escape through an unbuilt
		// edge. It is wrong for a renderer: it would stop every ray at the
		// residency frontier and paint a wall across the screen wherever
		// streaming lagged. A renderer's failure mode should be a hole, which is
		// visible as missing terrain, not a surface, which is visible as terrain.
		return false;
	}

	const int BrickIndex = VoxelMarchBrickIndex(WorldVoxel);
	if (BrickIndex != Brick.BrickIndex)
	{
		// THE FREE SKIP. One bit test against the 64-bit L1 mask already sitting
		// in a register from the record fetch. An empty brick -- 512 cells --
		// costs no memory traffic whatsoever to reject.
		const uint MaskWord = (BrickIndex < 32) ? Chunk.BrickSolid.x : Chunk.BrickSolid.y;
		// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - BrickIndex is 0..63 by construction and the shift is masked to 0..31
		if ((MaskWord & (1u << (uint(BrickIndex) & 31u))) == 0u)
		{
			Brick = VoxelMarchNoBrick();
			Brick.BrickIndex = BrickIndex;
			Brick.Kind = 0u; // uniform AIR
			return false;
		}
		Brick = VoxelMarchLoadBrick(Chunk, BrickIndex);
	}

	if (Brick.Kind == 0u)
	{
		return false;          // uniform AIR -- no fetch was needed and none was made
	}
	if (Brick.Kind == 1u)
	{
		OutMaterial = Brick.UniformMat;
		return true;           // uniform SOLID -- material is IN the descriptor
	}
	if (!Brick.bInBounds)
	{
		return false;          // corrupt offset: a hole, never someone else's bytes
	}

	const uint CellIndex = VoxelMarchCellIndex(WorldVoxel);
	const uint OccWord = VoxelBrickOcc[Brick.OccBase + (CellIndex >> 5u)];
	// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - CellIndex & 31 is 0..31 by construction
	if ((OccWord & (1u << (CellIndex & 31u))) == 0u)
	{
		return false;
	}
	OutMaterial = VoxelMarchMixedMaterial(Brick, CellIndex);
	return true;
}

// The eight in-plane neighbours of the hit face, one step out along the normal.
//
// UNDER THIS SOURCE THE BITS ARE NOT FREE, and the header's claim that they are
// is about the format's design rather than about this step. Format section 5's
// promise -- AO from occupancy bits already in registers -- is realised when a
// brick's 16 dwords are held across the walk, which P3-B1 deliberately does not
// do (see the deviation note above). Here the eight neighbours are eight
// solidity queries, most of which hit the chunk and brick caches already in
// hand, and it costs a handful of loads ONCE PER RAY on the hit path only.
//
// The caches go in BY VALUE. A neighbour that crosses a brick or chunk boundary
// would otherwise evict the cache the walk is still using, and the walk has
// already returned by then -- but relying on that is the kind of coupling that
// breaks the moment this is called from anywhere else.
uint VoxelMarchGatherFaceNeighbours(int3 Voxel, int FaceAxis, int FaceSign,
                                    FVoxelMarchChunkCache Chunk, FVoxelMarchBrickCache Brick)
{
	int U, V;
	VoxelMarchFaceAxes(FaceAxis, U, V);

	int3 Out = int3(0, 0, 0);
	if (FaceAxis == 0)      { Out.x = -FaceSign; }
	else if (FaceAxis == 1) { Out.y = -FaceSign; }
	else                    { Out.z = -FaceSign; }

	int3 StepU = int3(0, 0, 0);
	int3 StepV = int3(0, 0, 0);
	if (U == 0) { StepU.x = 1; } else if (U == 1) { StepU.y = 1; } else { StepU.z = 1; }
	if (V == 0) { StepV.x = 1; } else if (V == 1) { StepV.y = 1; } else { StepV.z = 1; }

	const int3 Base = Voxel + Out;
	uint Ignored = 0u;
	uint Mask = 0u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelMarchBrickSolidAt(Base - StepU - StepV, Chunk, Brick, Ignored)) << 0u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelMarchBrickSolidAt(Base         - StepV, Chunk, Brick, Ignored)) << 1u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelMarchBrickSolidAt(Base + StepU - StepV, Chunk, Brick, Ignored)) << 2u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelMarchBrickSolidAt(Base - StepU        , Chunk, Brick, Ignored)) << 3u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelMarchBrickSolidAt(Base + StepU        , Chunk, Brick, Ignored)) << 4u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelMarchBrickSolidAt(Base - StepU + StepV, Chunk, Brick, Ignored)) << 5u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelMarchBrickSolidAt(Base         + StepV, Chunk, Brick, Ignored)) << 6u;
	Mask |= uint(VoxelMarchBrickSolidAt(Base + StepU + StepV, Chunk, Brick, Ignored)) << 7u;
	return Mask;
}

// WHY DID THE BRICK PATH NOT CALL THIS VOXEL SOLID?
//
// Not used by the marcher. Used by voxel.March.VerifySource, on exactly the
// voxels where the occupancy source found a surface and the brick source did
// not -- which is the symptom under investigation.
//
// IT RETURNS THE BRANCH, NOT A BOOLEAN, and that is the whole design. A count of
// disagreements cannot separate "the storage says air here" from "the storage
// says SOLID here and the walk never asked" -- and those two have completely
// different fixes. Reason 0 is the second one, and if it dominates then the
// defect is the DDA skipping voxels rather than anything about the volume.
//
// Fresh caches every call: this is a point query, not a walk, and inheriting a
// walk's cache would let a stale brick answer for a voxel it does not cover.
#define VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_SOLID        0u  // the pool DOES call it solid
#define VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_NO_CHUNK     1u
#define VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_RECORD       2u
#define VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_L1_CLEAR     3u
#define VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_KIND_AIR     4u
#define VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_OCC_CLEAR    5u
#define VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_BOUNDS       6u
#define VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_COUNT        7u

uint VoxelMarchBrickProbeVoxel(int3 WorldVoxel)
{
	const int3 ChunkCoord = int3(WorldVoxel.x >> 5, WorldVoxel.y >> 5, WorldVoxel.z >> 5);

	const uint Entry = MarchChunkIndex[VoxelMarchIndexCell(ChunkCoord)];
	if ((Entry & VOXEL_MARCH_INDEX_RESIDENT_BIT) == 0u)
	{
		return VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_NO_CHUNK;
	}
	const FVoxelMarchChunkCache Chunk = VoxelMarchLookupChunk(ChunkCoord);
	if (!Chunk.bValid)
	{
		return VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_RECORD;
	}

	const int BrickIndex = VoxelMarchBrickIndex(WorldVoxel);
	const uint MaskWord = (BrickIndex < 32) ? Chunk.BrickSolid.x : Chunk.BrickSolid.y;
	// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - BrickIndex is 0..63 and the shift is masked to 0..31
	if ((MaskWord & (1u << (uint(BrickIndex) & 31u))) == 0u)
	{
		return VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_L1_CLEAR;
	}

	const FVoxelMarchBrickCache Brick = VoxelMarchLoadBrick(Chunk, BrickIndex);
	if (Brick.Kind == 0u)
	{
		return VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_KIND_AIR;
	}
	if (Brick.Kind == 1u)
	{
		return VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_SOLID;
	}
	if (!Brick.bInBounds)
	{
		return VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_BOUNDS;
	}
	const uint CellIndex = VoxelMarchCellIndex(WorldVoxel);
	const uint OccWord = VoxelBrickOcc[Brick.OccBase + (CellIndex >> 5u)];
	// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - CellIndex & 31 is 0..31 by construction
	if ((OccWord & (1u << (CellIndex & 31u))) == 0u)
	{
		return VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_OCC_CLEAR;
	}
	return VOXEL_MARCH_REJECT_SOLID;
}

// THE WALK. A structural mirror of VoxelFluidWalkVoxelLine -- see the long note
// at the top of this section for why a mirror exists at all and what gates it.
// Every line that is not the solidity call is that function's line.
FVoxelMarchHit VoxelMarchTraverseBrick(float3 OriginLocalUU, float3 DirWorld,
                                       float TMinUU, float TMaxUU, float ConeSlopeUU)
{
	FVoxelMarchHit H = (FVoxelMarchHit)0;
	H.FaceAxis = -1;
	H.Level = 0;
	H.VoxelSizeUU = VoxelMarchVoxelSizeUU(0);
	H.Material = VOXEL_MARCH_PLACEHOLDER_MATERIAL;

	const float3 Entry = OriginLocalUU + DirWorld * TMinUU;
	const float3 Exit  = OriginLocalUU + DirWorld * TMaxUU;

	FVoxelMarchChunkCache Chunk = VoxelMarchNoChunk();
	FVoxelMarchBrickCache Brick = VoxelMarchNoBrick();

	const int3 LocalStart = (int3)floor(Entry / VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU);
	const int3 StartVoxel = LocalStart + VoxelMarchOriginVoxel();
	// A plain array, not an int3: the walk indexes it by the axis it chose, and
	// dynamic component indexing of a VECTOR forces DXC to spill the whole
	// vector to scratch on every write.
	int v[3] = { StartVoxel.x, StartVoxel.y, StartVoxel.z };
	// WARNING (doctrine 9b): this SURVIVES A MISS. If the walk returns bHit
	// false, H.Voxel still holds this entry voxel and H.THitUU is 0 -- a legal
	// coordinate that is not a position. EVERY consumer must gate on bHit; three
	// of them did not, and the resulting ill-posed population was 30-43% of the
	// arbiter's input for four legs.
	H.Voxel = StartVoxel;

	uint Mat = VOXEL_MARCH_PLACEHOLDER_MATERIAL;
	if (VoxelMarchBrickSolidAt(StartVoxel, Chunk, Brick, Mat))
	{
		H.bHit = true;
		H.bStartedInside = true;
		H.Material = Mat;
		H.THitUU = TMinUU;
		H.HitPosLocalUU = Entry;
		return H;
	}
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
	// The flat walk reports absent crossings for the same reason the
	// hierarchical one does -- the comparator runs BOTH arms through
	// VoxelMarchTraverseRings under one kernel-wide fallthrough depth, and an
	// arm that never sets the gate would walk a different cascade than the arm
	// it is graded against. The solid test refreshed Chunk to the voxel just
	// tested, so the flag reads the same lookup the walk paid for.
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL
	// FIRST SHELL crossing only -- test the flag BEFORE the OR below sets it.
	// The nearest absent chunk that touches held ground is the one the reason
	// split attributes, at all three capture sites identically. Plain air (the
	// camera's own column, the sky above a distant ridge) sets neither flag
	// here and the ray falls through to whatever it crosses next -- which, since
	// 2026-08-24, is also what stops it from opening the retry ladder.
	if (!H.bCrossedShellAbsent && !Chunk.bValid && !Chunk.bResident &&
	    Chunk.bAbsentTouchesShell)
	{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2
		H.FirstAbsentReason = Chunk.AbsentReason;
#endif
		H.bCrossedShellAbsent = true;
	}
#endif
	H.bCrossedAbsentChunk = H.bCrossedAbsentChunk || (!Chunk.bValid && !Chunk.bResident);
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SKY
	// AND NOT PROVEN SKY. The extra term can only make this flag FALSE where the
	// old one is true, never the reverse, so the ladder it gates can only retry
	// FEWER rays than the control -- never more. That direction is the whole
	// safety argument: a retry that does not happen shows the fine level's own
	// answer, and the fine level PROVED this chunk is above the terrain.
	H.bCrossedNonSkyAbsent = H.bCrossedNonSkyAbsent ||
	                         (!Chunk.bValid && !Chunk.bResident && !Chunk.bOpenSky);
#endif
#endif

	const float3 d = Exit - Entry;
	float tMax[3], tDelta[3];
	int step[3];
	{
		const float pv[3] = { Entry.x, Entry.y, Entry.z };
		const float dv[3] = { d.x, d.y, d.z };
		const float lv[3] = { (float)LocalStart.x, (float)LocalStart.y, (float)LocalStart.z };
		[unroll]
		for (int a = 0; a < 3; ++a)
		{
			if (dv[a] > 0.0f)
			{
				step[a] = 1;
				tMax[a] = ((lv[a] + 1.0f) * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU - pv[a]) / dv[a];
				tDelta[a] = VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU / dv[a];
			}
			else if (dv[a] < 0.0f)
			{
				step[a] = -1;
				tMax[a] = (lv[a] * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU - pv[a]) / dv[a];
				tDelta[a] = -VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU / dv[a];
			}
			else
			{
				step[a] = 0;
				tMax[a] = VOXEL_FLUID_NO_CROSSING_T;
				tDelta[a] = VOXEL_FLUID_NO_CROSSING_T;
			}
		}
	}

	float tLast = 0.0f;
	int Steps = 0;
	[loop]
	for (int i = 0; i < MarchStepBudget; ++i)
	{
		// Ties step one axis at a time, lowest first -- raycast.h:74-80.
		int axis;
		if (tMax[0] <= tMax[1] && tMax[0] <= tMax[2])      { axis = 0; }
		else if (tMax[1] <= tMax[2])                       { axis = 1; }
		else                                               { axis = 2; }

		if (tMax[axis] > 1.0f)
		{
			H.Steps = Steps;
			return H;   // segment exhausted, nothing in the way
		}

		tLast = tMax[axis];
		v[axis] += step[axis];
		tMax[axis] += tDelta[axis];
		++Steps;

		const int3 vNow = int3(v[0], v[1], v[2]);
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
		// See the start-voxel site above: same gate, same reason, once per
		// step on values already in registers. The call is hoisted into a
		// bool ONLY on this arm so the depth-0 control keeps its original
		// text, not just its original meaning.
		const bool bSolidNow = VoxelMarchBrickSolidAt(vNow, Chunk, Brick, Mat);
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL
		// First SHELL crossing only -- see the start-voxel site above.
		if (!H.bCrossedShellAbsent && !Chunk.bValid && !Chunk.bResident &&
		    Chunk.bAbsentTouchesShell)
		{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2
			H.FirstAbsentReason = Chunk.AbsentReason;
#endif
			H.bCrossedShellAbsent = true;
		}
#endif
		H.bCrossedAbsentChunk = H.bCrossedAbsentChunk || (!Chunk.bValid && !Chunk.bResident);
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SKY
		H.bCrossedNonSkyAbsent = H.bCrossedNonSkyAbsent ||
		                         (!Chunk.bValid && !Chunk.bResident && !Chunk.bOpenSky);
#endif
		if (bSolidNow)
#else
		if (VoxelMarchBrickSolidAt(vNow, Chunk, Brick, Mat))
#endif
		{
			H.bHit = true;
			H.Voxel = vNow;
			H.FaceAxis = axis;
			H.FaceSign = step[axis];
			H.Material = Mat;
			H.THitUU = TMinUU + (TMaxUU - TMinUU) * tLast;
			H.HitPosLocalUU = OriginLocalUU + DirWorld * H.THitUU;
			H.Steps = Steps;
			H.FaceNeighbourMask = VoxelMarchGatherFaceNeighbours(vNow, axis, step[axis],
			                                                     Chunk, Brick);
			return H;
		}
	}

	H.bExhausted = true;
	H.THitUU = TMinUU + (TMaxUU - TMinUU) * tLast;
	H.HitPosLocalUU = OriginLocalUU + DirWorld * H.THitUU;
	H.Steps = Steps;
	return H;
}

#endif // VOXEL_MARCH_HAS_BRICKPOOL

// THE SAME ORIGIN, AT AN EXPLICIT LEVEL, for consumers that do not traverse.
//
// The emit pixel shader reconstructs a world voxel from the packed LOCAL one,
// but a ring-1 hit is packed in LEVEL-1 voxels -- so adding the level-0 frame
// origin back would place it at twice its distance with no error anywhere. The
// PS does not run the traversal, so the walk's level context is not set there
// and cannot be consulted; the level travels in the vis packet, which is the
// only thing that survives between the two passes.
//
// Exact at every level because the frame origin is snapped to 1024 level-0
// voxels when the cascade is on. The occupancy source has no levels, so it
// ignores the argument rather than pretending to honour it.
int3 VoxelMarchSourceOriginVoxelAtLevel(int Level)
{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE == VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE_OCCUPANCY
	return FluidVolumeOriginVoxel;
#else
	// COVER PACKS AGAINST THE CAMERA'S OWN COVER CELL, in both arms, and that is
	// a tighter bound than the terrain path's rather than a looser one.
	//
	// The cover reach is bounded on the host (voxel.Cover.ReachM, capped by the
	// index grid -- see FVoxelMarchChunkIndex::kCoverReachChunks), so a cover hit
	// is at most ReachM / 0.05 cover cells from the camera: 1,280 at the 64 m
	// default, which is the SAME 1,280 every ring level packs and comfortably
	// inside the 13-bit field. Doubling is exact -- one level-0 voxel is two
	// cover cells, no rounding anywhere.
	//
	// It does NOT go through the ring branch below, because that shifts RIGHT by
	// the level and cover is finer, not coarser: >> 7 would collapse the origin
	// to near zero and put every cover voxel tens of kilometres from where it is.
	if (Level == VOXEL_MARCH_COVER_LEVEL)
	{
		return MarchPackOriginVoxel * 2;
	}
#if VOXEL_MARCH_RINGS
	// CAMERA-RELATIVE, NOT FRAME-RELATIVE, AND SIX RINGS IS WHY.
	//
	// The frame origin sits at camera - reach - snap. At 4 km that is 40,960
	// level-0 voxels away, so a RING-0 hit -- which is at most 1,280 level-0
	// voxels from the camera -- packs as a local coordinate around 41,000 and
	// overflows a 13-bit field by a factor of ten. The frame origin worked at
	// two rings only because the frame was small.
	//
	// Relative to the camera at the hit's own level, EVERY ring is within 1,280
	// voxels OF THAT LEVEL by construction: ring L spans out to 128 * 2^L m and
	// a level-L voxel is 0.1 * 2^L m, so the ratio is the same 1,280 at every
	// level. That is the cascade property again, and it is what makes one field
	// width serve all six rings instead of needing six.
	const int L = clamp(Level, 0, int(VOXEL_MARCH_MAX_RINGS) - 1);
	return int3(MarchPackOriginVoxel.x >> L,
	            MarchPackOriginVoxel.y >> L,
	            MarchPackOriginVoxel.z >> L);
#else
	const int L = clamp(Level, 0, 5);
	return int3(MarchBrickOriginVoxel.x >> L,
	            MarchBrickOriginVoxel.y >> L,
	            MarchBrickOriginVoxel.z >> L);
#endif // VOXEL_MARCH_RINGS
#endif // VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE == VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE_OCCUPANCY
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The origin of the source's local voxel frame
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Both sources express hits as WORLD voxel coordinates and both work internally
// in a camera-relative local frame, but they name that frame's origin
// differently -- FluidVolumeOriginVoxel for the occupancy window,
// MarchBrickOriginVoxel for the brick pool's ring-local frame.
//
// The VisBuffer packs LOCAL voxel coordinates (three 13-bit fields since the
// cascade lengthened the reach -- see VoxelMarchPackVis; world
// coordinates at planet scale do not fit beside the attributes), so the march
// kernel subtracts this and the emit adds it back. One accessor rather than two
// call sites with an #if, because those two call sites have to agree exactly or
// the palette hash is keyed on a voxel that does not exist.
int3 VoxelMarchSourceOriginVoxel()
{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE == VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE_OCCUPANCY
	return FluidVolumeOriginVoxel;
#elif VOXEL_MARCH_RINGS
	// The march kernel packs at the level the ring loop just walked, and the
	// level context still holds it here. ONE authority with the emit's unpack.
	return VoxelMarchSourceOriginVoxelAtLevel(int(GVoxelMarchIndexLevel));
#else
	return VoxelMarchOriginVoxel();
#endif
}


// ===========================================================================
// THE HIERARCHICAL WALK (P3-B2a) -- chunks -> bricks -> cells
// ===========================================================================
//
// THE WHOLE PERFORMANCE CASE IS HERE. At level 0 with no skipping the brick pool
// costs +18.3% against the occupancy volume over a 51.2 m box (5.144 -> 6.087 ms,
// measured, same tiles to within one). That is the price of five indirections
// per step. The bet is that a hierarchy removes far more steps than indirection
// adds per step -- and until this runs, that is a hypothesis with a measured
// cost and an unmeasured benefit.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THREE NESTED DDAs, NOT ONE DDA WITH JUMPS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The tempting shape is a single voxel DDA that, on finding an empty brick,
// leaps t to the brick's exit. VoxelMarchSpike.usf already built that, and the
// note it left is the reason this is written the way it is:
//
//   "Amanatides & Woo crosses exactly ONE plane per iteration"
//
// A jump crosses up to three at once, and the state (tMax per axis) is no longer
// consistent with the position. The spike's version skipped diagonal cells --
// producing a LOWER step count and a plausible picture, which is the worst
// possible failure for a number whose whole purpose is to be lower.
//
// So each level runs its own DDA over its own cell size, and entering an
// occupied parent cell RESTARTS the child walk from that cell's entry point.
// Restarting is what keeps every walk's state self-consistent.
//
// THE PRICE OF RESTARTING, STATED: a fresh tMax computed at a cell boundary
// differs from one accumulated across thirty steps by a few float ULP, so the
// hierarchical walk and the flat walk will disagree on a small number of rays
// that cross two planes simultaneously. That is not a defect, it is the
// reference noise floor, and it is exactly what voxel.March.VerifySource
// measures -- the flat walk against a negligibly nudged copy of itself, on the
// same rays. Read the disagreement against that, never against zero.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT EACH LEVEL COSTS TO REJECT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   chunk (32^3 = 32,768 voxels)  one index dword. Not resident or anySolid
//                                 clear -> skip the whole thing.
//   brick (8^3 = 512 voxels)      one BIT TEST against a register already
//                                 loaded with the chunk record. Free.
//   cell                          one occupancy dword.
//
// The brick level is the one that should dominate: it costs nothing and it
// rejects 512 voxels. That is the property the format was designed around
// (docs/brick-volume-format.md section 5) and it has never been measured.

#if VOXEL_MARCH_HAS_BRICKPOOL

// Where the ray leaves the axis-aligned cell of edge CellUU that contains the
// point at TNow. Absolute t along the ray.
//
// Computed from the CELL BOUNDS and the ray origin rather than incrementally,
// so it cannot drift: every call is a fresh slab test against exact multiples of
// CellUU, which is what makes a restart-based walk safe to restart.
// CELL BOUNDARIES ARE IN WORLD VOXEL SPACE, NOT LOCAL UU SPACE, AND THAT
// DISTINCTION WAS THE BUG.
//
// The first version computed `floor(P / CellUU) * CellUU` in the LOCAL frame,
// which silently assumes the frame origin is a multiple of the cell size. It is
// not: UVoxelFluidSubsystem::DesiredOriginVoxel returns `CamVoxel - 256` for an
// arbitrary camera voxel, so MarchBrickOriginVoxel is 32-aligned only by luck.
//
// The consequence was structural and quiet. Every chunk and brick boundary in
// the hierarchy sat `origin mod 32` voxels away from the real one, so the walk
// tested the L1 bit of one brick while the ray was inside its neighbour --
// skipping real content and, because a skip removes steps, producing a BETTER
// skip ratio and a plausible picture. Measured: 156,031 of 1,354,896 rays
// disagreeing with the flat walk (11.5%) against a noise floor of 6, and a
// 66.9x ratio that was not real.
//
// Deriving the boundary from the WORLD voxel removes the assumption rather than
// documenting it. `>> CellShift` is an arithmetic shift, which is floor division
// for negative coordinates -- the property `/ (1 << n)` does NOT have, and the
// reason the shift is written rather than a divide.
float VoxelMarchCellExitT(float3 OriginLocalUU, float3 InvDir, int3 CellMinWorld,
                          int CellShift)
{
	// THE CELL IS PASSED IN. IT USED TO BE RECOMPUTED HERE, AND THAT WAS THE
	// FOURTH INSTANCE OF THE DOCTRINE -- the first one where BOTH AUTHORITIES
	// WERE THE SAME EXPRESSION.
	//
	// The caller already had `floor((Origin + Dir*t) / voxelUU)`; this recomputed
	// it verbatim. Textually identical, and therefore invisible in review -- but
	// the compiler may contract one site and not the other (FMA on Origin+Dir*t,
	// a different schedule for the divide), so at a cell plane the two floors can
	// land ONE CELL APART.
	//
	// AND THE CONSEQUENCE IS DIRECTIONAL, which is what made it findable:
	//   travelling POSITIVELY, a floor that rounds one cell further returns the
	//   NEXT cell's exit, so the advance lands TWO cells on and the intervening
	//   brick is never L1-tested and never descended -- `lost` with no `moved`,
	//   because a ray that never enters a brick cannot stop on a wrong cell in
	//   it;
	//   travelling NEGATIVELY, the same slip returns an exit BEHIND t, which
	//   max(..., t + NUDGE) clamps -- a one-nudge crawl that self-corrects.
	// Measured: 92% of disagreeing rays travelling positively.
	const float3 CellMinUU =
		float3(CellMinWorld - VoxelMarchOriginVoxel()) * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU;
	const float CellUU = VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * float(1 << CellShift);
	const float3 T0 = (CellMinUU - OriginLocalUU) * InvDir;
	const float3 T1 = (CellMinUU + CellUU - OriginLocalUU) * InvDir;
	const float3 THi = max(T0, T1);
	return min(min(THi.x, THi.y), THi.z);
}

// ONE AUTHORITY FOR THE RAY'S INVERSE DIRECTION. The traversal built this
// inline, and the coincidence diagnostic below needs the IDENTICAL value -- a
// ray whose slab test used a different epsilon than the walk did would report
// spans the walk never saw. Doctrine: the same expression written twice is
// still two authorities, and this is the cheapest possible place to have one.
// Returns the SAFE DIRECTION, not its inverse. Deliberate: the walk steps
// positions with SafeDir and divides for InvDir, and 1/(1/x) IS NOT x in float
// -- returning the inverse here and reconstructing SafeDir from it would have
// perturbed every position step in the traversal to buy nothing.
float3 VoxelMarchSafeDir(float3 DirWorld)
{
	return DirWorld + float3(abs(DirWorld) < VOXEL_MARCH_MIN_DIR_COMPONENT) *
	                      VOXEL_MARCH_MIN_DIR_COMPONENT;
}

// The NEAR half of the slab test whose FAR half is VoxelMarchCellExitT. Split
// this way ON PURPOSE: the exit t is load-bearing in the walk and must keep
// exactly one authority, so this computes only the entry and defers the exit.
// DIAGNOSTIC ONLY -- nothing in the traversal calls it.
float VoxelMarchCellEnterT(float3 OriginLocalUU, float3 InvDir, int3 CellMinWorld,
                           int CellShift)
{
	const float3 CellMinUU =
		float3(CellMinWorld - VoxelMarchOriginVoxel()) * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU;
	const float CellUU = VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * float(1 << CellShift);
	const float3 T0 = (CellMinUU - OriginLocalUU) * InvDir;
	const float3 T1 = (CellMinUU + CellUU - OriginLocalUU) * InvDir;
	const float3 TLo = min(T0, T1);
	return max(max(TLo.x, TLo.y), TLo.z);
}

// The smallest push that is guaranteed to move the sample point into the next
// cell. A pure `t = exitT` leaves the point exactly ON the boundary, where
// floor() can land either side -- and landing on the near side is an infinite
// loop, not a wrong pixel. One thousandth of a voxel is four orders of magnitude
// above float noise at these ranges and four orders below a voxel.
#define VOXEL_MARCH_HIER_NUDGE_UU (VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * 0.001f)

// ===========================================================================
// THE BLOCK EXIT'S CONSERVATIVE BACKOFF -- the arithmetic half of hole safety
// ===========================================================================
//
// ONE FULL CHUNK AT THIS LEVEL, SUBTRACTED FROM THE BLOCK EXIT, APPLIED AFTER
// THE FLOAT CONVERSION. The walk is written in units where one level-L voxel
// measures VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU (the ray is pre-divided by 2^L before it gets
// here), so this is automatically the right size at every level with no rescale.
//
// WHY A BACKOFF AT ALL, AND WHY IT IS THE JUMP AND NOT THE MASK THAT NEEDS ONE.
// Danskin & Hanrahan measured that a plain max/OR bound is NOT automatically
// conservative: "a ray taking even a tiny step can step through three pyramid
// nodes". The exposure is not the bit -- a block with no resident cell in it
// contains nothing to miss -- it is the LEAP to the block's far side. Computing
// that leap in floating point can land the ray PAST a cell it genuinely clipped,
// and that cell is then never tested. That is a hole, and holes are the defect
// this project reports on sight.
//
// AFTER THE CONVERSION, NEVER BEFORE, AND THAT IS THE LESSON RATHER THAN THE
// RULE. The Z bound's first version padded in INTEGER CHUNK COORDINATES
// UPSTREAM of the float conversion, so the converted interval carried no bias at
// all -- the pad was load-bearing for a different argument and did nothing for
// this one. VoxelMarchCellExitT rounds when it builds the cell's min corner in
// UU and rounds again on the multiply by InvDir; the backoff has to be applied
// to the RESULT of that, which is what this constant's only use site does.
//
// SHORT, NOT LONG. The Z bound biases OUTWARD (it is narrowing an interval, so
// safety is a wider interval); this biases INWARD (it is advancing a ray, so
// safety is a shorter advance). Same principle, opposite sign, and getting the
// sign wrong here is a hole rather than a slowdown.
//
// A CHUNK RATHER THAN AN EPSILON, for the reason the Z bound gives: a chunk is
// the unit actually being skipped, the ray lands inside a cell the block was
// going to be non-resident in anyway, and it costs at most one ordinary chunk
// step per block against the four to twelve it removes. Against float error it
// is roughly six orders of magnitude of margin at the ranges this walk reaches.
#define VOXEL_MARCH_BLOCK_BACKOFF_UU (VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * 32.0f)
// The reciprocal of one chunk in walk units, for BlockCellsAvoided. A
// compile-time constant so the counter costs one multiply on a skip and no
// divide anywhere.
#define VOXEL_MARCH_INV_CHUNK_UU (1.0f / (VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * 32.0f))

// The fine walk, INSIDE one brick whose descriptor is already in hand.
//
// A real DDA and not a point sample: the crossed face is what gives the normal
// and the AO their exactness (ADR-0008), so a walk that found the right voxel by
// the wrong route would still shade wrong.
bool VoxelMarchWalkCellsInBrick(float3 OriginLocalUU, float3 Dir, float TStart, float TEnd,
                                int3 BrickCoordWorld,
                                FVoxelMarchChunkCache Chunk, FVoxelMarchBrickCache Brick,
                                inout int Steps, out float OutTHit, out int3 OutVoxel,
                                out int OutFaceAxis, out int OutFaceSign, out uint OutMaterial)
{
	OutTHit = TStart;
	OutVoxel = int3(0, 0, 0);
	OutFaceAxis = -1;
	OutFaceSign = 0;
	OutMaterial = VOXEL_MARCH_PLACEHOLDER_MATERIAL;

	const float3 Entry = OriginLocalUU + Dir * TStart;
	const int3 LocalStart = (int3)floor(Entry / VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU);
	int v[3] = { LocalStart.x + VoxelMarchOriginVoxel().x,
	             LocalStart.y + VoxelMarchOriginVoxel().y,
	             LocalStart.z + VoxelMarchOriginVoxel().z };

	// The entry voxel itself. A restart lands INSIDE a cell, not on its face, so
	// the first voxel has to be tested before any stepping -- the flat walk's
	// StartedInside case, one level down.
	{
		const int3 V0 = int3(v[0], v[1], v[2]);
		// Should hold by construction -- the caller derived BrickCoordWorld from
		// this same t -- but it is three compares and it is the invariant the
		// whole function now rests on, so it is checked rather than trusted.
		if (any(int3(V0.x >> 3, V0.y >> 3, V0.z >> 3) != BrickCoordWorld))
		{
			return false;
		}
		const uint CellIndex = VoxelMarchCellIndex(V0);
		const uint OccWord = VoxelBrickOcc[Brick.OccBase + (CellIndex >> 5u)];
		// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - CellIndex & 31 is 0..31 by construction
		if ((OccWord & (1u << (CellIndex & 31u))) != 0u)
		{
			OutTHit = TStart;
			OutVoxel = V0;
			OutMaterial = VoxelMarchMixedMaterial(Brick, CellIndex);
			// ---- THE FACE, WHICH THIS PATH USED TO LEAVE AT -1 -------------
			//
			// A DEFECT, found by reading and living exactly where the dump says
			// the disagreements concentrate. This branch fires when a ray enters
			// a brick and the FIRST voxel is solid -- i.e. on a brick boundary --
			// and it returned with OutFaceAxis still at its -1 initialiser.
			//
			// -1 is not a neutral value here. VoxelMarchFaceNormal turns it into
			// (0,0,0), so the surface gets a zero normal; VoxelMarchFaceAxes maps
			// it to the (0,1) plane, so the corner AO samples the wrong
			// neighbours. Both are silent -- the hit is correct, the voxel is
			// correct, and only the SHADING is wrong, on precisely the boundary
			// voxels under investigation.
			//
			// It does not explain `lost` (the voxel matches, so the comparator
			// scores it as agreement) and it is fixed on its own terms.
			//
			// The face is the last plane crossed entering this voxel: slab-test
			// the voxel and take the axis whose ENTRY t is largest. Computed from
			// the voxel's own bounds in world-voxel space, for the same reason
			// every other boundary in this file is.
			{
				const float3 SafeD =
					Dir + float3(abs(Dir) < VOXEL_MARCH_MIN_DIR_COMPONENT) *
					          VOXEL_MARCH_MIN_DIR_COMPONENT;
				const float3 InvD = 1.0f / SafeD;
				const float3 VMinUU =
					float3(V0 - VoxelMarchOriginVoxel()) * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU;
				const float3 Ta = (VMinUU - OriginLocalUU) * InvD;
				const float3 Tb =
					(VMinUU + VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU - OriginLocalUU) * InvD;
				const float3 TLo = min(Ta, Tb);
				const int Axis = (TLo.x >= TLo.y && TLo.x >= TLo.z) ? 0
				                 : ((TLo.y >= TLo.z) ? 1 : 2);
				// Selected, not indexed: dynamic component indexing of a vector
				// spills the whole thing to scratch.
				const float DirOnAxis =
					(Axis == 0) ? SafeD.x : ((Axis == 1) ? SafeD.y : SafeD.z);
				OutFaceAxis = Axis;
				OutFaceSign = (DirOnAxis > 0.0f) ? 1 : -1;
			}
			return true;
		}
	}

	float tMax[3], tDelta[3];
	int step[3];
	{
		const float pv[3] = { Entry.x, Entry.y, Entry.z };
		const float dv[3] = { Dir.x, Dir.y, Dir.z };
		const float lv[3] = { (float)LocalStart.x, (float)LocalStart.y, (float)LocalStart.z };
		[unroll]
		for (int a = 0; a < 3; ++a)
		{
			if (dv[a] > 0.0f)
			{
				step[a] = 1;
				tMax[a] = ((lv[a] + 1.0f) * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU - pv[a]) / dv[a];
				tDelta[a] = VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU / dv[a];
			}
			else if (dv[a] < 0.0f)
			{
				step[a] = -1;
				tMax[a] = (lv[a] * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU - pv[a]) / dv[a];
				tDelta[a] = -VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU / dv[a];
			}
			else
			{
				step[a] = 0;
				tMax[a] = VOXEL_FLUID_NO_CROSSING_T;
				tDelta[a] = VOXEL_FLUID_NO_CROSSING_T;
			}
		}
	}

	// The iteration cap. A ray crosses at most 8 planes per axis inside one
	// brick, so 24 steps reach any exit face; 32 is margin, and it is now the
	// only STRUCTURAL bound on this loop since the brick-geometry float test was
	// removed. It cannot be reached by a correct walk -- the integer containment
	// test fires first -- so hitting it would mean the voxel stepping and the
	// brick arithmetic disagree, which is worth a bound rather than a hang.
	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
	// CONTAINMENT IS AN INTEGER PROPERTY. DECIDING IT IN FLOAT t WAS THE BUG,
	// TWICE.
	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
	//
	// First form: TEnd was the NUDGED brick exit, so `tMax > Span` was false by a
	// whole nudge and the walk stepped a voxel past the brick every crossing.
	// Fixed by passing the true exit -- and `lost` fell 156k -> 32 while `gained`
	// and `moved` only HALVED, because a second contributor shares the signature.
	//
	// Second form, and it is the residue: `Span` is a slab test from the brick's
	// min corner, `tMax[axis]` is accumulated from the segment entry. Those are
	// two different float computations of THE SAME geometric quantity, so at the
	// exit plane they differ by ULP in an unbiased direction -- a coin flip, once
	// per brick crossing. Heads, the walk stops correctly. Tails, it steps into
	// the next brick and tests that voxel against THIS brick's occupancy dwords,
	// which VoxelMarchCellIndex masks to 0..7 so it silently aliases onto an
	// in-brick bit. A hit in air (`gained`) or a hit at the wrong voxel (`moved`).
	//
	// So containment is no longer asked of t at all. The brick a voxel belongs to
	// is `voxel >> 3`, exactly, and comparing that against the brick we loaded is
	// three integer compares that cannot be half-right. The float bound stays as
	// the loop's cheap early-out; the integer test is what makes it correct.
	// THE FLOAT BOUND IS GONE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
	//
	// Half-applying the principle is what produced `lost` 32 -> 464. The integer
	// test below decides when the walk LEAVES the brick; this float test also
	// decided it, from a slab computation that disagrees with the accumulated
	// tMax by ULP -- and once the over-run was fixed, the float test became the
	// only thing ending the walk early, so its ULP error could only cut SHORT.
	// One voxel short at the far face, which the brick loop then skips past
	// entirely because it advances t to the brick exit regardless. The exact
	// mirror of the over-run, from the same cause.
	//
	// So containment is now decided in ONE place and it is the integer one. What
	// remains here is a genuine bound -- the END OF THE RAY, which is a real
	// limit rather than a restatement of the brick geometry.
	const float RayEndSpan = TEnd - TStart;
	[loop]
	for (int i = 0; i < 32; ++i)
	{
		int axis;
		if (tMax[0] <= tMax[1] && tMax[0] <= tMax[2])      { axis = 0; }
		else if (tMax[1] <= tMax[2])                       { axis = 1; }
		else                                               { axis = 2; }

		if (tMax[axis] > RayEndSpan)
		{
			// Past the end of the ray's own segment. NOT a statement about the
			// brick -- the integer test below owns that.
			return false;
		}

		v[axis] += step[axis];
		const float tHere = tMax[axis];
		tMax[axis] += tDelta[axis];
		++Steps;

		const int3 VNow = int3(v[0], v[1], v[2]);
		// THE INTEGER CONTAINMENT TEST. A step that left the brick is a step this
		// function has no data for -- returning false hands it back to the brick
		// loop, which will load the right descriptor for it.
		if (any(int3(VNow.x >> 3, VNow.y >> 3, VNow.z >> 3) != BrickCoordWorld))
		{
			return false;
		}
		const uint CellIndex = VoxelMarchCellIndex(VNow);
		const uint OccWord = VoxelBrickOcc[Brick.OccBase + (CellIndex >> 5u)];
		// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - CellIndex & 31 is 0..31 by construction
		if ((OccWord & (1u << (CellIndex & 31u))) != 0u)
		{
			OutTHit = TStart + tHere;
			OutVoxel = VNow;
			OutFaceAxis = axis;
			OutFaceSign = step[axis];
			OutMaterial = VoxelMarchMixedMaterial(Brick, CellIndex);
			return true;
		}
	}
	return false;
}

// WatchChunk: the ONE chunk whose descents are recorded into FineBrickMask.
//
// The mask used to follow the LAST chunk walked, resetting on every crossing --
// and a `lost` ray by definition continues PAST the hit, so by the time it
// finished the mask described a chunk far beyond the one being asked about.
// Result: 100% "inconclusive" on 1046/1046, 30/30 and 983/983 rays. Not a weak
// signal -- zero signal, from an instrument built specifically to separate
// "skipped past it" from "walked it and missed".
//
// The caller knows which chunk it cares about (the comparator runs the flat walk
// first, so it has the hit), so it says so. Pass a coordinate no chunk can hold
// to record nothing.
FVoxelMarchHit VoxelMarchTraverseBrickHierWatch(float3 OriginLocalUU, float3 DirWorld,
                                                float TMinUU, float TMaxUU, float ConeSlopeUU,
                                                int3 WatchChunk)
{
	FVoxelMarchHit H = (FVoxelMarchHit)0;
	H.FaceAxis = -1;
	H.Level = 0;
	H.VoxelSizeUU = VoxelMarchVoxelSizeUU(0);
	H.Material = VOXEL_MARCH_PLACEHOLDER_MATERIAL;

	const float3 SafeDir = VoxelMarchSafeDir(DirWorld);
	const float3 InvDir = 1.0f / SafeDir;

	// Cell sizes as SHIFTS, because the exit test now works in world voxels:
	// 32 voxels per chunk (>>5), 8 per brick (>>3).
	const int ChunkShift = 5;
	const int BrickShift = 3;

	FVoxelMarchChunkCache Chunk = VoxelMarchNoChunk();
	FVoxelMarchBrickCache Brick = VoxelMarchNoBrick();

#if VOXEL_MARCH_BLOCK_SKIP
	// ---- THE COARSE OCCUPANCY LEVEL'S PER-WALK CACHE ----------------------
	//
	// CACHED FOR THE SAME REASON THE CHUNK RECORD IS. Consulting the bitfields
	// on every chunk step would ADD a load per chunk to buy a load per chunk --
	// exactly nothing. A walk crosses 4 to 12 chunk cells inside one 4^3 block,
	// so this turns the coarse level's cost into roughly one pair of loads per
	// twelve chunk steps, and into ZERO extra loads on the blocks it skips.
	//
	// THE SENTINEL COORDINATE IS ONE NO BLOCK CAN HOLD, so the first iteration
	// always consults rather than trusting the initial values -- the same
	// constructor discipline VoxelMarchNoChunk uses, and for the same reason
	// (block (0,0,0) is a legal block).
	int3 BlockCoord = int3(0x7FFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF);
	// THE INITIAL VALUES ARE THE CONTROL'S BEHAVIOUR, not zeroes. Occupied means
	// "descend", so an unconsulted cache can never skip; AnyAbsent false means
	// "claim nothing", so it can never assert a crossing it did not make. Both
	// are unreachable (the sentinel forces a consult) and both are chosen so
	// that if they ever WERE reached the arm degrades to the control.
	bool bBlockOccupied = true;
	// FALSE IS THE CONTROL'S BEHAVIOUR HERE, and the asymmetry against
	// bBlockOccupied above is deliberate: "not licensed" is what makes an
	// unconsulted cache unable to skip, which is the same property `Occupied =
	// true` buys for the other bit. Both degrade to the control if ever reached.
	bool bBlockAllSky = false;
	bool bBlockAnyAbsent = false;
	// Whether skipping this block can drop a bCrossedShellAbsent -- see the
	// consult below. Compile-time true where no shell flag exists.
	bool bBlockShellSafe = true;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
	// Whether the PREVIOUS consult came back sky-licensed, which is all the run
	// counter needs.
	//
	// TALLIED FORWARD, NOT CLOSED AT THE END, and that is the difference between
	// a run counter that is right and one that is systematically wrong on
	// exactly the rays it exists for. A run held open and flushed at the end of
	// the walk needs a flush at EVERY exit -- and this function has four, three
	// of them hits -- so any exit that forgot would silently drop the run. And
	// the run most likely to still be open when the walk ends is a long one
	// through sky, i.e. the case being measured. So instead: every licensed
	// consult adds a block, and a licensed consult whose predecessor was NOT
	// licensed opens a run. Nothing has to be closed, and the mean is
	// RunBlocks / Runs with no end-of-walk bookkeeping at all.
	//
	// COUNTED AT THE CONSULT AND NOT AT THE SKIP, for the reason BlockConsulted
	// is: counting at the skip would make the denominator the numerator, and
	// "licensed but not skipped" -- a block whose backed-off exit did not beat
	// the ordinary one-chunk advance -- would become invisible. That is the
	// finding that separates "open sky is scattered at this granularity" from
	// "the jump geometry is refusing", and they have different fixes.
	bool bPrevConsultSky = false;
#endif
	// One authority for the block's size as a shift, derived from the chunk's
	// rather than spelled again: a 4^3 block of chunks is 4 x 32 = 128 level-L
	// voxels, i.e. ChunkShift + 2. Doctrine 5 -- the same expression written
	// twice is still two authorities.
	const int BlockShift = ChunkShift + 2;
#endif

	int Steps = 0;
	float t = TMinUU;
	H.LastBrickIndex = -1;
	H.LastFineBrickIndex = -1;
	H.LastFineEntryT = -1.0f;
	H.FineBrickMask = uint2(0u, 0u);
	// Fixed for the whole walk: the mask describes THIS chunk and no other, so a
	// bit can never be read against the wrong brick numbering and the verdict is
	// never "inconclusive" for a reason the caller could have avoided.
	H.FineMaskChunk = WatchChunk;
	H.LastChunkCoord = int3(0x7FFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF);

	// ---- level 2: chunks ---------------------------------------------------
	[loop]
	for (int ci = 0; ci < 512 && t < TMaxUU; ++ci)
	{
		const float3 P = OriginLocalUU + SafeDir * t;
		const int3 LocalVoxel = (int3)floor(P / VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU);
		const int3 WorldVoxel = LocalVoxel + VoxelMarchOriginVoxel();
		const int3 ChunkCoord = int3(WorldVoxel.x >> 5, WorldVoxel.y >> 5, WorldVoxel.z >> 5);

		// TWO VALUES, AND CONFLATING THEM WAS A BUG.
		//
		//   ...ExitTrue  the geometric boundary. Bounds the CHILD walk.
		//   ...Next      that plus a nudge. Advances t past the boundary so
		//                floor() cannot land back on the near side.
		//
		// The first version used the nudged value for BOTH, which let the child
		// walk step one cell PAST its parent and test that cell against the
		// PARENT'S occupancy data -- a wrong-brick read reporting a hit where
		// there is air. See the note on the fine walk's Span.
		// The cell the CALLER already resolved -- not a second floor of the same t.
		const int3 ChunkMinWorld = int3(WorldVoxel.x >> ChunkShift, WorldVoxel.y >> ChunkShift,
		                                WorldVoxel.z >> ChunkShift) << ChunkShift;
		const float tChunkExitTrue =
			VoxelMarchCellExitT(OriginLocalUU, InvDir, ChunkMinWorld, ChunkShift);
		const float tChunkNext =
			max(tChunkExitTrue + VOXEL_MARCH_HIER_NUDGE_UU, t + VOXEL_MARCH_HIER_NUDGE_UU);

		// ONE BLOCK, BECAUSE THERE WAS NEVER TWO. This sat behind
		// `#if VOXEL_MARCH_SKIP_LEVELS >= 2` / `#else` with the two branches
		// BYTE-IDENTICAL, under a comment on the #else claiming that at skip level
		// 1 "the chunk level is NOT skipped ... it simply does not use a miss to
		// jump" -- while the code jumped, `t = tChunkNext; continue;`, in both.
		//
		// SO SkipLevels 1 AND SkipLevels 2 DO NOT DIFFER AT THE CHUNK LEVEL, and a
		// sweep across 1 and 2 measured one arm twice. Said here rather than fixed
		// by inventing a brick-granular advance: the arm is a measurement control
		// nobody currently runs (shipping is 2, the honest control is 0), and
		// changing traversal behaviour to satisfy a comment is how this file earns
		// its next silent defect. If a genuine level-1 arm is ever wanted it must
		// be WRITTEN, and then it must be shown to differ from level 2 before any
		// number is read off it.
		H.LastChunkCoord = ChunkCoord;

#if VOXEL_MARCH_BLOCK_SKIP
		// ===================================================================
		// THE COARSE OCCUPANCY LEVEL (voxel.March.BlockSkip)
		// ===================================================================
		//
		// ONE BIT ABOVE THE CHUNK, CONSULTED BEFORE THE INDEX LOAD BELOW AND
		// THAT ORDER IS THE ENTIRE POINT. The chunk index is 99.7% empty and
		// each miss in it costs one scattered 4-byte load into 64 MiB; this
		// answers "is there anything at all in the 12.8 m cube around me" out of
		// a 32 KiB buffer that stays resident, and when the answer is no it
		// removes 4 to 12 of those scattered loads in one step.
		//
		// PLACED AFTER LastChunkCoord AND BEFORE THE LOOKUP: the diagnostic
		// records the cell the walk reached, and the load is the thing being
		// avoided.
		{
			// INTEGER CONTAINMENT, NOT THE FLOAT t. Which block the ray is in is
			// `ChunkCoord >> 2`, exactly, and an arithmetic shift is the correct
			// floor for negative coordinates where a divide is not. The brick
			// loop decides its own membership the same way and for the same
			// reason (doctrine 4): a float guard on t cannot be the authority on
			// which cell the ray occupies, because it disagrees with the floor
			// that produced the cell by ULP at exactly the boundary.
			const int3 BlockNow = int3(ChunkCoord.x >> 2, ChunkCoord.y >> 2, ChunkCoord.z >> 2);
			if (any(BlockNow != BlockCoord))
			{
				BlockCoord = BlockNow;
				// THE SHARED DERIVATION, not a local spelling of it: the CPU
				// writes these blocks through FVoxelMarchChunkIndex::BlockOf and
				// this reads them through the same math in
				// VoxelMarchIndexCell.ush. A writer and a reader with private
				// copies of the wrap drift into blocks that are published and
				// never consulted, which renders as skipped ground with every
				// counter healthy -- the shape this project has paid for three
				// times in one night.
				const uint Blk = VoxelMarchIndexBlockCompute(
					ChunkCoord, MarchIndexDimChunks, uint(VOXEL_MARCH_BLOCKS_PER_SLOT),
					GVoxelMarchIndexGrid);
				const uint BlkWord = MarchBlockOccupied[Blk >> 5u];
				const uint BlkAbsentWord = MarchBlockAnyAbsent[Blk >> 5u];
				// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - Blk & 31 is 0..31 by construction
				const uint BlkMask = 1u << (Blk & 31u);
				bBlockOccupied = (BlkWord & BlkMask) != 0u;
				bBlockAnyAbsent = (BlkAbsentWord & BlkMask) != 0u;
				// THE SKY LICENCE. Loaded ONLY when the mode asks for it: the
				// branch is on a uniform, so it is coherent across the entire
				// dispatch and mode 0 issues no load at all -- which is what
				// keeps the residency-only arm byte-comparable with the result
				// it has to beat.
				bBlockAllSky = false;
				if (MarchBlockSkyMode != 0u)
				{
					const uint BlkSkyWord = MarchBlockAllSky[Blk >> 5u];
					bBlockAllSky = (BlkSkyWord & BlkMask) != 0u;
				}
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
				if (bBlockAllSky)
				{
					++H.BlockSkyLicensed;
					// RunBlocks TRACKS Licensed EXACTLY, by construction, and
					// that redundancy is deliberate: the CPU prints both, and
					// RunBlocks != Licensed is a self-indictment of this
					// instrument rather than a fact about the world. A counter
					// that cannot disagree with itself cannot report its own
					// breakage.
					++H.BlockSkyRunBlocks;
					if (!bPrevConsultSky)
					{
						// A licensed consult whose predecessor was not opens a
						// run. Consults are consecutive DISTINCT blocks along
						// the ray (the cache only re-consults on a block
						// change), so "consecutive consults" is "consecutive
						// blocks" with no gaps to reason about.
						++H.BlockSkyRuns;
					}
				}
				bPrevConsultSky = bBlockAllSky;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL
				// ===========================================================
				// THE SHELL FLAG'S DEBT, PAID BEFORE THE SKIP IS OFFERED
				// ===========================================================
				//
				// bCrossedShellAbsent has gated the retry ladder since
				// 2026-08-24, and it does NOT fire for every absent chunk -- it
				// fires for an absent chunk FACE-ADJACENT TO A RESIDENT ONE, a
				// gap in ground we hold rather than air outside the shell. A
				// block with no resident cell in it can still CONTAIN such a
				// chunk: one on the block's own boundary whose neighbour lives
				// in the next block along. Skipping that block would drop the
				// flag, the ladder would not open, and the black arcs it exists
				// to prevent come back. That is a hole and it outranks every
				// millisecond here.
				//
				// SO THE SKIP IS REFUSED UNLESS IT IS PROVABLY SAFE, and the
				// proof is six more bit tests. Every cell in this block has its
				// six face neighbours either INSIDE the block or in one of the
				// six FACE-ADJACENT blocks. If none of those seven blocks holds
				// a resident cell, then no cell in this block has a resident
				// face neighbour, VoxelMarchAbsentTouchesShell would have
				// answered FALSE for every one of them, and leaving the flag
				// alone REPRODUCES the full walk's answer rather than
				// approximating it.
				//
				// +/- 4 CHUNKS MOVES EXACTLY ONE BLOCK, whatever the position
				// within the block, because 4 IS the block size: (4b + k + 4)>>2
				// is b+1 for every k in 0..3, and the arithmetic shift floors
				// correctly on the negative side. So the neighbour coordinate is
				// fed through the SAME derivation rather than through a second
				// one that works in block space.
				//
				// AND THE EMPTY SLOT IS REFUSED OUTRIGHT: for a slot holding
				// nothing, VoxelMarchAbsentTouchesShell SHORT-CIRCUITS to TRUE
				// (see MarchIndexLevelPopulated), so every absent chunk there
				// sets the flag and the argument above does not hold at all. The
				// proving run -- -VoxelMaxRingLevel=0, which must read near-100%
				// holes above L0 -- is exactly that case.
				//
				// COMPILED ONLY WHERE SOMETHING READS THE FLAG. With the shell
				// gate off (the default, VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL 0) and
				// hole stats below 2, none of this exists and the skip is
				// offered on the two bits alone.
				bBlockShellSafe =
					(MarchIndexLevelPopulated & (1u << GVoxelMarchIndexGrid)) != 0u;
				if (bBlockShellSafe && !bBlockOccupied)
				{
					const int3 NOffs[6] = { int3(4, 0, 0),  int3(-4, 0, 0), int3(0, 4, 0),
					                        int3(0, -4, 0), int3(0, 0, 4),  int3(0, 0, -4) };
					[unroll]
					for (int nf = 0; nf < 6; ++nf)
					{
						const uint NBlk = VoxelMarchIndexBlockCompute(
							ChunkCoord + NOffs[nf], MarchIndexDimChunks,
							uint(VOXEL_MARCH_BLOCKS_PER_SLOT), GVoxelMarchIndexGrid);
						// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - NBlk & 31 is 0..31 by construction
						if ((MarchBlockOccupied[NBlk >> 5u] & (1u << (NBlk & 31u))) != 0u)
						{
							bBlockShellSafe = false;
						}
					}
				}
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
				// COUNTED AT THE CONSULT, WHICH IS THE DECISION. Counting at the
				// skip instead would make the denominator the numerator and the
				// rate unreadable -- and "consulted, and it held something"
				// would become invisible, which is the finding that separates
				// "the arm is inert" from "the world is not empty here".
				++H.BlockConsulted;
#endif
			}
			// ---- WHAT LICENSES THE JUMP ----------------------------------
			//
			// Mode 0 is the shipped predicate, character for character. Mode 1
			// adds the sky proof as an ALTERNATIVE licence. Mode 2 REMOVES the
			// residency licence so the sky proof is measured on its own -- which
			// is the only way to attribute a delta to the new predicate rather
			// than to the pair of them.
			//
			// THE SHELL-SAFETY TEST STILL GATES ALL THREE, unchanged. It exists
			// because skipping a block can drop a bCrossedShellAbsent that the
			// per-chunk walk would have set, and that flag is a hole question,
			// not a speed one. A sky-licensed block is no more exempt from it
			// than an unoccupied one: a block with no resident cell can still
			// CONTAIN a chunk face-adjacent to a resident one in the next block
			// along, and the sky proof says nothing about that neighbour.
			//
			// AND bCrossedAbsentChunk IS UNCHANGED BELOW, deliberately. A
			// sky-marked cell is STILL NON-RESIDENT. The mark says the streaming
			// system chose not to hold it; it does not say the fine->coarse
			// ladder should stop being told. Making the mark suppress that flag
			// is a separate arm with a separate switch, because it changes which
			// rays retry and therefore what the owner sees.
			const bool bBlockLicensed =
				(MarchBlockSkyMode == 2u) ? bBlockAllSky
				                          : (!bBlockOccupied || (MarchBlockSkyMode == 1u &&
				                                                 bBlockAllSky));
			if (bBlockLicensed && bBlockShellSafe)
			{
				// The block's min corner from the WORLD voxel, exactly as the
				// chunk's and the brick's are -- not from a local-UU floor that
				// assumes an aligned frame origin.
				const int3 BlockMinWorld = int3(WorldVoxel.x >> BlockShift,
				                                WorldVoxel.y >> BlockShift,
				                                WorldVoxel.z >> BlockShift) << BlockShift;
				const float tBlockExitTrue =
					VoxelMarchCellExitT(OriginLocalUU, InvDir, BlockMinWorld, BlockShift);
				// THE BACKOFF, APPLIED HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE -- see the constant
				// for why it is subtracted from the CONVERTED value rather than
				// applied in integer block space upstream, which is the mistake
				// the Z bound's pad made and paid for.
				const float tBlockJump = tBlockExitTrue - VOXEL_MARCH_BLOCK_BACKOFF_UU;
				// THE PROGRESS GUARD, AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THE BACKOFF SAFE
				// RATHER THAN MERELY CONSERVATIVE.
				//
				// A backoff with no guard has a failure mode worse than a hole:
				// a ray that CLIPS A CORNER of the block crosses it over less
				// than a chunk, so the backed-off exit lands BEFORE the ray's
				// current position, `t` does not move, the block is consulted
				// again, and the walk spins on one cell until the 512-iteration
				// cap ends it -- terrain that simply stops, reported as
				// TERM_CHUNK_CAP.
				//
				// Taking the jump ONLY when it beats the ordinary one-chunk
				// advance removes that entirely, and buys two more properties
				// for free: this loop can never take MORE iterations than the
				// control (every iteration advances at least as far as the
				// control's would), and the landing point is strictly past the
				// current chunk's exit, so no cell is ever re-tested.
				//
				// WHERE THE RAY LANDS, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A HOLE. Between
				// tBlockJump and the true block exit lies at most one chunk of
				// this block, and the block holds NO resident cell -- so the
				// cell the ray lands in is one the walk would have found absent.
				// The chunk loop below reaches it on the next iteration, takes
				// the `!Chunk.bValid` branch, sets the same flags it always
				// would, and steps out of the block by itself.
				if (tBlockJump > tChunkNext)
				{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
					// bCrossedAbsentChunk MUST STILL FIRE FOR THE CHUNKS THE
					// SKIP JUMPED, or the fine->coarse gate silently changes
					// meaning and the black arcs come back.
					//
					// WHY THIS IS EXACT AND NOT MERELY CONSERVATIVE. Occupied
					// is clear, so NO cell in this block is resident; the
					// walk's own site (`if (!Chunk.bValid)` ->
					// `H.bCrossedAbsentChunk || !Chunk.bResident`) would have
					// set the flag on the FIRST cell it reached in here.
					// AnyAbsent is read rather than assumed for the reason the
					// second buffer exists at all -- see its declaration.
					//
					// AND WHAT IS NOT PERTURBED: a block holding ANY resident
					// cell is never skipped, so resident-empty chunks are still
					// walked one at a time and still do NOT set the flag. That
					// asymmetry is the whole correctness requirement, and it
					// survives because the skip's precondition is residency,
					// not emptiness.
					H.bCrossedAbsentChunk = H.bCrossedAbsentChunk || bBlockAnyAbsent;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SKY
					// CONSERVATIVE, AND DELIBERATELY NOT bBlockAllSky. The block
					// bit is only loaded when voxel.March.BlockSkySkip asks for
					// it, so on a leg with the block level on and that mode 0 it
					// is a stale `false` -- using it would make the ladder's
					// answer depend on a switch that is supposed to be
					// orthogonal. Treating every jumped block as non-sky opens
					// the ladder exactly as today: LOST BENEFIT on the
					// BlockSkip-on legs, never a dropped fill. The two arms are
					// measured separately anyway, and both default off.
					H.bCrossedNonSkyAbsent = H.bCrossedNonSkyAbsent || bBlockAnyAbsent;
#endif
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
					++H.BlockSkipped;
					// RAY LENGTH IN CHUNKS, NOT 64 PER BLOCK. 64 x BlockSkipped
					// carries no information beyond BlockSkipped AND reaches
					// ~8e9 in a frame, which wraps a uint32 silently -- a
					// counter that reads small precisely when the arm worked
					// hardest. This is a LOWER bound: a diagonal DDA visits up
					// to sqrt(3) times the axis count over the same span.
					H.BlockCellsAvoided +=
						uint(max((tBlockJump - t) * VOXEL_MARCH_INV_CHUNK_UU, 0.0f));
#endif
					t = tBlockJump;
					continue;
				}
			}
		}
#endif

		if (any(ChunkCoord != Chunk.ChunkCoord))
		{
			Chunk = VoxelMarchLookupChunk(ChunkCoord);
			Brick = VoxelMarchNoBrick();
		}
		if (!Chunk.bValid)
		{
			// 32,768 voxels rejected on one dword. Outside the resident set is
			// AIR for a renderer -- a hole is visible, a wall is not.
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
			// ...and this is the one place a hole is CREATED, so it is where
			// the fallthrough learns about it. Absent only -- a resident-empty
			// chunk (bResident) is real air and must not send the ring loop
			// coarser. One OR per skipped chunk, on values already in
			// registers.
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL
			// First SHELL crossing only -- see the flat walk's start-voxel
			// site. bValid is already false in this branch, so the gate is
			// just "not resident, touching held ground, nothing captured yet".
			// TRACK_SHELL and no longer the level-2 guard: since 2026-08-24 the
			// retry ladder gates on this flag, so the shipping fallthrough arm has
			// to set it as well as the instrument does.
			if (!H.bCrossedShellAbsent && !Chunk.bResident && Chunk.bAbsentTouchesShell)
			{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2
				// The REASON stays level-2-only: the ladder needs the flag, the
				// breakdown needs the bucket, and only the breakdown reads
				// AbsentReason.
				H.FirstAbsentReason = Chunk.AbsentReason;
#endif
				H.bCrossedShellAbsent = true;
			}
#endif
			H.bCrossedAbsentChunk = H.bCrossedAbsentChunk || !Chunk.bResident;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SKY
			// THE SITE THAT MATTERS: this is where essentially every ray's first
			// absent crossing happens -- the near-field air the camera sits in,
			// which the sky trim declined and this mark now names. bOpenSky is
			// decoded from the same dword the residency test above read.
			H.bCrossedNonSkyAbsent =
				H.bCrossedNonSkyAbsent || (!Chunk.bResident && !Chunk.bOpenSky);
#endif
#endif
			t = tChunkNext;
			continue;
		}


		// Set when the brick loop discovers the ray has already crossed into the
		// next chunk. The advance below must then NOT fire: t is already correct
		// and past the boundary, and pushing it to tChunkNext would step over
		// whatever the new chunk holds against its own near face.
		bool bChunkChanged = false;

		const float tChunkEnd = min(tChunkExitTrue, TMaxUU);

		// ---- level 1: bricks within this chunk -----------------------------
		//
		// REVERTED 2026-08-20 from a brick-level DDA rewrite that regressed by
		// 188x (disagree 1,060 -> 199,839, lost offFace 6 -> 79,769). See the
		// doctrine block, entry 8: the diagnosis that motivated it may still be
		// correct while that implementation of it was not, and the two are being
		// kept separable.
		[loop]
		for (int bi = 0; bi < 64 && t < tChunkEnd; ++bi)
		{
			const float3 PB = OriginLocalUU + SafeDir * t;
			const int3 LV = (int3)floor(PB / VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU);
			const int3 WV = LV + VoxelMarchOriginVoxel();

			// CHUNK MEMBERSHIP, DECIDED IN INTEGERS (doctrine entry 4). The
			// loop's float guard `t < tChunkEnd` cannot be the only authority on
			// which chunk the ray is in.
			if (any(int3(WV.x >> 5, WV.y >> 5, WV.z >> 5) != Chunk.ChunkCoord))
			{
				bChunkChanged = true;
				break;
			}

			// Derived from WV -- the same cell the L1 test below uses. One
			// authority for "which brick am I in", used by both the test and the
			// advance (doctrine entry 5).
			const int3 BrickMinWorld = int3(WV.x >> BrickShift, WV.y >> BrickShift,
			                                WV.z >> BrickShift) << BrickShift;
			const float tBrickExitTrue =
				VoxelMarchCellExitT(OriginLocalUU, InvDir, BrickMinWorld, BrickShift);
			const float tBrickNext =
				max(tBrickExitTrue + VOXEL_MARCH_HIER_NUDGE_UU,
				    t + VOXEL_MARCH_HIER_NUDGE_UU);

			++H.BrickEntries;
			// The corner-clip configuration, counted where it is decided.
			if ((tBrickExitTrue - t) < VOXEL_MARCH_HIER_NUDGE_UU)
			{
				++H.ThinClips;
			}

			const int BrickIndex = VoxelMarchBrickIndex(WV);
			H.LastBrickIndex = BrickIndex;
			const uint MaskWord = (BrickIndex < 32) ? Chunk.BrickSolid.x : Chunk.BrickSolid.y;
			// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - BrickIndex is 0..63 by construction and the shift is masked to 0..31
			const bool bBrickHasContent =
				(MaskWord & (1u << (uint(BrickIndex) & 31u))) != 0u;

			if (!bBrickHasContent)
			{
				// THE FREE SKIP: 512 voxels rejected by one bit test against a
				// register already loaded with the chunk record.
				t = tBrickNext;
				continue;
			}

			if (BrickIndex != Brick.BrickIndex)
			{
				Brick = VoxelMarchLoadBrick(Chunk, BrickIndex);
			}

			if (Brick.Kind == 0u)
			{
				t = tBrickNext;      // uniform AIR: 512 voxels, no occupancy fetch
				continue;
			}
			if (Brick.Kind == 1u)
			{
				// Uniform SOLID. The hit is the brick's ENTRY, and the face is
				// the one the ray crossed to get in.
				H.bHit = true;
				H.THitUU = t;
				H.HitPosLocalUU = OriginLocalUU + SafeDir * t;
				H.Voxel = WV;
				H.Material = Brick.UniformMat;
				H.Steps = Steps;
				{
					// Same world-voxel anchoring as the exit test: the brick's
					// min corner comes from the WORLD voxel, not from a local-UU
					// floor that assumes an aligned frame origin.
					const int3 BMinWorld = int3(WV.x >> BrickShift, WV.y >> BrickShift,
					                            WV.z >> BrickShift) << BrickShift;
					const float3 BMin =
						float3(BMinWorld - VoxelMarchOriginVoxel()) * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU;
					const float BrickUU = VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * 8.0f;
					const float3 T0 = (BMin - OriginLocalUU) * InvDir;
					const float3 T1 = (BMin + BrickUU - OriginLocalUU) * InvDir;
					const float3 TLo = min(T0, T1);
					const int Axis = (TLo.x >= TLo.y && TLo.x >= TLo.z) ? 0
					                 : ((TLo.y >= TLo.z) ? 1 : 2);
					// Selected, not indexed: dynamic component indexing of a
					// VECTOR makes DXC spill the whole thing to scratch.
					const float DirOnAxis = (Axis == 0) ? SafeDir.x
					                        : ((Axis == 1) ? SafeDir.y : SafeDir.z);
					H.FaceAxis = Axis;
					H.FaceSign = (DirOnAxis > 0.0f) ? 1 : -1;
				}
				H.FaceNeighbourMask =
					VoxelMarchGatherFaceNeighbours(H.Voxel, H.FaceAxis, H.FaceSign, Chunk, Brick);
				return H;
			}
			if (!Brick.bInBounds)
			{
				t = tBrickNext;
				continue;
			}

			// ---- level 0: cells within this brick --------------------------
			const int3 BrickCoordWorld = int3(WV.x >> 3, WV.y >> 3, WV.z >> 3);
			H.LastFineBrickIndex = BrickIndex;
			H.LastFineEntryT = t;
			++H.FineWalks;
			if (all(BrickCoordWorld >> 2 == WatchChunk))
			{
				// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - BrickIndex is 0..63 and the shift is masked to 0..31
				if (BrickIndex < 32) { H.FineBrickMask.x |= (1u << (uint(BrickIndex) & 31u)); }
				else                 { H.FineBrickMask.y |= (1u << (uint(BrickIndex) & 31u)); }
			}

			float THit;
			int3 HitVoxel;
			int FaceAxis, FaceSign;
			uint Material;
			// TMaxUU, NOT the brick exit. The fine walk leaves this brick when
			// the INTEGER test says the voxel is no longer in it (doctrine 2/3).
			if (VoxelMarchWalkCellsInBrick(OriginLocalUU, SafeDir, t, TMaxUU,
			                               BrickCoordWorld, Chunk, Brick, Steps, THit, HitVoxel,
			                               FaceAxis, FaceSign, Material))
			{
				H.bHit = true;
				H.THitUU = THit;
				H.HitPosLocalUU = OriginLocalUU + SafeDir * THit;
				H.Voxel = HitVoxel;
				H.FaceAxis = FaceAxis;
				H.FaceSign = FaceSign;
				H.Material = Material;
				H.Steps = Steps;
				H.FaceNeighbourMask =
					VoxelMarchGatherFaceNeighbours(HitVoxel, FaceAxis, FaceSign, Chunk, Brick);
				return H;
			}
			t = tBrickNext;

			if (Steps >= MarchStepBudget)
			{
				H.bExhausted = true;
				H.TermReason = VOXEL_MARCH_TERM_BUDGET;
				H.THitUU = t;
				H.HitPosLocalUU = OriginLocalUU + SafeDir * t;
				H.Steps = Steps;
				return H;
			}
		}
		if (!bChunkChanged)
		{
			t = max(t, tChunkNext);
		}
	}

	// WHY THE OUTER LOOP ENDED. `t < TMaxUU` here means the 512-iteration cap
	// stopped it with segment left -- which a correct walk cannot reach (a 51.2 m
	// ray crosses 16 chunks) and which would look identical to a clean miss in
	// every counter we have.
	H.TermReason = (t < TMaxUU) ? VOXEL_MARCH_TERM_CHUNK_CAP : VOXEL_MARCH_TERM_SEGMENT;
	H.Steps = Steps;
	H.THitUU = TMaxUU;
	H.HitPosLocalUU = OriginLocalUU + SafeDir * TMaxUU;
	return H;
}

// The plain form the dispatcher calls: no chunk watched, so the mask records
// nothing and the compiler removes it. Inside the HAS_BRICKPOOL guard, because
// under source 0 neither this nor the function it forwards to exists.
FVoxelMarchHit VoxelMarchTraverseBrickHier(float3 OriginLocalUU, float3 DirWorld,
                                           float TMinUU, float TMaxUU, float ConeSlopeUU)
{
	return VoxelMarchTraverseBrickHierWatch(OriginLocalUU, DirWorld, TMinUU, TMaxUU, ConeSlopeUU,
	                                        int3(0x7FFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF));
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE RING CASCADE (P3-B2b-1). Two rings, L0 + L1, no overlap.
//
// A loop over levels, each running the EXISTING walk over its own t-interval,
// first hit wins -- rings are ordered near to far, so the first hit is the
// nearest and there is nothing to compare. The walk itself is unmodified; the
// ray is rescaled into level-L units before it is handed over (see the level
// context above for why that is the whole trick).
//
// OVERLAP IS ZERO AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. The measured cost of padding every
// ring's admit band was +9.2% resident chunks, p50 14.9 -> 17.3 ms, chunks/s
// 968 -> 672, hitches 1 -> 47. With overlap 0 the seam is VISIBLE and
// ATTRIBUTABLE: a silhouette pop is then either the missing overlap or the
// traversal, and the ring-discordant column separates them. The marcher has no
// mesh, so the crack the overlap exists to hide may not exist for it at all --
// a question to test, not to assume in either direction.
//
// WHAT `flat` MEANS HERE, because a naive control would destroy the ratio: the
// flat arm is RING TRANSITIONS WITHOUT CHUNK OR BRICK SKIPPING, not a level-0
// walk across the whole reach. A level-0 flat walk over 256 m is 2,560 voxel
// steps and would inflate the ratio into meaninglessness. Both arms cross the
// same rings; only the skipping differs.
// WatchChunk/WatchLevel: the descent mask is a LEVEL-LOCAL question. A chunk
// coordinate at level 0 names a different place than the same triple at level 3,
// so the watch is armed ONLY on the ring whose level matches -- passing a
// level-0 coordinate to a level-3 walk would fill the mask with a confident
// wrong answer, which is worse than the zeros it replaces. WatchLevel < 0
// disarms it entirely.
FVoxelMarchHit VoxelMarchTraverseRings(float3 OriginLocalUU, float3 DirWorld,
                                       float TMinUU, float TMaxUU, float ConeSlopeUU,
                                       bool bHierarchical, int3 WatchChunk, int WatchLevel)
{
	FVoxelMarchHit Miss = (FVoxelMarchHit)0;
	Miss.FaceAxis = -1;
	Miss.Material = VOXEL_MARCH_PLACEHOLDER_MATERIAL;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2
	// SENTINEL, NOT ZERO. Level 0 is a legal answer -- and the prime suspect
	// (R0 pending=242 mid-flight) -- so a zero default would silently vote
	// for the hypothesis under test. The kernel refuses to count a level word
	// unless this is < 6, which turns "the capture logic missed a ray" into a
	// visible identity shortfall (sum(byLevel) < uncovered) instead of a
	// phantom L0 hole.
	Miss.AbsentLevel = 0xFFFFFFFFu;
#endif
	uint EnteredMask = 0u;

	const uint RingCount = min(MarchRingCount, VOXEL_MARCH_MAX_RINGS);

	// ===================================================================
	// RING BOUNDARIES ARE CYLINDERS, NOT SPHERES
	// ===================================================================
	//
	// This was a real defect, owner-visible, and it produced two symptoms
	// that did not obviously share a cause:
	//
	//   * circular gaps at 128 / 256 / 512 m, whose FAR side was jagged in
	//     the shape of whole missing chunks;
	//   * flying straight up eventually stopped rendering the ground
	//     DIRECTLY BELOW the camera, in a ring, and descending brought it
	//     back -- with no chunk-shaped popping either way.
	//
	// The cause: these segment bounds are ray parameter t, i.e. 3D distance
	// FROM THE CAMERA, while the streaming admission that decides which
	// chunks are RESIDENT uses horizontal distance only --
	//   DistSq = (CenterX - Anchor.X)^2 + (CenterY - Anchor.Y)^2
	// with no Z term (VoxelWorldSubsystem.cpp, "a finer ring owns this
	// footprint"). Streaming rings are CYLINDERS. This walk treated them as
	// SPHERES, and the two diverge by exactly the camera's height.
	//
	// So from 300 m up, ground directly below sits at horizontal distance ~0
	// -- level 0 territory, and streaming therefore never admits a level-2
	// chunk there -- while the ray reaches it at t ~ 300 m, so this walk
	// entered ring 2 and looked up level 2. Nothing resident, nothing drawn,
	// a hole under the camera. At a seam the same error switches the ring at
	// sqrt(Outer^2 - h^2) horizontally, i.e. EARLY, into an annulus streaming
	// has only covered at the finer level.
	//
	// THE CORRECTION IS ONE SCALE FACTOR because the ray starts at the camera
	// and the streaming anchor IS the camera's XY: horizontal distance at
	// parameter t is exactly t * |Dir.xy|, so a boundary at radius R is
	// crossed at t = R / |Dir.xy|. Linear and monotonic -- no quadratic, no
	// second root, because the ray's XY origin is the axis of the cylinder.
	//
	// A PERFECTLY VERTICAL RAY THEN NEVER LEAVES RING 0, which is not a
	// degenerate case to guard against -- it is the correct answer. Ground
	// straight down is at horizontal distance 0 and is level-0 ground however
	// high the camera is. The clamp only keeps the scale finite; TMaxUU is
	// what actually bounds the walk.
	//
	// Horizontal rays are unaffected: |Dir.xy| = 1 makes this identity.
	const float RingHorizLen = length(DirWorld.xy);
	const float RingTScale = 1.0f / max(RingHorizLen, 1e-4f);

	// SegL PICKS THE t-INTERVAL; WalkL PICKS THE LEVEL WALKED. One variable
	// used to do both jobs, and that is precisely why the cascade could not
	// fall through: with the intervals disjoint and consecutive, "advance to
	// L+1" meant "advance to ground that is FURTHER AWAY", never "re-examine
	// the same ground at coarser detail". A miss at level 0 over 0-128 m was
	// followed by level 1 over 128-256 m; nothing ever re-covered 0-128 m, so
	// a missing level-0 chunk was a hole BY CONSTRUCTION however many coarser
	// levels held that ground. Splitting the two jobs is the entire fix:
	// SegIn/SegOut are ray parameters in world UU and are level-independent --
	// only the InvS rescale into the walk's units changes per WalkL -- so the
	// same segment can be handed to a coarser walk unmodified.
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
	// The OR of every walk's absent-crossing flag, across every segment. The
	// per-walk H below is overwritten each iteration, so without this a miss
	// would report only the LAST walk's crossings and the hole metric would
	// under-count exactly the rays it exists for (an absent chunk in segment 2
	// of 6, resident air after it). Folded into Miss at the bottom.
	bool bAnyAbsent = false;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2
	// The FIRST absent crossing's attribution, ray-wide. Captured only from a
	// walk at the segment's OWN level (WalkL == SegL): the first walk of every
	// ladder is that walk, so the capture below cannot be starved by a retry
	// however the gate is set -- and under VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL the
	// ladder continues on the SAME flag this capture triggers on, which makes
	// the argument tighter than it was, not looser. Attributing a coarser
	// retry's crossing would answer "which level was WALKED" when the question
	// is "which level was WANTED".
	uint RayAbsentLevel = 0xFFFFFFFFu;
	uint RayAbsentReason = VOXEL_MARCH_MISS_UNATTRIBUTED;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL
	// The OR of every walk's SHELL-crossing flag, the twin of bAnyAbsent above.
	// Separate because the two flags mean different things: bAnyAbsent says "the
	// ray crossed ground we do not hold" -- air included, which is why
	// `uncovered` read 25.27% of rays on a SETTLED STATIONARY world
	// (Saved/capture-zcut-alt120.log) -- and this says "the ray crossed a GAP in
	// ground we DO hold". The kernel counts both and prints the ratio, so the
	// size of the old counter's contamination is a reading rather than an
	// argument. Since 2026-08-24 this flag also OPENS THE RETRY LADDER, which is
	// why it is tracked on the fallthrough arm and not only under the level-2
	// instrument.
	bool bAnyShellAbsent = false;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
	// THE ENGAGEMENT PAIR (see the gate below). Ray-wide, folded into whichever
	// of the hit or the miss leaves this function. uint and not bool, because
	// the question is a RATE -- retries per opportunity -- and one bit per ray
	// would collapse a six-segment ladder into "did anything retry", which is
	// the same averaging that laundered three failures into one number and cost
	// the level-2 breakdown its first shape.
	uint RayFtConsidered = 0u;
	uint RayFtTaken = 0u;
	// The resident-Z bound's engagement trio, ray-wide. See the fields on
	// FVoxelMarchHit for what each one is and why one counter will not do.
	uint RayZCutConsulted = 0u;
	uint RayZCutSkipped = 0u;
	uint RayZCutClipped = 0u;
	// The coarse occupancy level's engagement trio, ray-wide. SUMMED FROM THE
	// WALKS rather than counted here, because the decision is taken inside the
	// chunk loop -- but summed HERE rather than left on the per-walk H, because
	// the ladder can run the same segment at several levels and H is overwritten
	// on every rung. See the fields on FVoxelMarchHit for what each one is.
	// NO RAY-WIDE FOLD FOR bCrossedNonSkyAbsent, deliberately, and the asymmetry
	// against bAnyAbsent below is the point. bAnyAbsent exists because
	// Miss.bCrossedAbsentChunk -- read by the uncovered counter and the hole
	// metric -- is a statement about the WHOLE RAY. The sky gate is a statement about ONE RUNG:
	// it asks whether THIS walk crossed a non-sky absence, exactly as the
	// shipped gate asks whether this walk crossed any absence. A ray-wide
	// version would keep the ladder open on later rungs because of an earlier
	// segment, which is neither what the shipped gate does nor what this arm
	// means. An unused accumulator here would also be the only variable in this
	// function with no reader, which is how a fold gets quietly repurposed.
	uint RayBlockConsulted = 0u;
	uint RayBlockSkipped = 0u;
	uint RayBlockCells = 0u;
	uint RayBlockSkyLicensed = 0u;
	uint RayBlockSkyRunBlocks = 0u;
	uint RayBlockSkyRuns = 0u;
#endif
#endif
	[loop]
	for (uint SegL = 0; SegL < RingCount; ++SegL)
	{
		const float SegIn  = max(VoxelMarchRingInnerUU(SegL) * RingTScale, TMinUU);
		const float SegOut = min(VoxelMarchRingOuterUU(SegL) * RingTScale, TMaxUU);
		if (SegOut <= SegIn)
		{
			continue;
		}
		EnteredMask |= (1u << SegL);

#if VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH > 0
		// The retry ladder: this segment at its own level first, then up to
		// VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH coarser levels, and ONLY while the previous
		// walk crossed a non-resident chunk (the gated break at the bottom).
		// Bounded by the cascade's top -- past RingCount-1 there is nothing
		// coarser to stand in.
		const uint LastWalkL = min(SegL + uint(VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH), RingCount - 1u);
		[loop]
		for (uint WalkL = SegL; WalkL <= LastWalkL; ++WalkL)
#else
		// Depth 0: WalkL is a const alias of SegL and the block below is the
		// pre-fallthrough loop body verbatim -- the byte-identical control.
		const uint WalkL = SegL;
#endif
		{
			VoxelMarchBeginLevel(WalkL);
			// 2^L. The ray goes in divided; the hit comes back multiplied.
			const float S = float(1u << WalkL);
			const float InvS = 1.0f / S;

			// ===========================================================
			// THE RESIDENT-Z BOUND (voxel.March.ZCut)
			// ===========================================================
			//
			// Intersect this walk's t-interval with the Z SLAB THAT CONTAINS
			// every chunk this level holds. An empty intersection means the
			// walk cannot reach a single resident chunk and is skipped
			// outright; a partial one is narrowed and the walk gets the
			// narrower interval. Both are the same test.
			//
			// PER WalkL, NOT PER SegL, and that is stricter rather than looser:
			// the slab has to describe the grid the walk will actually READ,
			// and under the retry ladder that is WalkL's grid, not the
			// segment owner's. A level-0 segment retried at level 2 is tested
			// against level 2's own slab.
			//
			// AFTER VoxelMarchBeginLevel, ON PURPOSE. VoxelMarchOriginVoxel()
			// is the single authority for the frame origin expressed in
			// level-L voxels, and recomputing MarchBrickOriginVoxel >> WalkL
			// here would be a second spelling of it -- doctrine 5, and the
			// exact shape that pinned this file to level 0 once already.
			float WalkIn = SegIn;
			float WalkOut = SegOut;
			// Set when the cut removed ANY part of the interval. It is what
			// stands in for the absent chunks the walk will now not visit --
			// see the fold below the traverse call.
			bool bZCutRemoved = false;
			// Set when nothing at all is left: the walk does not run.
			bool bZCutEmpty = false;
			if (MarchZCutEnable != 0 && MarchLevelChunkZ[WalkL].z != 0)
			{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
				RayZCutConsulted += 1u;
#endif
#endif
				// The slab's faces in the SAME space SegIn/SegOut live in:
				// world-UU t along DirWorld from OriginLocalUU. A level-L
				// chunk is 32 level-L voxels, and one level-L voxel is
				// VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * S of world UU.
				//
				// * 32 AND NOT << 5: the min bound is routinely negative and
				// a left shift of a negative int is the kind of thing that is
				// fine until a compiler decides otherwise. A multiply says
				// what is meant.
				//
				// .y + 1 BECAUSE .y IS INCLUSIVE. The top face of the last
				// chunk is the START of the one above it, and using .y here
				// would clip a whole chunk of resident ground off the top --
				// a hole, from an off-by-one, in the one direction this
				// change may never round.
				const int OriginZL = VoxelMarchOriginVoxel().z;
				const float VoxUU = VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * S;
				const float SlabLoUU = float(MarchLevelChunkZ[WalkL].x * 32 - OriginZL) * VoxUU;
				const float SlabHiUU =
					float((MarchLevelChunkZ[WalkL].y + 1) * 32 - OriginZL) * VoxUU;

				// DirWorld IS NORMALISED -- the ring scale above depends on
				// exactly that ("horizontal distance at parameter t is
				// t * |Dir.xy|"), so t and world UU are the same unit and the
				// slab test is a plain slab test with no rescale.
				//
				// THE PARALLEL CASE IS NOT A DIVIDE-BY-ZERO GUARD, it is a
				// different answer: a ray with no Z component either lies
				// inside the slab for its whole length or outside it for its
				// whole length. Tested against DirWorld.z rather than
				// SafeDir.z deliberately -- SafeDir nudges an exact zero to a
				// tiny non-zero, and reading that would produce an enormous
				// finite t whose sign is an artefact of the nudge. The
				// threshold is generous for the same reason, and every ray it
				// captures is treated as INSIDE unless it is provably outside,
				// which is the conservative direction.
				if (abs(DirWorld.z) < 1e-6f)
				{
					if (OriginLocalUU.z < SlabLoUU || OriginLocalUU.z > SlabHiUU)
					{
						WalkOut = WalkIn;
					}
				}
				else
				{
					const float InvDz = 1.0f / DirWorld.z;
					const float tA = (SlabLoUU - OriginLocalUU.z) * InvDz;
					const float tB = (SlabHiUU - OriginLocalUU.z) * InvDz;
					WalkIn = max(WalkIn, min(tA, tB));
					WalkOut = min(WalkOut, max(tA, tB));
				}
				// ===================================================
				// THE OUTWARD BIAS -- the arithmetic half of hole safety
				// ===================================================
				//
				// ONE FULL CHUNK AT THIS LEVEL, OUTWARD ON BOTH ENDS, APPLIED
				// AFTER THE CONVERSION AND AFTER THE DIVIDE. Everything above
				// this line is exact in integers right up to the moment it is
				// not: the slab faces are an exact int expression, but
				// float(...) * VoxUU rounds once and the divide by Dir.z
				// rounds twice more -- and the result is THE START OF A JUMP.
				// Every chunk between SegIn and WalkIn is skipped unexamined,
				// so a WalkIn that lands even a few ULPs INSIDE the true slab
				// entry silently drops a cell nobody ever tests. That is a
				// hole, and holes are this project's report-on-sight defect.
				//
				// Danskin & Hanrahan measured exactly this class: a ray taking
				// even a tiny step can step through three pyramid nodes, so a
				// max/OR bound is NOT automatically conservative once you JUMP
				// to the far side of it. VoxelRT works around the same problem
				// by biasing intersection distances. This is that bias.
				//
				// NOT THE SAME KNOB AS voxel.March.ZCutPadChunks, ON PURPOSE.
				// That pad acts in INTEGER CHUNK SPACE BEFORE this conversion,
				// and it is load-bearing for a DIFFERENT claim: it is what
				// keeps every removed chunk two chunks clear of anything
				// resident, so bCrossedShellAbsent is preserved exactly. One
				// number serving both arguments would break both at once the
				// first time somebody lowered it, and they fail for unrelated
				// reasons -- one geometric, one arithmetic.
				//
				// A CHUNK RATHER THAN AN EPSILON because a chunk is the unit
				// actually being skipped, and because it costs at most one
				// extra chunk step per segment end against the hundreds this
				// removes -- insurance at a price not worth measuring. Sized
				// at the WALK's level, since the cells at risk are WalkL's.
				//
				// RE-CLAMPED INTO [SegIn, SegOut]: widening past the segment
				// would walk ground this ring does not own. The bias may only
				// ever give back what the cut took, never more.
				const float ZCutBiasUU = 32.0f * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * S;
				WalkIn = max(SegIn, WalkIn - ZCutBiasUU);
				WalkOut = min(SegOut, WalkOut + ZCutBiasUU);

				// COMPUTED AFTER THE BIAS, NOT BEFORE. These two decide what
				// the counters report AND what the absent-chunk fold below
				// claims, so they must describe the interval actually handed
				// to the walk. Computing them first would let the arm report a
				// skip it did not take and -- far worse -- assert
				// bCrossedAbsentChunk for chunks the biased interval still
				// visits.
				//
				// THE SAFETY ARGUMENT COMES OUT STRONGER, NOT WEAKER: a chunk
				// still removed after biasing outward by a full chunk is now
				// at least one chunk FURTHER outside the slab than the
				// unbiased test claimed. Both "every removed chunk is
				// non-resident" and "no removed chunk touches the resident
				// shell" now hold with a margin they did not have before.
				bZCutEmpty = (WalkOut <= WalkIn);
				bZCutRemoved = bZCutEmpty || (WalkIn > SegIn) || (WalkOut < SegOut);
				// AN EMPTY INTERVAL COLLAPSES TO (SegIn, SegIn), NOT TO
				// (WalkIn, WalkIn), AND THE DIFFERENCE IS CORRECTNESS.
				//
				// The walk is still CALLED on the empty case rather than
				// replaced by a hand-built miss struct: its chunk loop is
				// `t < TMaxUU`, so an empty interval exits before the first
				// iteration and returns the function's OWN miss, with every
				// field initialised the way every other miss on this path is.
				// A synthetic struct here would be a second spelling of "what
				// a miss looks like" and would drift from the real one.
				//
				// But WalkIn on an empty interval can sit PAST SegOut (the
				// slab is entered only after the segment ends), and the FLAT
				// walk tests its START VOXEL before its loop and can return a
				// bStartedInside hit from it. Starting it out there would let
				// it report a hit from beyond the ring it was asked about.
				// SegIn is inside the segment by construction -- the
				// `SegOut <= SegIn` guard above already ran -- and a start
				// voxel at SegIn cannot produce a false hit either, because an
				// empty intersection means SegIn is OUTSIDE the slab and the
				// slab contains every resident chunk at this level, so the
				// chunk there is absent and nothing in it can be solid.
				if (bZCutEmpty)
				{
					WalkIn = SegIn;
					WalkOut = SegIn;
				}
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
				RayZCutSkipped += bZCutEmpty ? 1u : 0u;
				RayZCutClipped += (!bZCutEmpty && bZCutRemoved) ? 1u : 0u;
#endif
#endif
			}

			// if/else, not a ternary: HLSL's conditional operator takes numeric
			// scalars, vectors and matrices only, never a struct.
			// Armed only where the level matches -- see the note on the
			// signature. The level WALKED, not the segment: a chunk coordinate
			// names a different place at each level, and the watch follows the
			// lookup actually performed.
			const int3 RingWatch = (int(WalkL) == WatchLevel)
			                           ? WatchChunk
			                           : int3(0x7FFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF);
			// WalkIn / WalkOut, NOT SegIn / SegOut. With the Z bound off, or on
			// a level with no usable bound, they ARE SegIn / SegOut -- assigned
			// from them and never touched -- so the control arm hands the walk
			// byte-identical arguments.
			FVoxelMarchHit H;
			if (bHierarchical)
			{
				H = VoxelMarchTraverseBrickHierWatch(OriginLocalUU * InvS, DirWorld,
				                                     WalkIn * InvS, WalkOut * InvS, ConeSlopeUU,
				                                     RingWatch);
			}
			else
			{
				H = VoxelMarchTraverseBrick(OriginLocalUU * InvS, DirWorld,
				                            WalkIn * InvS, WalkOut * InvS, ConeSlopeUU);
			}
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
			// DRAINED FROM THE WALK IMMEDIATELY, BEFORE THE NEXT RUNG
			// OVERWRITES H. The flat walk zeroes these fields like every other,
			// so the `+=` is correct on both arms of the branch above and the
			// non-hierarchical control contributes a measured zero rather than
			// whatever the previous rung left behind.
			RayBlockConsulted += H.BlockConsulted;
			RayBlockSkipped += H.BlockSkipped;
			RayBlockCells += H.BlockCellsAvoided;
			// SUMMED PER SEGMENT, NOT PER RAY, and that matters for the run
			// mean: a run cannot span two ring segments, because each segment
			// starts its own walk with its own block cache and its own sentinel.
			// Folding here keeps a segment boundary acting as a run boundary,
			// which is the honest reading -- the marcher really does re-consult
			// there.
			RayBlockSkyLicensed += H.BlockSkyLicensed;
			RayBlockSkyRunBlocks += H.BlockSkyRunBlocks;
			RayBlockSkyRuns += H.BlockSkyRuns;
#endif
			// ===========================================================
			// THE Z BOUND PAYS ITS DEBT TO THE FALLTHROUGH GATE HERE
			// ===========================================================
			//
			// bCrossedAbsentChunk MUST STILL FIRE FOR THE CHUNKS THE CUT
			// SKIPPED, or the fine->coarse gate silently changes meaning and
			// the black arcs at LOD boundaries come back -- which is a hole,
			// and holes outrank every millisecond this change is worth.
			//
			// WHY THIS IS EXACT AND NOT MERELY CONSERVATIVE. Every chunk the
			// cut removed lies outside the padded slab, and the slab contains
			// every chunk this level holds, so every removed chunk is
			// NON-RESIDENT. The walk's own site (`if (!Chunk.bValid)` ->
			// `H.bCrossedAbsentChunk || !Chunk.bResident`) would have set the
			// flag on the first one it reached. bZCutRemoved is true exactly
			// when at least one such chunk was removed -- if WalkIn > SegIn
			// then the point at SegIn is beyond a slab face, i.e. in a chunk at
			// least one Z step outside the resident extent, and that is the
			// very chunk the walk evaluates on its first iteration.
			//
			// bCrossedShellAbsent IS DELIBERATELY NOT TOUCHED, AND THAT IS ALSO
			// EXACT. VoxelMarchAbsentTouchesShell asks whether an absent chunk
			// is FACE-ADJACENT to a resident one -- six probes, and the Z pair
			// first. The host pads the slab by at least ONE chunk, so every
			// removed chunk sits at least TWO chunks in Z outside the resident
			// extent and all six of its neighbours are themselves outside it.
			// The test would have answered FALSE for every one of them, so
			// leaving the flag alone reproduces the full walk's answer rather
			// than approximating it. THIS IS WHY THE PAD MINIMUM IS CLAMPED TO
			// 1 ON THE HOST AND NOT MERELY DEFAULTED TO 4.
			//
			// The empty-slot refusal (.z == 0) is the third leg of the same
			// argument: for a slot holding nothing that test SHORT-CIRCUITS to
			// TRUE, so the reasoning above does not hold there -- and the host
			// therefore never hands such a slot a usable bound.
			//
			// Net effect on both gates: with VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL on
			// the ladder reads bCrossedShellAbsent and sees exactly what it saw
			// before; with it off the ladder reads bCrossedAbsentChunk and
			// again sees exactly what it saw before. `uncovered` in the kernel
			// reads the same flag and is likewise unchanged.
			H.bCrossedAbsentChunk = H.bCrossedAbsentChunk || bZCutRemoved;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SKY
			// CONSERVATIVE, for the block skip's reason: the cut removes an
			// interval without consulting any cell in it, so nothing here has
			// proved anything about those chunks. Opening the ladder is the
			// same behaviour as today.
			H.bCrossedNonSkyAbsent = H.bCrossedNonSkyAbsent || bZCutRemoved;
#endif
#endif
			if (H.bHit)
			{
				// Back into world units. Voxel and VoxelSizeUU stay in LEVEL-
				// WalkL terms and H.Level says which -- a level-1 voxel
				// coordinate is not a level-0 one and silently promoting it
				// would put terrain at half its size. Consumers read Level.
				// WalkL, NOT SegL: under fallthrough a hit can come from a
				// coarser walk than the segment's owner, and packing it at the
				// owner's level would draw the stand-in at half its size.
				// (WalkL > SegL at this return IS the `substituted` metric,
				// set just below -- countable here and nowhere else, because
				// only this loop knows both levels.)
				H.THitUU *= S;
				H.HitPosLocalUU *= S;
				H.VoxelSizeUU = VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * S;
				H.Level = int(WalkL);
				H.RingsEntered = EnteredMask;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
				// `substituted`: the winning walk was coarser than the
				// segment's owner, so the pixel shows a stand-in. Counts HITS,
				// which is what makes it sky-proof -- the naive hole metric
				// ("exited the cascade with no hit") counts every sky ray and
				// can never read zero. With VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH 0 this is
				// WalkL == SegL by construction and folds to false.
				//
				// WHAT MOVES IT UP: flying with fallthrough on while streaming
				// is behind -- fine chunks absent, coarse stand-ins drawn.
				// WHAT MOVES IT DOWN: standing still until streaming settles
				// (the fine chunks arrive and win their own segment), or
				// fallthrough off (structurally zero). A counter that could
				// not come down would be worthless; this one falling to zero
				// while stationary is the invariant's success reading.
				H.bSubstituted = (WalkL > SegL);
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
				// The ladder rungs this ray had already consumed when it hit. See
				// the fold onto the miss at the bottom of this function for why the
				// hit has to carry them too.
				H.FallthroughConsidered = RayFtConsidered;
				H.FallthroughTaken = RayFtTaken;
				// Carried onto the HIT as well as onto the miss, for the reason
				// the pair above is: a ray that hit still consulted the bound on
				// every segment it walked to get there, and dropping those would
				// make the denominator read better exactly where the arm worked.
				H.ZCutConsulted = RayZCutConsulted;
				H.ZCutSkipped = RayZCutSkipped;
				H.ZCutClipped = RayZCutClipped;
				// Assigned from the ray-wide sums and NOT left as the winning
				// walk's own fields, which is the whole reason those sums exist:
				// H here is the LAST walk's struct, so leaving it alone would
				// report only the rung that hit and delete every block the
				// earlier segments consulted -- a denominator that shrinks in
				// proportion to how early the ray succeeded.
				H.BlockConsulted = RayBlockConsulted;
				H.BlockSkipped = RayBlockSkipped;
				H.BlockCellsAvoided = RayBlockCells;
				H.BlockSkyLicensed = RayBlockSkyLicensed;
				H.BlockSkyRunBlocks = RayBlockSkyRunBlocks;
				H.BlockSkyRuns = RayBlockSkyRuns;
#endif
#endif
				return H;
			}
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2
			// First segment whose owner-level walk crossed absent ground wins
			// the attribution; see the declaration of RayAbsentLevel for why
			// WalkL == SegL is required and sufficient.
			if (H.bCrossedShellAbsent && WalkL == SegL && RayAbsentLevel == 0xFFFFFFFFu)
			{
				RayAbsentLevel = SegL;
				RayAbsentReason = H.FirstAbsentReason;
			}
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL
			// Folded BEFORE the gate below, which now reads the SAME flag: the
			// ladder can break out of this segment on the next statement, and a
			// ray-wide OR that ran after the break would lose the very crossing
			// that caused it.
			bAnyShellAbsent = bAnyShellAbsent || H.bCrossedShellAbsent;
#endif
			// Fold this walk's absent crossings into the ray-wide flag BEFORE
			// the fallthrough gate can break out of the ladder.
			bAnyAbsent = bAnyAbsent || H.bCrossedAbsentChunk;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH > 0
			// THE GATE, AND IT MOVED ON 2026-08-24. A miss that crossed no absent
			// chunk is a TRUE miss: every chunk on the segment was resident (solid
			// parts missed, empties genuinely empty), so a coarser retry could only
			// add rock where this level proved air -- cave mouths, doorways,
			// overhangs, the sky above a ridge. Break, do not retry.
			//
			// WHAT THE OLD GATE GOT WRONG, and it is not a tuning miss. It asked
			// bCrossedAbsentChunk -- "did this ray cross ANYTHING not resident" --
			// and the streamed set is a thin shell around the surface (about 4.4
			// chunks per column at level 0). The sky-band trim leaves everything
			// above the terrain legitimately non-resident, and ring segment 0 starts
			// AT THE CAMERA, so the first absent chunk on essentially every ray is
			// the air the camera is sitting in. The comment this one replaces said
			// so in one line -- "sky rays DO take the retries" -- and then priced it
			// as a cost ceiling. It is worse than a cost: where the coarser walk
			// DOES hit, the retry puts COARSE ROCK WHERE THE FINE LEVEL PROVED AIR,
			// which is the exact regression the resident/invalid distinction was
			// written to prevent, arriving through the other door.
			//
			// THE SIZE OF THE POPULATION IS A READING, NOT AN ARGUMENT: this file's
			// own note on bAnyShellAbsent (:3186 before this edit, quoting
			// Saved/capture-zcut-alt120.log) records the old flag firing on 25.27%
			// of rays on a SETTLED STATIONARY world. Near-horizon rays enter every
			// ring, so the doubled population is the expensive TAIL, which is what a
			// 1%-low frame-rate gate is made of.
			//
			// THE NEW GATE asks bCrossedShellAbsent: did the ray cross a GAP IN
			// GROUND THE STREAMING SYSTEM HOLDS. That is the question the no-hole
			// invariant was always about. Air nobody holds is not a hole and buys
			// no second walk.
			//
			// WHAT WOULD REFUTE IT: `uncShell` RISING against the control arm. That
			// would mean the shell test is rejecting REAL gaps and this gate is
			// dropping fills the owner can see -- the change would be wrong, not
			// merely cheap. `uncovered` collapsing while `uncShell` holds is the
			// intended reading. The full falsifier list is at
			// VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL.
			//
			// ONE DECISION, WRITTEN ONCE. The gate is hoisted into a bool so the
			// counters below and the break cannot become two spellings of one
			// decision -- that is how a counter ends up certifying a branch it does
			// not actually describe.
#if VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL
			const bool bGateOpen = H.bCrossedShellAbsent;
#elif VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SKY
			// THE SKY GATE. Same question the shell gate asks -- is this absence
			// air, or a gap in ground we hold -- answered from the CPU's own
			// coverage proof instead of from six scattered neighbour probes.
			//
			// FIRST IN THE #elif CHAIN AFTER THE SHELL GATE, not before it: if
			// someone ever compiles the shell arm, that one is the more
			// conservative of the two (it opens the ladder for any absence
			// touching held ground, marked or not) and must win. Ordering the
			// chain so the stricter gate is reachable only when the looser one
			// is off would be the way round that quietly drops fills.
			//
			// AND A WALK THAT DID NOT FINISH ITS INTERVAL HAS PROVED NOTHING
			// ABOUT THE PART IT DID NOT WALK (added 2026-08-26).
			//
			// Both walks can return a MISS with segment left. The hierarchical
			// one caps its chunk loop at 512 (`for (int ci = 0; ci < 512 && t <
			// TMaxUU; ++ci)`) and sets TermReason = VOXEL_MARCH_TERM_CHUNK_CAP;
			// it also returns bExhausted with TERM_BUDGET when MarchStepBudget
			// runs out inside the brick loop. The flat walk sets bExhausted when
			// its own MarchStepBudget loop falls through. NONE of those three
			// paths sets bCrossedAbsentChunk or bCrossedNonSkyAbsent for the
			// cells they never reached -- there is no write site on them.
			//
			// THIS IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL AND IT IS NOT RARE. Ring segments are
			// bounded by HORIZONTAL distance and converted to a ray parameter by
			// RingTScale = 1/|Dir.xy|, so a ray within ~2.24 degrees of vertical
			// makes segment 0 own the whole ray: 1,310 level-0 chunk cells
			// against a 512 cap. VoxelMarchRenderer.cpp's own note on ZCut says
			// so in those words -- "hits the 512-chunk cap, returns
			// TERM_CHUNK_CAP and produces nothing".
			//
			// THE CONTROL GATE GOT AWAY WITH IT AND THIS ONE CANNOT. On the
			// shipped gate a truncated walk had almost always crossed some
			// non-resident air before it stopped, so bCrossedAbsentChunk was set
			// and the ladder retried at a coarser level -- where the same
			// interval costs half the chunk steps and the walk COMPLETES. That
			// retry is not a hole-filler there, it is the reach. The moment the
			// sky mark reclassifies that same air as PROVEN, the only evidence
			// keeping the ladder open on a truncated walk disappears, and the
			// ray abandons the untraversed tail of its segment and drops to the
			// next ring -- which is a near hit lost and whatever is behind it
			// drawn instead.
			//
			// WRITTEN AS A SUBSET OF THE CONTROL, DELIBERATELY. bCrossedAbsentChunk
			// is ANDed on top so this arm can still only ever retry FEWER rays
			// than the shipped gate, never more -- the safety property this whole
			// file is written around. A truncated walk that crossed nothing
			// absent behaves exactly as the control does: no retry.
			const bool bWalkTruncated =
				H.bExhausted || H.TermReason == int(VOXEL_MARCH_TERM_CHUNK_CAP);
			const bool bGateOpen =
				H.bCrossedAbsentChunk && (H.bCrossedNonSkyAbsent || bWalkTruncated);
#else
			const bool bGateOpen = H.bCrossedAbsentChunk;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
			// PROOF OF TRAFFIC, and it is the reason any frame-time reading off
			// this change can be believed. `Considered` counts rungs where a
			// coarser level existed and the gate was actually consulted; `Taken`
			// counts the rungs the gate opened, i.e. the extra walks paid for.
			// Taken / Considered IS THE RETRY RATE, and it must FALL between the
			// control arm and this one. If it does not move, THE SWITCH IS INERT
			// and nothing else measured on that leg means anything -- seven
			// switches in this repo were inert while looking armed, which is why
			// this is a counter and not an inference from `substituted`.
			//
			// WalkL < LastWalkL, not "always": at the top of the ladder there is no
			// coarser level to consult, so counting a rung there would fill the
			// denominator with decisions nobody made and make the rate read better
			// than it is on exactly the deepest rays.
			if (WalkL < LastWalkL)
			{
				RayFtConsidered += 1u;
				RayFtTaken += bGateOpen ? 1u : 0u;
			}
#endif
			if (!bGateOpen)
			{
				break;
			}
#endif
		}
	}
	Miss.RingsEntered = EnteredMask;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_ABSENT
	// The `uncovered` half of the hole metric reads this off the miss: no hit
	// at any level AND the ray crossed a chunk the streaming system does not
	// HOLD (bResident false -- absent, never resident-and-empty). The horizon
	// discriminator lives at the kernel, not here: direction is the kernel's
	// input and this function should not grow a second copy of it.
	Miss.bCrossedAbsentChunk = bAnyAbsent;
#if VOXEL_MARCH_TRACK_SHELL
	// Folded under TRACK_SHELL rather than under the level-2 guard it used to
	// sit in, because the flag now has TWO readers: the kernel's `uncShell`
	// counter and the retry gate above (VOXEL_MARCH_FALLTHROUGH_SHELL). Same
	// value, same fold, one more arm that compiles it.
	Miss.bCrossedShellAbsent = bAnyShellAbsent;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS >= 2
	Miss.AbsentLevel = RayAbsentLevel;
	Miss.FirstAbsentReason = RayAbsentReason;
#endif
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
	// PROOF OF TRAFFIC. Carried on the miss AND on the hit (see the hit return
	// above), because the retry rate's denominator is ladder rungs, not misses:
	// a ray that hits on its third rung paid two retries, and dropping it would
	// bias the rate towards whichever arm returns hits earlier -- i.e. towards
	// flattering the change under test.
	Miss.FallthroughConsidered = RayFtConsidered;
	Miss.FallthroughTaken = RayFtTaken;
	Miss.ZCutConsulted = RayZCutConsulted;
	Miss.ZCutSkipped = RayZCutSkipped;
	Miss.ZCutClipped = RayZCutClipped;
	Miss.BlockConsulted = RayBlockConsulted;
	Miss.BlockSkipped = RayBlockSkipped;
	Miss.BlockCellsAvoided = RayBlockCells;
	Miss.BlockSkyLicensed = RayBlockSkyLicensed;
	Miss.BlockSkyRunBlocks = RayBlockSkyRunBlocks;
	Miss.BlockSkyRuns = RayBlockSkyRuns;
#endif
#endif
	return Miss;
}

// ===========================================================================
// PHASE 6 -- THE COVER SEGMENT
// ===========================================================================
//
// ONE WALK, THE SAME WALK, AT HALF THE VOXEL SIZE. Everything below the ray
// rescale is the terrain traversal unchanged: the same index lookup, the same
// record validation, the same descriptor decode, the same popcount-ranked
// palette read, the same face axis and the same AO gather. That is not a
// convenience -- it is why a cover voxel can be shaded by the SAME
// VoxelMaterialColor(mat, faceClass, voxel) the terrain uses, with no second
// palette, no second decode and no second thing to keep in step.
//
// WHY IT IS AN OVERLAY AND NOT A RING. Rings partition the ray: ring L owns
// [R*2^(L-1), R*2^L) and nothing else looks there, so first-hit-wins is
// automatically nearest-hit-wins. COVER DOES NOT PARTITION -- it occupies the
// SAME space as ring 0, sitting on top of the ground. So the two walks both run
// over the near segment and the NEARER hit wins, which is a comparison the ring
// loop deliberately does not have to make. Folding cover into the ring loop as
// "ring -1" would have made it own an interval it does not own, and the symptom
// would be terrain vanishing wherever grass grows.
//
// COST, STATED SO IT IS MEASURED AND NOT ASSUMED: with the overlay on, the near
// segment is walked TWICE. voxel.Cover.March 0 is the control arm and it is a
// permutation, not a branch, for the reason VOXEL_MARCH_RINGS is.
FVoxelMarchHit VoxelMarchTraverseCoverSeg(float3 OriginLocalUU, float3 DirWorld,
                                          float TMinUU, float TMaxUU, float ConeSlopeUU,
                                          bool bHierarchical)
{
	FVoxelMarchHit Miss = (FVoxelMarchHit)0;
	Miss.FaceAxis = -1;
	Miss.TermReason = int(VOXEL_MARCH_TERM_SEGMENT);
	Miss.LastBrickIndex = -1;
	Miss.Material = VOXEL_MARCH_PLACEHOLDER_MATERIAL;

	const float SegOut = min(TMaxUU, MarchCoverReachUU);
	if (SegOut <= TMinUU)
	{
		return Miss;   // reach 0, or the whole segment is past it
	}

	VoxelMarchBeginCover();
	// 1/2, spelled as its reciprocal for the same reason the rings do: the ray
	// goes IN scaled so that one cover cell measures VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU in the
	// walk's units, and the hit comes back scaled the other way.
	const float S = 0.5f;
	const float InvS = 2.0f;

	FVoxelMarchHit H;
	if (bHierarchical)
	{
		H = VoxelMarchTraverseBrickHier(OriginLocalUU * InvS, DirWorld, TMinUU * InvS,
		                                SegOut * InvS, ConeSlopeUU);
	}
	else
	{
		H = VoxelMarchTraverseBrick(OriginLocalUU * InvS, DirWorld, TMinUU * InvS,
		                            SegOut * InvS, ConeSlopeUU);
	}

	// THE CONTEXT IS RESTORED WHETHER OR NOT WE HIT, and it is not tidiness.
	// VoxelMarchSourceOriginVoxel() reads GVoxelMarchIndexLevel, so leaving the
	// cover context set would make a TERRAIN hit pack against the cover origin --
	// terrain drawn at the wrong place, no error anywhere. The march kernel now
	// packs at the hit's OWN level instead, which removes the dependency
	// entirely; this restore is the belt to that braces.
	VoxelMarchBeginLevel(0u);

	if (!H.bHit)
	{
		return Miss;
	}
	H.THitUU *= S;
	H.HitPosLocalUU *= S;
	H.VoxelSizeUU = VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * S;
	// H.Voxel stays in COVER CELLS. Promoting it to level-0 voxels here would
	// halve it and lose the odd cell -- the hit would move up to 5 cm and the
	// per-voxel palette hash would key on a voxel that is not the one hit.
	// Consumers read Level, exactly as they do for a ring-1 hit.
	H.Level = VOXEL_MARCH_COVER_LEVEL;
	return H;
}

#endif // VOXEL_MARCH_HAS_BRICKPOOL

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The one the renderer calls. A dispatcher, so that the two implementations can
// coexist for the comparator without either of them knowing about the other.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FVoxelMarchHit VoxelMarchTraverse(float3 OriginLocalUU, float3 DirWorld,
                                  float TMinUU, float TMaxUU, float ConeSlopeUU)
{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE == VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE_OCCUPANCY
	return VoxelMarchTraverseOccupancy(OriginLocalUU, DirWorld, TMinUU, TMaxUU, ConeSlopeUU);
#elif VOXEL_MARCH_RINGS
	// The ring arm is a PERMUTATION, not a runtime branch, for the same reason
	// the source is: a runtime branch leaves both paths in the binary and
	// silently re-bases whichever arm is being measured. With rings off the
	// generated code is byte-identical to what every level-0 leg measured, which
	// is what makes rings-off a control rather than a near-control.
	return VoxelMarchTraverseRings(OriginLocalUU, DirWorld, TMinUU, TMaxUU, ConeSlopeUU,
	                               VOXEL_MARCH_SKIP_LEVELS > 0,
	                               int3(0x7FFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF), -1);
#elif VOXEL_MARCH_SKIP_LEVELS > 0
	return VoxelMarchTraverseBrickHier(OriginLocalUU, DirWorld, TMinUU, TMaxUU, ConeSlopeUU);
#else
	return VoxelMarchTraverseBrick(OriginLocalUU, DirWorld, TMinUU, TMaxUU, ConeSlopeUU);
#endif
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PHASE 6: the terrain walk with ground cover overlaid on its near segment.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// THE ORDER IS TERRAIN FIRST, THEN COVER, AND IT IS NOT ARBITRARY. The terrain
// walk is the one whose hit is used when cover misses, so running it first means
// the common path is unchanged and the cover walk is a tail. Running cover first
// would leave the level context set to cover across the terrain walk, and the
// two walks share that context.
//
// NEAREST WINS, not first. See VoxelMarchTraverseCoverSeg for why cover cannot
// be a ring: the two intervals OVERLAP, so a comparison is required and the ring
// loop's implicit ordering does not apply.
//
// TIES GO TO TERRAIN. Both t values come from the same ray but from walks at
// different lattice scales, so an exactly-equal t is a coincidence rather than a
// shared face; `<` rather than `<=` makes the choice deterministic and keeps
// the cover-off arm's picture reachable from the cover-on arm.
//
// With VOXEL_MARCH_COVER 0 this is the dispatcher above and nothing else -- no
// compare, no second walk, no cover uniform read.
FVoxelMarchHit VoxelMarchTraverseWithCover(float3 OriginLocalUU, float3 DirWorld,
                                           float TMinUU, float TMaxUU, float ConeSlopeUU)
{
	const FVoxelMarchHit T =
		VoxelMarchTraverse(OriginLocalUU, DirWorld, TMinUU, TMaxUU, ConeSlopeUU);
#if VOXEL_MARCH_COVER && VOXEL_MARCH_HAS_BRICKPOOL
	// ITS OWN DIAL. See VOXEL_MARCH_COVER_SKIP -- cover must not inherit the
	// terrain arm's hierarchical walk while that walk has an open directional
	// skip defect, or a cover bug and that defect are one symptom.
	// NOT const since 2026-08-24 -- see the counter carry below. Removing the
	// qualifier changes no codegen and no arm's behaviour; it is here only so
	// the one field that belongs to the RAY rather than to the winning walk can
	// be written before the return.
	FVoxelMarchHit C =
		VoxelMarchTraverseCoverSeg(OriginLocalUU, DirWorld, TMinUU, TMaxUU, ConeSlopeUU,
		                           VOXEL_MARCH_COVER_SKIP > 0);
	if (C.bHit && (!T.bHit || C.THitUU < T.THitUU))
	{
#if VOXEL_MARCH_HOLE_STATS
		// THE COVER WALK RUNS NO LADDER, so its FallthroughConsidered /
		// FallthroughTaken are structurally zero -- and returning them
		// unchanged would DELETE the terrain walk's rungs for every pixel
		// cover happens to win. That is exactly the shape of an instrument
		// that reads better the more work the frame did, which is the failure
		// this file has retracted findings over; so the counters are carried
		// across from the walk that actually paid them. Nothing else on C
		// moves: the PICTURE is the cover hit's, the ACCOUNTING is the ray's.
		//
		// WHAT WOULD SHOW THIS MATTERING: a leg with voxel.March.Cover on
		// reading a lower retry rate than the same leg with it off. The rate
		// is a property of the terrain cascade and cover cannot lower it.
		C.FallthroughConsidered = T.FallthroughConsidered;
		C.FallthroughTaken = T.FallthroughTaken;
		// Same argument, same direction: the cover walk consults no Z bound
		// (its slot is never given a usable one), so returning C's structural
		// zeros would delete the terrain walk's decisions for every pixel cover
		// wins and make the arm read inert in proportion to how much cover is
		// on screen.
		C.ZCutConsulted = T.ZCutConsulted;
		C.ZCutSkipped = T.ZCutSkipped;
		C.ZCutClipped = T.ZCutClipped;
		// SUMMED, NOT ASSIGNED, and that is the one place this group departs
		// from the two above it. The cover walk consults no Z bound (its slot is
		// never given a usable one), so carrying the terrain walk's trio across
		// is the whole of the accounting there. The cover walk DOES consult the
		// coarse level -- the cover slot has its own 32,768 blocks like every
		// other -- so its decisions are real and were really paid for. T and C
		// are separate walks over separate ground; adding them counts each
		// decision exactly once, and assigning T's would delete however much
		// work the cover overlay did.
		C.BlockConsulted = T.BlockConsulted + C.BlockConsulted;
		C.BlockSkipped = T.BlockSkipped + C.BlockSkipped;
		C.BlockCellsAvoided = T.BlockCellsAvoided + C.BlockCellsAvoided;
		C.BlockSkyLicensed = T.BlockSkyLicensed + C.BlockSkyLicensed;
		C.BlockSkyRunBlocks = T.BlockSkyRunBlocks + C.BlockSkyRunBlocks;
		C.BlockSkyRuns = T.BlockSkyRuns + C.BlockSkyRuns;
#endif
		return C;
	}
#endif
	return T;
}
